ommunication between devices on a network","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"protocol layers","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the term protocol layer."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for a definition that captures grouping of related protocols by function."},{"type":"list","items":["Accept: a grouping of related protocols that handle a specific part of network communication.","Accept: a level in a model where protocols of similar purpose are organised together."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition describes a grouping of related protocols handling one stage or function of communication","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"protocol layers","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe one advantage of organising network protocols into layers."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for an identification and 1 mark for a linked description, to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Accept: layers are self-contained (1) so a change in one layer does not require changes in the others (1).","Accept: it breaks the problem of communication into smaller parts (1) which makes the protocols easier to develop and maintain (1)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies a valid advantage of layering (e.g. self-contained, simpler to develop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Develops the advantage with a clear consequence for design or maintenance","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(c)","parent_question":7,"topic":"network topology","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school is wiring up a new computer room as a star topology."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why a star topology is suitable for the school."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each valid reason, to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Accept: if one cable fails only that single computer is affected, the rest of the network keeps working.","Accept: it is easy to add or remove computers without disrupting the rest of the network.","Accept: high performance because each device has its own dedicated link to the switch.","Reject: 'it is fast' on its own without justification."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid reason given for choosing star topology in a school","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid reason given for choosing star topology in a school","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)(i)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A small business uses a wireless network in its office."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how a **firewall** helps to protect the network."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 2 marks: 1 mark for identifying the action, 1 mark for a linked explanation of how the action protects the network."},{"type":"list","items":["Identifies action: monitors / inspects traffic entering and leaving the network (1).","Linked explanation: blocks any packets that do not meet the configured rules, so unwanted or malicious traffic is prevented from reaching internal devices (1).","Accept references to packet filtering or to allowing only traffic on specific ports."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies action (monitors or filters traffic)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains how this protects the network (blocks traffic that breaks the rules)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)(ii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how **encryption** helps to protect the small business network."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 2 marks: 1 mark for identifying the action, 1 mark for a linked explanation of how it protects the network."},{"type":"list","items":["Identifies action: scrambles the data using a key before it is transmitted (1).","Linked explanation: any traffic intercepted on the wireless network cannot be read without the matching decryption key, keeping the data confidential (1).","Accept references to ciphertext or to WPA2/WPA3 as examples."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies action (scrambles data using a key)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains protection (intercepted data is unreadable without the key)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)(iii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how **MAC address filtering** helps to protect the small business network."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 2 marks: 1 mark for identifying the action, 1 mark for a linked explanation of how it protects the network."},{"type":"list","items":["Identifies action: maintains an allow list of approved hardware (MAC) addresses (1).","Linked explanation: connection requests from any device whose MAC address is not on the list are refused, so unknown devices cannot join the network (1).","Do not award the explanation mark for simply restating that 'unauthorised devices are blocked' without reference to the MAC address check."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies action (allow list of MAC addresses)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains protection (only approved devices may connect)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(b)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security","marks":6,"command_word":"identify and explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Read the scenario below."},{"type":"text","value":"Priya works from home for an online retailer. She uses the same short password ('priya123') for her work email and her social media accounts. She often connects her laptop to the free public Wi-Fi at her local cafe to read company spreadsheets. When the company sent her an anti-malware tool, she did not install it because she felt the laptop was running slowly already."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify three errors Priya has made and, for each one, explain how it could allow an attacker to access or damage the company's data."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 2 marks per error, to a maximum of 6. Award 1 mark for clearly identifying the error and 1 mark for a developed explanation that links the error to a realistic security consequence for the company's data. Do not award the explanation mark for vague answers such as 'it is not safe' or 'a hacker could get in' without reference to a mechanism."},{"type":"list","items":["Reuse of a short, weak password across work and social media (1). Explanation: the password is easy to guess by brute force or to obtain from a leaked social media database, after which the attacker can sign in to the work email and access company correspondence and any attached files (1).","Connecting to public Wi-Fi to read company spreadsheets (1). Explanation: open Wi-Fi traffic is not encrypted by the network, so other users on the same hotspot can intercept the spreadsheet contents (or perform a man-in-the-middle attack) and read confidential company data (1).","Refusing to install the company-supplied anti-malware tool (1). Explanation: without up-to-date anti-malware, viruses, spyware or keyloggers can run on the laptop undetected, capturing company files or login credentials and forwarding them to an attacker (1).","Accept other valid errors such as storing company spreadsheets locally without encryption, provided the explanation links the error to a credible mechanism by which company data is exposed."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies the weak / reused password as an error","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains a realistic mechanism by which the weak / reused password exposes company data (e.g. credential stuffing from a leaked social media breach into the work email)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Identifies the use of public Wi-Fi to handle company data as an error","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains a realistic mechanism (e.g. interception of unencrypted traffic or man-in-the-middle on an open hotspot)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Identifies refusing to install the anti-malware tool as an error","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains a realistic mechanism (e.g. malware capturing files or credentials and exfiltrating them)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(a)","parent_question":9,"topic":"defragmentation","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school uses computers with hard disk drives. Over the school year the computers feel slower when opening files."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how a hard disk drive becomes fragmented over time."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 4 marks for a connected explanation. Award marks for distinct points, to a maximum of 4."},{"type":"list","items":["Files are stored in available blocks across the disk surface.","When files are deleted they leave gaps of free space between other files.","When new or updated files are saved they may be larger than any single gap.","The file is therefore split and stored in non-contiguous blocks across the disk.","As more files are saved and deleted, the proportion of split files grows.","The read/write head must move further to access all the parts of a file, so reading takes longer."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"States that files are stored in blocks on the disk","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains that deleting files leaves gaps of free space","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains that new files may be split across these non-contiguous gaps","marks":1},{"criteria":"Links fragmentation to slower access because the read/write head must move further","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(b)","parent_question":9,"topic":"defragmentation","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how defragmentation software fixes a fragmented hard disk drive."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 3 marks for a connected explanation. Award marks for distinct points, to a maximum of 3."},{"type":"list","items":["The software analyses the disk to find files that are split across the surface.","It moves the parts of each file together so that the file is stored in contiguous blocks.","It also gathers free space into one continuous area at the end of the used space.","After defragmentation the read/write head moves less to access a file, so files open more quickly."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"States that the software locates files that are split / fragmented","marks":1},{"criteria":"Describes moving the parts of a file together into contiguous blocks","marks":1},{"criteria":"Links the result to faster access (head moves less, files open more quickly)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(a)","parent_question":10,"topic":"WAN","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the term Wide Area Network (WAN)."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for a definition that captures a network spanning a large geographic area."},{"type":"list","items":["Accept: a network that connects computers over a large geographic area, often using third party infrastructure such as the public telephone system.","Accept: the internet as an example of a WAN.","Reject: an answer that confuses WAN with LAN."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition references a network spanning a large geographic area, typically using shared infrastructure","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)","parent_question":10,"topic":"cloud storage","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A music studio is recording a new album. The producer wants to store all the audio files in cloud storage so the band, the producer and the mixing engineer can collaborate."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain four advantages of using cloud storage for the music studio."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per developed advantage that is contextualised to the music studio scenario, to a maximum of 4 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Files can be accessed from any location with an internet connection, so the band can record at home and the engineer can mix in the studio.","Multiple users can collaborate on the same project files, with the latest mix always available to all members.","The provider takes care of automatic backups, so a hardware failure in the studio will not lose any takes.","Storage space can be scaled up easily as the project grows, without the studio buying new hard drives.","Files are accessible across different devices (laptop, phone, tablet), allowing the producer to review on the move.","Charges are typically pay-as-you-go, so the studio only pays for the space actually used."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First contextualised advantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second contextualised advantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third contextualised advantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth contextualised advantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(c)","parent_question":10,"topic":"cloud storage","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain four disadvantages of using cloud storage for the same music studio."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per developed disadvantage that is contextualised to the music studio scenario, to a maximum of 4 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Without an internet connection no one can access the audio files, so a studio outage halts work entirely.","Uploading and downloading large multi-track recordings can be slow on a typical broadband connection.","There are ongoing subscription costs, which can become significant as the project grows over many months.","The studio is dependent on the cloud provider's reliability, and an outage at the provider stops all work.","Sensitive unreleased recordings could be exposed if the provider suffers a data breach.","Long-term legal control of the master files lies with whoever holds the account, which can complicate ownership."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First contextualised disadvantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second contextualised disadvantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third contextualised disadvantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth contextualised disadvantage of cloud storage explained","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11(a)","parent_question":11,"topic":"legislation","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to identify the UK legislation that most directly applies to the scenario."},{"type":"table","headers":["Scenario","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Data Protection Act 2018","Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988"],"rows":[["A pupil downloads a film from a website that does not own the rights to share it.","","",""],["A school stores pupil contact details on its server but does not keep the records up to date.","","",""],["A former employee guesses an old colleague's password and reads private company emails.","","",""],["A blogger copies large sections of a paid-for textbook into a free article without permission.","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per correct row, to a maximum of 4 marks. Do not award the mark if more than one box is ticked in a row."},{"type":"list","items":["Pupil downloading a film without rights: Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.","School not keeping pupil records up to date: Data Protection Act 2018.","Former employee guessing a password to read emails: Computer Misuse Act 1990.","Blogger copying large sections of a textbook: Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Pupil downloading film correctly mapped to Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","marks":1},{"criteria":"Out-of-date pupil records correctly mapped to Data Protection Act 2018","marks":1},{"criteria":"Former employee accessing emails correctly mapped to Computer Misuse Act 1990","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blogger copying textbook correctly mapped to Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11(b)","parent_question":11,"topic":"hex to denary","marks":2,"command_word":"convert","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert the hexadecimal value B7 to denary. Show your working."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for valid working and 1 mark for the correct final answer, to a maximum of 2 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Working: B = 11, so (11 x 16) + 7 = 176 + 7 = 183.","Correct final answer: 183.","Award 1 mark for the correct answer of 183 even if no working is shown.","Award 1 mark for valid working that reaches an arithmetic slip if 183 is not given."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid working shown (e.g. B = 11, multiplied by 16, then add 7)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct denary answer 183 given","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12","parent_question":12,"topic":"school tablets impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A secondary school is replacing all paper textbooks with tablets issued to every pupil. Each pupil keeps their tablet for the full school year and can take it home."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of this decision. You should refer to cultural, environmental and legal issues in your answer."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Levels-of-response banded mark scheme."},{"type":"table","headers":["Mark band","Marks","Descriptor"],"rows":[["Mark Band 3 - High Level","6-8","Thorough knowledge and understanding of the issues. The discussion is clearly focused on the school tablet scenario and addresses cultural, environmental and legal issues with developed reasoning. Points are balanced and a clear judgement or conclusion is reached."],["Mark Band 2 - Mid Level","3-5","Reasonable knowledge and understanding. The discussion makes some reference to the tablet scenario and covers at least two of the three required issues. Reasoning is partially developed and a basic conclusion may be offered."],["Mark Band 1 - Low Level","1-2","Basic knowledge with limited reference to the scenario. The response lists points with little development and may only address one of the three required issues."],["0 marks","0","No attempt or response not worthy of credit."]]},{"type":"text","value":"Indicative content (not exhaustive; points may cover more than one issue)."},{"type":"list","items":["Cultural shift in classroom practice: lessons can use interactive resources, video and adaptive software in place of fixed textbook content.","Cultural concerns over a digital divide: pupils from low-income families may not have reliable home Wi-Fi to use the tablet for homework.","Cultural and safeguarding worries about increased screen time and the displacement of paper-based study habits.","Environmental impact of e-waste from broken or end-of-life tablets, and from the regular replacement cycle imposed by manufacturers.","Environmental cost of the cloud services and Wi-Fi infrastructure that constantly back up pupil work, which uses energy from data centres.","Environmental concerns about the supply chain for tablets, including rare earth metals and the human cost of mining them.","Environmental savings from removing the printing, transport and disposal of paper textbooks each year.","Legal duties under the Data Protection Act 2018 to keep pupil records, login data and usage analytics secure and only use them for stated purposes.","Legal need for age-appropriate content filtering and safeguarding software on every tablet, especially when the device is taken home.","Legal questions around copyright on digital textbooks, where a school typically buys a licence per pupil rather than owning the book outright as it would on paper.","Accessibility benefits with built-in screen readers, font resizing and translation tools that can help pupils with additional needs.","Overall judgement weighing up tangible benefits (accessibility, paper savings, interactive learning) against costs (e-waste, equity, ongoing legal compliance) is required for the top band."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Mark Band 3 (6-8 marks): comprehensive discussion meeting band descriptor","marks":8}]}}]`),K3={paper_id:W3,qualification:Q3,component:V3,title:U3,year:H3,session:G3,total_marks:X3,questions:Y3},Z3="J277-01-TeacherVariant-D",eO="J277",tO="01",aO="Computer Systems",rO=null,nO="TeacherVariant-D",iO=80,sO=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"number base conversion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computing student is revising number bases."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the missing values in the empty cells."},{"type":"table","headers":["Denary","8-bit Binary","Hexadecimal"],"rows":[["42","","2A"],["","01101100","6C"],["207","11001111",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each correct missing value. Accept binary written with or without leading zeros provided the value is correct in 8 bits. Accept hexadecimal in upper or lower case."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1 binary: 00101010","Row 2 denary: 108","Row 3 hexadecimal: CF","Reject answers with extra digits beyond 8 bits in the binary column."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 binary 00101010 correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 denary 108 correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 hexadecimal CF correct","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary addition","marks":2,"command_word":"add","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Add the following two 8-bit binary values and state whether overflow occurs."},{"type":"table","headers":["Value","Binary"],"rows":[["A","10110101"],["B","01001110"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Write the 8-bit result and state whether overflow has occurred."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for the correct 8-bit sum and 1 mark for correctly identifying that overflow has occurred."},{"type":"list","items":["Full sum: 10110101 + 01001110 = 100000011 (181 + 78 = 259).","Correct 8-bit result: 00000011 (the leading 1 is discarded).","Overflow has occurred because a carry is produced out of bit 7 and the true result (259) cannot be represented in 8 unsigned bits.","Accept 'yes' or 'overflow' for the second mark."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct 8-bit result 00000011","marks":1},{"criteria":"Overflow stated as yes/overflow occurred","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(c)","parent_question":1,"topic":"hex to denary","marks":1,"command_word":"convert","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert the hexadecimal value B7 to denary."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for the correct denary value."},{"type":"list","items":["Correct answer: 183.","Working not required. Reject answers expressed in any base other than denary."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Denary value 183 stated","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"CPU registers","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The CPU contains a number of registers used during the fetch-decode-execute cycle."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the role of each register."},{"type":"table","headers":["Register","Role"],"rows":[["Program Counter (PC)",""],["Memory Address Register (MAR)",""],["Memory Data Register (MDR)",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each correct role. Accept any clear wording that conveys the function. Reject answers that simply rename the register."},{"type":"list","items":["PC: holds the address of the next instruction to be fetched from memory.","MAR: holds the address of the memory location currently being read from or written to.","MDR: holds the data or instruction that has been fetched from, or is to be written to, memory."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct role of PC","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct role of MAR","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct role of MDR","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"CPU performance","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A laptop manufacturer is upgrading its processors so that they have a larger cache."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how a larger cache will affect the performance of the CPU."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for a developed explanation. One mark for an identified effect, with further marks for justification linked to CPU performance. Accept references to hit rate, RAM access and pipeline stalls."},{"type":"list","items":["More frequently used instructions and data can be stored close to the CPU.","The CPU has to fetch fewer items from slower main memory (RAM).","This increases the cache hit rate so instructions are retrieved more quickly.","Overall this increases the number of instructions executed per second, improving performance.","Reject vague answers such as 'makes the computer faster' with no link to memory access."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies that more data/instructions can be held closer to the CPU","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains that fewer fetches from RAM are required / higher hit rate","marks":1},{"criteria":"Links this to faster instruction throughput / improved CPU performance","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)","parent_question":3,"topic":"IPv4 addresses","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A network technician is checking which addresses in a list are valid IPv4 addresses."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show whether the address is a valid IPv4 address."},{"type":"table","headers":["Address","Valid IPv4","Not valid IPv4"],"rows":[["192.168.0.25","",""],["256.10.10.1","",""],["10.0.0.300","",""],["172.16.45.9","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each correct row. Two ticks on the same row score 0 for that row."},{"type":"list","items":["192.168.0.25: Valid IPv4.","256.10.10.1: Not valid (octets must be 0 to 255).","10.0.0.300: Not valid (final octet exceeds 255).","172.16.45.9: Valid IPv4."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 correctly identified as valid","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 correctly identified as not valid","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 correctly identified as not valid","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 correctly identified as valid","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"network security","marks":4,"command_word":"name and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A small business has connected its computers to the internet for the first time."},{"type":"text","value":"Name and describe two different network threats that the business should be aware of."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for naming each threat and 1 mark for a correct description of how that threat works (up to 2 marks per threat, maximum 4 marks). Two threats must be clearly different."},{"type":"list","items":["Phishing: fraudulent emails or messages designed to trick employees into revealing login details or clicking malicious links.","Malware (virus / worm / ransomware): malicious software that can damage files, encrypt data for ransom or spread between computers.","Brute force attack: automated attempts to guess passwords by trying many combinations.","Denial of service (DoS): flooding the business network or website with traffic so legitimate users cannot access it.","SQL injection: malicious SQL entered into a website input that allows access to or modification of the underlying database.","Social engineering: manipulating staff into giving away confidential information.","Reject answers that only name a threat with no description, and reject two examples of the same threat type."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First threat correctly named","marks":1},{"criteria":"First threat correctly described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second threat correctly named","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second threat correctly described","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(c)","parent_question":3,"topic":"secondary storage","marks":4,"command_word":"identify and justify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A small dental practice needs a secondary storage device to store patient records, including X-ray images, on the practice's main computer. The device must be reliable, fast to access and reasonably priced."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify a suitable secondary storage device and justify your choice with three reasons linked to the practice's needs."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for identifying a suitable internal storage device and up to 3 marks for justification points linked to the context of patient records and X-ray images."},{"type":"list","items":["Suitable devices: solid state drive (SSD) or hard disk drive (HDD). Award the identification mark for either.","SSD justification: very fast access to records during appointments; no moving parts so more reliable; quiet operation in a clinical environment; sufficient capacity (commonly 500 GB to 2 TB) for X-ray image files.","HDD justification: high capacity at low cost suitable for storing many years of patient records and large image files; non-volatile so data persists when powered off; widely supported by practice management software.","Reject removable media such as USB pen drives or DVD-RW as the primary device because they are slow, easily lost or have limited capacity for X-ray files.","Reject cloud storage as this is not a secondary storage device installed in the computer."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Suitable storage device identified (SSD or HDD)","marks":1},{"criteria":"First justification linked to dental practice needs","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second justification linked to dental practice needs","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third justification linked to dental practice needs","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the term embedded system."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for a definition that conveys a computer system built into a larger device to perform a specific (dedicated) function."},{"type":"list","items":["A computer system built into a larger device to perform a specific or dedicated task.","Accept references to having limited functions, no user-installable software, or being part of a non-computing product.","Reject definitions that simply describe a general-purpose computer."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct definition of embedded system","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify three real-world devices that contain an embedded system and state one feature of each that the embedded system controls."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark per correct device and feature pair, up to 3 marks. Three different devices must be given. The feature must be a function that an embedded system can plausibly control."},{"type":"list","items":["Microwave oven: controlling cooking time and power level via the keypad.","Traffic light controller: changing the light sequence based on timing or sensor input.","Smartwatch: tracking heart rate and counting steps.","Car engine management unit: monitoring fuel injection and emissions.","Smart thermostat: regulating room temperature based on a target value.","Digital camera: handling autofocus and image capture.","Reject general computing devices such as a laptop or smartphone."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First device with valid feature","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second device with valid feature","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third device with valid feature","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school is teaching pupils about different types of computer system."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by stating whether each device is best described as an embedded system or a general-purpose computer, and give one short reason."},{"type":"table","headers":["Device","Embedded or general-purpose","Reason"],"rows":[["Smart fridge","",""],["Laptop","",""],["Washing machine","",""],["Tablet","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark per correct row. Both the classification and a sensible reason must be given to score the mark."},{"type":"list","items":["Smart fridge: embedded; dedicated controller manages temperature/door alerts and the user cannot install general software.","Laptop: general-purpose; runs many different applications chosen and installed by the user.","Washing machine: embedded; controller runs only fixed wash programmes for that appliance.","Tablet: general-purpose; user can install apps for browsing, gaming, productivity and more.","Reject reasons that simply restate the classification (for example 'because it is embedded')."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Smart fridge correctly classified with reason","marks":1},{"criteria":"Laptop correctly classified with reason","marks":1},{"criteria":"Washing machine correctly classified with reason","marks":1},{"criteria":"Tablet correctly classified with reason","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(d)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two characteristics that distinguish embedded systems from general-purpose computers. Each characteristic is worth up to 2 marks."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for identifying each characteristic and 1 mark for a developed description that contrasts embedded systems with general-purpose computers, up to 4 marks total."},{"type":"list","items":["Dedicated function: embedded systems are designed to perform one specific task whereas general-purpose computers run many different programs.","Limited or fixed software: the program is usually stored in ROM and cannot be changed by the user, unlike a general-purpose computer where users install applications.","Lower power and resource requirements: embedded systems typically use less powerful processors and smaller memory because the workload is fixed and predictable.","Real-time operation: many embedded systems must respond within strict time limits (for example anti-lock brakes) while general-purpose computers are not normally constrained in this way.","Smaller form factor: embedded systems are built into the host device with no separate keyboard, mouse or monitor in many cases.","Reject answers giving two versions of the same point (e.g. 'small' and 'tiny')."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First characteristic identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First characteristic developed with contrast","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second characteristic identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second characteristic developed with contrast","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pupil is learning about the role of an operating system (OS)."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show whether the statement is true or false."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","True","False"],"rows":[["The operating system manages the allocation of memory to running programs.","",""],["The operating system is the only application installed on the computer.","",""],["A device driver is software that allows the OS to communicate with a peripheral.","",""],["User accounts created by the OS can be used to control which files a user can open.","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark per correct row. Two ticks on the same row score 0 for that row."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1: True.","Row 2: False (applications are installed in addition to the OS).","Row 3: True.","Row 4: True."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 ticked True","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 ticked False","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 ticked True","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 ticked True","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A user has several applications open at the same time on their laptop, including a web browser, a word processor and a music player."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the operating system performs memory management when several applications are open."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for points that show how the OS shares main memory between processes. Allow references to virtual memory."},{"type":"list","items":["The OS allocates a region of RAM to each running application when it is loaded.","It keeps track of which memory addresses are in use and which are free.","It ensures that one application cannot read from or write to the memory of another, providing protection.","When RAM becomes full, the OS can move less-used data into virtual memory on secondary storage.","When an application closes, its memory is released and can be reused by other applications.","Reject vague answers such as 'it manages the memory' with no further detail."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Allocation of memory to each program","marks":1},{"criteria":"Tracks free and used memory / protects between processes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Use of virtual memory / freeing memory when programs close","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school technician has plugged a new printer into a teacher's computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the operating system handles peripheral management using device drivers."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for points that explain the role of device drivers and how they let the OS communicate with peripherals."},{"type":"list","items":["A device driver is a piece of software that translates between the operating system and a specific peripheral.","When the printer is connected, the OS loads (or downloads) the correct driver for that model.","Applications send a generic print request to the OS, which uses the driver to convert it into commands the printer understands.","Updated drivers can be installed when manufacturers release new versions to fix bugs or add features.","Reject 'it just works' style answers with no reference to drivers."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Defines a device driver as software for a specific peripheral","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explains that the OS uses the driver to send/receive data with the peripheral","marks":1},{"criteria":"Adds detail about loading/updating drivers or translating commands","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing a one-line description of each operating system function."},{"type":"table","headers":["OS function","One-line description"],"rows":[["File management",""],["User interface",""],["Multitasking",""],["User accounts",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark per correct description. Accept any clear wording that conveys the function."},{"type":"list","items":["File management: organising files and folders on storage and providing tools to create, copy, move, rename and delete them.","User interface: providing a way (graphical or command line) for the user to interact with the computer.","Multitasking: allowing more than one application to appear to run at the same time by sharing CPU time between them.","User accounts: allowing different users to log in with their own settings and permissions, keeping their files separate.","Reject answers that simply restate the function name."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"File management correctly described","marks":1},{"criteria":"User interface correctly described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Multitasking correctly described","marks":1},{"criteria":"User accounts correctly described","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Pupils and teachers share computers in a school IT room."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe how user accounts and permissions contribute to the security of a school computer."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for points that link user account features to school security. Examples must be plausible in a school context."},{"type":"list","items":["Each pupil and teacher logs in with a unique username and password so the system knows who is using it.","Permissions can restrict pupils from installing software or changing system settings, reducing the risk of malware.","Teachers and administrators can be given higher permissions to access shared resources or pupil work.","Files saved by one pupil cannot be read or modified by another pupil if permissions are set correctly.","Audit logs of logins help identify misuse such as attempts to access another user's account.","Reject answers that only describe passwords without linking to permissions."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Authentication of individual users via login","marks":1},{"criteria":"Permissions restrict actions such as installing software or accessing files","marks":1},{"criteria":"Different permission levels for pupils, teachers and administrators","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(f)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pupil is comparing a single-core CPU laptop with a newer multi-core CPU laptop."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how multitasking works on a single-core CPU compared with a multi-core CPU."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for points that compare time-slicing on a single core with true parallel execution on multiple cores."},{"type":"list","items":["On a single-core CPU only one process can execute at any instant, so the OS rapidly switches between processes (time-slicing).","The switching happens so quickly that the user perceives the applications as running simultaneously.","On a multi-core CPU each core can execute a different process at the same time, providing genuine parallel execution.","The OS scheduler distributes processes across the available cores, improving throughput and responsiveness.","Reject answers that say a single-core CPU cannot multitask at all."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Single-core uses time-slicing/rapid switching of one process at a time","marks":1},{"criteria":"Multi-core can execute several processes truly in parallel","marks":1},{"criteria":"OS scheduler decides which process runs on which core / when","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"legislation","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the most appropriate piece of legislation for each scenario. Choose from: Computer Misuse Act 1990, Data Protection Act 2018, Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988."},{"type":"table","headers":["Scenario","Legislation"],"rows":[["A pupil guesses a teacher's password to access the school network without permission.",""],["A music streaming service uploads songs without permission from the artist.",""],["A company sells a customer's personal details to a third party without consent.",""],["A user releases a program that deletes files on other people's computers.",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark per correct row. Accept the act name with or without the year provided the legislation is unambiguous."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1: Computer Misuse Act 1990 (unauthorised access).","Row 2: Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988.","Row 3: Data Protection Act 2018.","Row 4: Computer Misuse Act 1990 (unauthorised modification of computer material).","Reject GDPR alone in row 3 unless Data Protection Act is also referenced; accept either for examiner discretion."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 correct (Computer Misuse Act)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 correct (Copyright Designs and Patents Act)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 correct (Data Protection Act)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 correct (Computer Misuse Act)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)","parent_question":6,"topic":"Computer Misuse Act","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the Computer Misuse Act 1990 and give one example of an offence that it covers."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for describing the purpose and offences covered by the Act, and 1 mark for a clear specific example. Maximum 4 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Designed to protect computer systems and data from unauthorised access and modification.","Offence 1: unauthorised access to computer material (for example logging into another user's account without permission).","Offence 2: unauthorised access with intent to commit a further offence (for example accessing a system to commit fraud).","Offence 3: unauthorised modification of computer material (for example creating or distributing malware).","Offence 4: making, supplying or obtaining tools used to commit computer misuse offences.","Example: writing a virus designed to delete files on other people's computers.","Reject examples that fit other legislation only (for example simple piracy of music)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"States overall purpose of the Act (protects against unauthorised access/modification)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Identifies one specific offence","marks":1},{"criteria":"Identifies a second specific offence or further detail of the Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gives a relevant example of an offence","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(c)","parent_question":6,"topic":"Data Protection Act","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the Data Protection Act 2018 protects personal data. Cover at least three principles in your answer."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each principle correctly explained, up to a maximum of 4 marks. Principles must be clearly different."},{"type":"list","items":["Personal data must be processed lawfully, fairly and transparently.","Data must be collected for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes only.","Data must be adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary (data minimisation).","Data must be accurate and kept up to date.","Data must not be kept for longer than necessary.","Data must be processed securely, including protection against unauthorised access or loss.","Data subjects have rights such as access, rectification and erasure.","Reject vague answers such as 'it keeps data safe' without naming a principle."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First principle correctly explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second principle correctly explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third principle correctly explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth principle, data subject right or further detail explained","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(d)","parent_question":6,"topic":"ethical issues","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Many companies collect large amounts of personal data from users of their websites and apps."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two ethical concerns that arise when companies collect user data. Each concern is worth up to 2 marks."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for identifying each ethical concern and 1 mark for developing it with a relevant impact. Two clearly different concerns required."},{"type":"list","items":["Lack of informed consent: users often accept long terms and conditions without understanding what data is collected, undermining their autonomy.","Profiling and targeted advertising: aggregated data can be used to manipulate purchasing decisions or political views.","Sharing or selling data with third parties: users lose control of where their data ends up and may receive unwanted marketing or scams.","Surveillance and loss of privacy: continuous tracking of location and behaviour can feel intrusive and chill freedom of expression.","Bias and discrimination: decisions made from collected data (loans, insurance) may unfairly disadvantage some groups.","Risk of data breaches: collecting more data than necessary increases the harm if a breach occurs.","Reject purely legal answers that only restate the Data Protection Act."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First ethical concern identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First concern developed with impact on users","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second ethical concern identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second concern developed with impact on users","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(e)","parent_question":6,"topic":"AI school reports","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A secondary school is trialling AI software that automatically writes pupil end-of-term reports. Teachers enter grades and a few short notes about each pupil, and the software produces a paragraph of personalised written feedback that is sent home to parents."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative impacts of the school using this AI software. Your answer should include:"},{"type":"list","items":["ethical issues","legal issues","cultural issues"]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Levels-of-response banded mark scheme."},{"type":"table","headers":["Mark band","Marks","Descriptor"],"rows":[["Mark Band 3 - High Level","6-8","thorough knowledge and understanding of the impact of AI-generated reports, addressing all three bullets (ethical, legal, cultural) in a balanced way with developed reasoning, sustained focus on the school context and accurate use of subject terminology"],["Mark Band 2 - Mid Level","3-5","reasonable knowledge and understanding with some balance between positive and negative impacts; covers at least two of the three bullets; reasoning shown but may be uneven and use of terminology partial"],["Mark Band 1 - Low Level","1-2","basic knowledge with limited application to the school context; mostly one-sided; addresses only one bullet; little use of subject terminology"],["0 marks","0","no attempt / response not worthy of credit"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Indicative content (not exhaustive; points may cover more than one issue)."},{"type":"list","items":["Ethical: teachers retain accountability for any inaccurate or misleading comment generated by the AI, even though they did not write it.","Ethical: AI-generated comments may contain bias inherited from training data, for example more positive language for some pupil groups than others.","Ethical: parents may be misled if they assume that the report has been written personally by their child's teacher; transparency about AI use is required.","Ethical: time savings for teachers can free up time for marking and pastoral support, which benefits pupils.","Ethical: risk of deskilling teachers, who may lose the practice of writing detailed personal feedback over time.","Legal: pupil names, grades and behaviour notes are personal data; the school must have a lawful basis under the Data Protection Act 2018 / UK GDPR to share them with the AI provider.","Legal: a Data Protection Impact Assessment may be required and parental information notices should be updated to explain the new processing.","Legal: contracts with the AI provider must cover data security, retention and any use of pupil data to train future models.","Legal: copyright on AI-generated text is unclear; the school may not own the output in the same way as a teacher-written report.","Cultural: parents and pupils place trust in personal communication from the school; widespread use of AI may damage that trust if discovered.","Cultural: homogenisation of report language could mean reports across pupils and schools begin to sound the same, reducing the sense of individual recognition.","Cultural: it may shift the role of the teacher and how the school is perceived in the local community.","Cultural: pupils with English as an additional language or specific needs may not see their voice reflected if the AI is not trained on diverse contexts.","Balanced conclusion drawing the points together is expected at the top of Mark Band 3."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Mark Band 3 (6-8 marks): comprehensive discussion meeting band descriptor","marks":8}]}}]`),oO={paper_id:Z3,qualification:eO,component:tO,title:aO,year:rO,session:nO,total_marks:iO,questions:sO},cO="J277-01-TeacherVariant-E",lO="J277",dO="01",uO="Computer Systems",pO=null,hO="TeacherVariant-E",mO=80,fO=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"number base conversion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following table contains either the binary, denary or hexadecimal value of three numbers."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by filling in the three missing values."},{"type":"table","headers":["8-bit Binary","Denary","Hexadecimal"],"rows":[["10110010","","B2"],["","75","4B"],["11001100","204",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each correct cell. Binary answers must be 8 bits."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1 denary: 178","Row 2 binary: 01001011","Row 3 hexadecimal: CC","Accept lower-case hexadecimal letters (cc).","Reject answers given in the wrong base."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"10110010 converted to denary = 178","marks":1},{"criteria":"75 converted to 8-bit binary = 01001011","marks":1},{"criteria":"11001100 converted to hexadecimal = CC","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary shift","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The 8-bit binary value 11011000 is shifted right by 2 places."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the result of this shift, including the new 8-bit value and its effect on the original number."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for the correct shifted value, 1 mark for the effect."},{"type":"list","items":["Result value: 00110110.","Effect: the original number is divided by 4 (or divided by 2 to the power 2).","Accept: integer division by 4.","Reject: 'multiplied by' or any reference to a left shift."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Shifted value identified as 00110110","marks":1},{"criteria":"Effect described as division by 4","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(c)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary conversion","marks":1,"command_word":"convert","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert the 8-bit binary value 01010101 into denary."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for the correct denary value."},{"type":"list","items":["Correct answer: 85.","Reject any answer not equal to 85."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"01010101 converted to denary = 85","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"fetch-execute cycle","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The CPU continually carries out the fetch-execute cycle."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe what happens during the fetch and decode stages of this cycle."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for a correct description of fetch and 1 mark for a correct description of decode."},{"type":"list","items":["Fetch: the next instruction is copied from main memory (RAM) into the CPU using the address held in the program counter.","Decode: the control unit interprets the fetched instruction so the CPU knows what action to perform.","Accept references to MAR, MDR or program counter being incremented as part of fetch.","Reject answers that only describe the execute stage."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Fetch stage described (instruction copied from memory to CPU)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Decode stage described (control unit interprets the instruction)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"CPU performance","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A student is comparing two laptops with different clock speeds and different cache sizes."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how clock speed and cache size each affect CPU performance."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Up to 2 marks for clock speed and up to 2 marks for cache size. Award 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for a linked explanation in each case."},{"type":"list","items":["Clock speed: the number of fetch-execute cycles the CPU can complete per second (1) so a higher clock speed allows more instructions to be processed in a given time, improving performance (1).","Cache size: a small, fast memory inside or close to the CPU that stores frequently used instructions and data (1) so a larger cache means fewer slow trips to RAM, reducing wait time and improving performance (1).","Accept references to GHz for clock speed and to L1, L2 or L3 cache.","Reject vague statements such as 'it is faster' with no link to instructions or data."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Clock speed identified as cycles per second","marks":1},{"criteria":"Higher clock speed linked to more instructions per second","marks":1},{"criteria":"Cache identified as fast memory storing frequently used data or instructions","marks":1},{"criteria":"Larger cache linked to fewer accesses to RAM and improved performance","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)","parent_question":3,"topic":"network topology","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A small office connects 5 client devices using a star topology."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe how a star topology connects the 5 client devices, including the role of any central component."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct point, up to 3 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Each of the 5 client devices is connected by its own cable or wireless link to a central device (1).","The central device is a switch (or hub or wireless access point) that forwards data between the connected devices (1).","Devices do not connect directly to each other; all data passes through the central device (1).","Accept: if one cable fails only that device is affected.","Reject: descriptions of a bus, ring or mesh topology."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Each device connects to a central device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Central device named as switch (or hub) that forwards data","marks":1},{"criteria":"All communication passes through the central device","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"MAC addresses","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the role of a MAC address on a network."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct point, up to 3 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["A MAC address uniquely identifies a network interface card (NIC) on a device (1).","It is assigned by the manufacturer and does not change (1).","It is used to deliver data frames to the correct device on a local area network (1).","Accept: 48-bit address often shown as 12 hexadecimal digits.","Reject: descriptions of an IP address."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Uniquely identifies a NIC or device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Assigned by manufacturer and permanent","marks":1},{"criteria":"Used to route frames to the correct device on a LAN","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(c)","parent_question":3,"topic":"network security","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school is concerned about malware spreading across its network."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two methods the school can use to prevent malware infection."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 2 marks for one method and 1 mark for a second method, or split 2 + 1 in either order. Identification with no description gains 1 mark only."},{"type":"list","items":["Anti-malware software: scans files and emails for known malicious signatures and quarantines or removes infected files.","Firewall: monitors incoming and outgoing network traffic and blocks suspicious connections that could carry malware.","User access controls: restrict which users can install software so untrusted programs cannot be added by students.","Patching/updates: keeping operating system and applications updated removes vulnerabilities that malware exploits.","Staff and student training: teaches users not to open suspicious email attachments or click unknown links.","Accept any other valid prevention method with a clear description.","Reject: backups (these are recovery, not prevention)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First method identified and described","marks":2},{"criteria":"Second method identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(d)","parent_question":3,"topic":"sound file size","marks":3,"command_word":"calculate","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A 30-second sound clip is recorded in mono at a sample rate of 44,100 Hz with a bit depth of 16 bits."},{"type":"text","value":"Calculate the file size of the sound clip in kilobytes (KB). Show your working."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award marks for working as well as the final answer."},{"type":"list","items":["Total bits = 44100 x 16 x 30 = 21,168,000 bits (1).","Convert to bytes = 21,168,000 / 8 = 2,646,000 bytes (1).","Convert to KB = 2,646,000 / 1000 = 2646 KB, or / 1024 = approximately 2584 KB (1).","Accept either 2646 KB (decimal) or approximately 2584 KB (binary).","Accept correct answer with no working for full marks.","Reject: answers left in bits or bytes only."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct calculation of total bits (sample rate x bit depth x duration)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct conversion from bits to bytes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct conversion to KB with appropriate units","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the term embedded system."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for a clear definition."},{"type":"list","items":["A computer system built into another device to carry out a specific, dedicated task.","Accept: a special purpose computer integrated within a larger device.","Reject: any answer that describes a general purpose computer such as a laptop or desktop PC."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Clear definition: dedicated computer built into a larger device for a specific task","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insulin pump is a small medical device that delivers measured doses of insulin to a patient throughout the day."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why an insulin pump is an example of an embedded system. Give three reasons."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct reason, up to 3 marks. Each point must be linked to the insulin pump context."},{"type":"list","items":["It performs a single dedicated task: delivering insulin doses, rather than running general purpose software (1).","It is built into the larger medical device with its hardware and software permanently configured for that role (1).","It needs to operate reliably in real time so that doses are delivered at the correct moment (1).","It has limited inputs and outputs (sensors, buttons and a small screen) suited only to its task (1).","It uses low power so it can run on a small battery for extended periods (1).","Accept any other valid reason linked to the insulin pump context.","Reject generic statements with no link to the medical context."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid context-linked reason","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid context-linked reason","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third valid context-linked reason","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following table contains four statements about embedded systems."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show whether the statement is true or false."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","True","False"],"rows":[["Embedded systems usually run a full general purpose operating system such as Windows.","",""],["Embedded systems are designed to perform a specific, dedicated task.","",""],["Embedded systems are commonly found in washing machines and microwave ovens.","",""],["Embedded systems can never include a microcontroller.","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each correct row."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1: False.","Row 2: True.","Row 3: True.","Row 4: False.","Do not award the mark for a row if both boxes are ticked."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 correct (False)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 correct (True)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 correct (True)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 correct (False)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(d)","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two reasons why embedded systems are commonly used in medical devices."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for a linked explanation, up to 4 marks total."},{"type":"list","items":["Reliability: dedicated hardware and software run a single task (1) so the device is less likely to crash or be affected by other programs, which is critical for patient safety (1).","Real-time response: the system can react instantly to sensor readings (1) so for example a heart monitor can immediately raise an alarm when a reading falls outside safe limits (1).","Low power consumption: simple hardware uses little electricity (1) which allows portable medical devices such as pacemakers to run on a small battery for years (1).","Compact size: an embedded system can be miniaturised (1) so it fits inside small wearable or implantable medical devices (1).","Lower cost per unit: dedicated chips are cheaper to manufacture in volume (1) which keeps the price of essential medical equipment affordable (1).","Accept any other valid reason with a linked explanation.","Reject vague benefits such as 'it is good' with no link to medical use."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First reason identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First reason linked to medical context","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second reason identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second reason linked to medical context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":5,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table below lists five tasks performed by an operating system."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show which operating system function performs each task."},{"type":"table","headers":["Task","Memory management","Process management","File management","Peripheral management","User management"],"rows":[["Allocates RAM to running applications and reclaims it when no longer needed.","","","","",""],["Schedules which task uses the CPU next.","","","","",""],["Organises documents into folders and controls access permissions.","","","","",""],["Communicates with a connected printer using the correct device driver.","","","","",""],["Allows different family members to log in with their own username and password.","","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each correct row."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1: Memory management.","Row 2: Process management.","Row 3: File management.","Row 4: Peripheral management.","Row 5: User management.","Do not award the mark for a row if more than one box is ticked."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 correct (Memory management)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 correct (Process management)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 correct (File management)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 correct (Peripheral management)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 5 correct (User management)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the operating system manages files on a computer. Your answer should refer to file hierarchy, permissions and storage locations."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each of the three areas covered with a clear explanation."},{"type":"list","items":["Hierarchy: the OS organises files into a structure of folders and subfolders so users can locate files easily (1).","Permissions: the OS controls which users can read, write, edit or delete each file based on their account, protecting private data (1).","Storage locations: the OS keeps track of where each file is physically stored on the disk and updates this when files are moved, copied or deleted (1).","Accept references to file naming conventions or file extensions linked to the relevant area.","Reject: references only to opening or saving files with no explanation of how the OS manages this."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Hierarchy of folders explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Permissions for users explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"Tracking physical storage locations explained","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the operating system provides a user interface and what types of user interface may be available."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 3 marks for any combination of the points below."},{"type":"list","items":["The OS provides a user interface so the user can interact with the hardware and software without needing technical knowledge (1).","A graphical user interface (GUI) uses windows, icons, menus and pointers, allowing users to interact by clicking or tapping (1).","A command line interface (CLI) requires the user to type text commands, which is faster for skilled users and uses fewer resources (1).","A menu-driven or voice-controlled interface may also be provided for accessibility or for limited devices (1).","Accept any two relevant interface types named with a brief description.","Reject: simply naming an interface with no description (only credit one such mark)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Purpose of user interface explained","marks":1},{"criteria":"GUI described","marks":1},{"criteria":"CLI or other interface type described","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe how virtual memory is used by the operating system."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct point, up to 3 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["When RAM becomes full the OS uses an area of secondary storage as additional memory (1).","Sections of data not currently being used are moved out of RAM into virtual memory (1).","When that data is needed again it is swapped back into RAM, replacing other inactive data (1).","Accept references to 'paging' or to the page file.","Reject: any suggestion that virtual memory is faster than RAM."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Virtual memory uses secondary storage when RAM is full","marks":1},{"criteria":"Inactive data moved out of RAM into virtual memory","marks":1},{"criteria":"Data swapped back to RAM when needed","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A family shares one home computer. Each member has their own user account."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe how user accounts on a shared computer keep each family member's data separate."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct point, up to 3 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["Each user must log in with their own username and password before they can use the computer (1).","The OS gives each account its own personal folders such as Documents and Pictures that other users cannot access by default (1).","Permissions stop one user from reading or editing another user's private files (1).","Each account stores its own preferences, desktop layout and installed apps so settings do not interfere with each other (1).","Accept references to administrator versus standard accounts limiting what children can install.","Reject: 'parental controls' alone without linking to data separation."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Login with username and password","marks":1},{"criteria":"Separate personal folders per account","marks":1},{"criteria":"Permissions prevent access to other users' files","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(f)","parent_question":5,"topic":"operating system","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A user plugs a new printer into a computer using a USB cable."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain what the operating system does so that the printer can be used."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award 1 mark for each distinct point, up to 3 marks."},{"type":"list","items":["The OS detects that a new device has been connected to the USB port (1).","The OS identifies the printer and locates the correct device driver, either already installed or downloaded automatically (1).","The driver allows the OS to translate print jobs into instructions the printer understands so applications can send documents to it (1).","Accept references to managing the print queue or sharing the printer between users.","Reject: 'the printer just works' with no description of the OS process."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"OS detects the new USB device","marks":1},{"criteria":"OS loads the appropriate device driver","marks":1},{"criteria":"Driver enables communication so applications can print","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"legislation","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table below contains four scenarios involving the use of computers."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the name of the UK law that most closely applies to each scenario."},{"type":"table","headers":["Scenario","UK law"],"rows":[["A user gains access to a school network without permission to read other students' coursework.",""],["A company stores customers' addresses but fails to keep them secure.",""],["A student copies an entire textbook and shares it for free on a website.",""],["A retailer disposes of unwanted printers and laptops in general waste rather than at a recycling point.",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each correctly named law."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1: Computer Misuse Act 1990.","Row 2: Data Protection Act 2018 (accept GDPR).","Row 3: Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.","Row 4: Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment Regulations 2013 (accept WEEE Regulations).","Accept the act name without the year.","Reject: 'data protection law' without naming the Act for row 2 only if no further detail is given."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 Computer Misuse Act identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 Data Protection Act identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 Copyright, Designs and Patents Act identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 WEEE Regulations identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)","parent_question":6,"topic":"environmental impact","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Many smartphone owners replace their phone every one to two years."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain two environmental impacts of frequent smartphone upgrades."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 2 marks for each impact: 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for a linked explanation, up to 4 marks total."},{"type":"list","items":["Increased e-waste in landfill (1) because old phones contain toxic substances such as lead and mercury that can leach into soil and water (1).","Greater extraction of rare earth metals (1) which causes habitat destruction, water pollution and high carbon emissions during mining (1).","Higher energy and resource use in manufacturing new devices (1) which increases greenhouse gas emissions linked to climate change (1).","Lithium battery production (1) which uses large quantities of water in already dry regions and creates hazardous waste (1).","Accept any other valid environmental impact with a clear explanation.","Reject: purely social or economic impacts."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First environmental impact identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First impact explained with consequence","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second environmental impact identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second impact explained with consequence","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(c)","parent_question":6,"topic":"cultural impact","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Many people are continually connected to social media through their smartphones."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two cultural impacts of constant connectivity to social media."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 2 marks for each impact: 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for a linked description, up to 4 marks total. Impacts can be positive or negative."},{"type":"list","items":["Changes to communication: people interact more often through short messages and posts (1) which can weaken face-to-face social skills, especially among younger users (1).","Spread of news and ideas is faster (1) allowing global movements and campaigns to gain support quickly, but also enabling misinformation to spread widely (1).","Pressure to present a certain image online (1) which can affect mental health and self-esteem when users compare themselves to filtered or edited content (1).","Greater inclusion of remote or marginalised groups (1) who can find communities and support networks that were not accessible before (1).","Cyberbullying becomes harder to escape because contact is constant (1) which has led to changes in school policies and safeguarding (1).","Accept any other valid cultural impact with a clear description.","Reject: technical or environmental impacts."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First cultural impact identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First impact described with effect on people","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second cultural impact identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second impact described with effect on people","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(d)","parent_question":6,"topic":"environmental impact","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two ways smartphone manufacturers can reduce e-waste."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award up to 2 marks for each method: 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for a linked description, up to 4 marks total."},{"type":"list","items":["Modular design (1) so individual parts such as the battery or screen can be replaced rather than the whole phone being thrown away (1).","Take-back or trade-in schemes (1) where customers return old phones for refurbishment, resale or proper recycling (1).","Longer software support (1) by providing security updates for many years so older phones remain safe and useful (1).","Use of recycled materials (1) such as recycled aluminium or rare earth metals reclaimed from old devices (1).","Standardised parts and chargers (1) so accessories such as USB-C cables can be reused with future devices, reducing waste (1).","Repair-friendly designs and supplying spare parts to independent repairers (1) which extends device lifetime (1).","Accept any other valid manufacturer action with a clear description.","Reject: actions that only the consumer or government can take."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First manufacturer method identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First method described with link to reducing e-waste","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second manufacturer method identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second method described with link to reducing e-waste","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(e)","parent_question":6,"topic":"e-waste impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Smartphone manufacturers release new flagship phones every year. Many users replace their phone within 24 months, even when their existing device still works."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative impacts of frequent smartphone upgrades. In your answer you should include the environmental, cultural and legal issues raised by this trend."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Levels-of-response banded mark scheme."},{"type":"table","headers":["Mark band","Marks","Descriptor"],"rows":[["Mark Band 3 - High Level","6-8","Thorough knowledge and understanding of the impacts of frequent smartphone upgrades. The discussion gives a balanced view of positive and negative impacts and clearly addresses all three issues (environmental, cultural and legal) with well-developed, linked points and accurate use of terminology."],["Mark Band 2 - Mid Level","3-5","Reasonable knowledge and understanding. The discussion considers more than one impact and addresses at least two of the three issues. Some points are developed but the response may be unbalanced or lack supporting detail."],["Mark Band 1 - Low Level","1-2","Basic knowledge with limited understanding. The response identifies one or two relevant points but lacks development. Use of terminology is limited and only one issue area is addressed."],["0 marks","0","No attempt or response not worthy of credit."]]},{"type":"text","value":"Indicative content (not exhaustive; points may cover more than one issue)."},{"type":"list","items":["Environmental: mining of rare earth metals such as cobalt and neodymium causes habitat destruction, water pollution and high carbon emissions in extracting countries.","Environmental: lithium battery production uses large amounts of water in dry regions and produces hazardous chemical waste.","Environmental: discarded phones in landfill leak heavy metals such as lead and mercury into soil and groundwater, harming ecosystems.","Environmental: only a small percentage of e-waste is currently recycled globally, so most materials are lost from the supply chain.","Environmental: manufacturer take-back and trade-in schemes can recover useful materials and reduce the need for fresh mining.","Cultural: social pressure and advertising encourage users to see the latest model as a status symbol, driving unnecessary upgrades.","Cultural: a thriving second-hand and refurbished device market gives lower-income users access to capable phones at a fraction of the price.","Cultural: newer phones often include accessibility improvements such as better screen readers, larger text and improved cameras for sign language users.","Cultural: constant upgrading normalises a throwaway culture and may shape attitudes towards other consumer goods.","Legal: the Waste Electrical and Electronic Equipment (WEEE) Regulations 2013 require manufacturers and retailers in the UK to take back old devices for proper recycling.","Legal: planned obsolescence, where devices are designed to fail or stop receiving updates after a short period, has prompted right to repair legislation in the UK and EU.","Legal: data protection law requires users and refurbishers to wipe personal data securely from old devices before resale or recycling.","Legal: when manufacturers stop providing security updates, older phones become non-compliant with workplace data protection requirements, effectively forcing upgrades.","Balanced view: frequent upgrades can support innovation, jobs in the technology sector and improvements in safety features, but the cumulative environmental and ethical costs are significant and increasingly regulated."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Mark Band 3 (6-8 marks): comprehensive discussion meeting band descriptor","marks":8}]}}]`),gO={paper_id:cO,qualification:lO,component:dO,title:uO,year:pO,session:hO,total_marks:mO,questions:fO},_O="J277-01-2024-June",yO="J277",wO="01",bO="Computer Systems",kO=2024,vO="June",xO=80,CO=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary and denary conversion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following table has either the binary or denary value of 3 numbers."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by converting the 8-bit binary number into denary and the denary number into 8-bit binary."},{"type":"table","headers":["8-bit Binary","Denary"],"rows":[["11110000",""],["","105"],["00011110",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each. Binary must be 8 bits. Row 1: 11110000 = 240. Row 2: 105 = 01101001. Row 3: 00011110 = 30.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"11110000 converted to denary = 240","marks":1},{"criteria":"105 converted to 8-bit binary = 01101001","marks":1},{"criteria":"00011110 converted to denary = 30","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27701_Computer_systems_June_2024 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-1-2024-june/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-1-2024-june/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-1-2024-june/dev-qa/q-1-a.png"}},{"question_id":"1(b)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary number properties","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the answer to each statement."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","Answer"],"rows":[["The smallest denary number that can be represented by a 4-bit binary number",""],["The largest denary number that can be represented by a 6-bit binary number",""],["The maximum number of different colours that can be represented with a colour depth of 7-bits",""],["The minimum number of bits needed to represent 150 different characters in a character set",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark each. Accept calculations that equate to the same answer. Accept any number of 0s for the first answer."},{"type":"list","items":["Smallest denary number in 4-bit binary = 0.","Largest denary number in 6-bit binary = 63.","Maximum number of colours with 7-bit colour depth = 128.","Minimum bits needed for 150 characters = 8."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Smallest denary number represented by 4 bits = 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Largest denary number represented by 6 bits = 63","marks":1},{"criteria":"Maximum colours with 7-bit colour depth = 128","marks":1},{"criteria":"Minimum bits needed for 150 characters = 8","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27701_Computer_systems_June_2024 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-1-2024-june/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-1-2024-june/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/p