 correctly outputs both True and False. BP2 and BP3 must check for both ends of the range — must check that input data is not negative. Example: miles = input(...); age = input(...); valid = True; if miles > 10000 or miles < 0 then valid = False; elseif age > 5 or age < 0 then valid = False; endif; print(valid).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Miles and age input separately (two input statements, stored in sensible variables)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid mileage (miles < 10000 or equivalent)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid age (age <= 5 or equivalent)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks both values are >= 0 / not negative","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly outputs both True (when valid) and False (when invalid)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"test data types","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The validation routine from part (i) must be tested with normal, erroneous and boundary test data."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify suitable test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test type","Miles","Age"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Erroneous","",""],["Boundary","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per row to max 3. Specific data must be given, not a description. Normal: miles in the range 0 to 9,999 and age in the range 0 to 5 (e.g. 7,000 and 3). Erroneous: miles less than 0 or greater than 9,999 and age less than 0 or greater than 5 / non-numeric data (e.g. 12,000 and 7). Boundary: miles of -1, 0, 9,999 or 10,000 and age of -1, 0, 5 or 6 (e.g. 10,000 and 5).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal row: miles in 0-9,999 AND age in 0-5 (specific numeric values, e.g. 7,000 and 3)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous row: miles < 0 or > 9,999 AND age < 0 or > 5 (or non-numeric), e.g. 12,000 and 7","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary row: miles = -1 / 0 / 9,999 / 10,000 AND age = -1 / 0 / 5 / 6 (e.g. 10,000 and 5)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(iii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"iterative testing definition","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify when iterative testing is performed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. Accept: during development / whilst writing the program / before development is complete.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"During development / whilst writing the program / before development is complete","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-iii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"battery charge algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership sells electric cars, which require charging before they can be driven. Charging the battery by 1% takes 10 minutes."},{"type":"text","value":"For example, a battery has 80% charge. It would take 200 minutes, or 3 hours and 20 minutes to charge to 100%."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:"},{"type":"text","value":"- asks the user for the current battery charge percentage\\n- outputs \`\\"full\\"\` for a battery currently at 100%\\n- calculates how long this battery would take to charge\\n- outputs this in hours and minutes."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 6. Points: inputs the current battery charge percentage; outputs \\"full\\" if the charge is 100%; calculates the amount to charge (100 - current); calculates the time in minutes (amount * 10); converts to hours and minutes (e.g. using DIV and MOD, or repeated division); outputs the time in hours and minutes. Allow FT for the final bullet if a reasonable attempt at conversion has been made. Allow output of 0 hours 0 minutes if full. Example: charge = input(\\"enter battery charge\\"); if charge == 100 then print(\\"full\\") else time = (100-charge) * 10; hours = time DIV 60; mins = time MOD 60; print(hours, mins); endif.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs the current battery charge percentage and stores it in a variable","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"full\\" when the charge is 100%","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the amount still to charge (e.g. 100 - charge)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the time in minutes (amount to charge multiplied by 10)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Converts the time into hours and minutes (e.g. DIV 60 / MOD 60)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs the time in hours and minutes","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-021.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-c.png"}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"SQL query selection","marks":3,"command_word":"tick","content"