gorithm to calculate the price of an item during a sale period."},{"type":"text","value":"[Refer to flowchart in paper — discounts £20 off if price > £100 and not > £200, or £40 off if price > £200.]"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Price input","Test type","Expected price output"],"rows":[["50","Normal",""],["100","Boundary",""],["150","Normal",""],["200","Boundary",""],["250","Normal",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark if two of the expected outputs are correct; 2 marks if four are correct; 3 marks if all five are correct. Expected outputs: 50 → 50, 100 → 100, 150 → 130, 200 → 180, 250 → 210.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"At least 2 expected outputs correct (from: 50→50, 100→100, 150→130, 200→180, 250→210)","marks":1},{"criteria":"At least 4 expected outputs correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"All 5 expected outputs correct (50, 100, 130, 180, 210)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"flowchart to code","marks":6,"command_word":"rewrite","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewrite the algorithm above."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet point to max 6. High-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required — do not accept pseudocode / natural language. Points: input and store price; check if price is > 200; if true, reduce price by 40; check if price is > 100 and not > 200; if true, reduce price by 20; output price. BP4 must check that price is both larger than 100 and not larger than 200 (may be implicit, e.g. via elseif). Example: price = input(\\"enter price\\"); if price > 200 then price = price - 40 elseif price > 100 then price = price - 20 endif; print(price).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input price and store it in a variable","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 200 (sensible check against the higher threshold)","marks":1},{"criteria":"If that check is true, reduce the price by 40","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 100 and not > 200 (e.g. via elseif)","marks":1},{"criteria":"If that check is true, reduce the price by 20","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output the (possibly discounted) price","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"5(c)","parent_question":5,"topic":"debug program errors","marks":5,"command_word":"refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An item is classified as \\"In demand\\" if OCR Tech have between 5 and 25 inclusive in stock."},{"type":"text","value":"A program is written that allows the user to input the current stock level and output whether the item is in demand or not."},{"type":"code","value":"stocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel >= 5 or =< 25 then\\n print(Not in demand)\\nelse\\n print(In demand)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"The program contains syntax and logic errors."},{"type":"text","value":"Refine the program to correct the errors and write the refined version of the program."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet point to max 5. High-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required — do not accept pseudocode / natural language. Points: checks both values (e.g. or changed to and, if appropriate); if statement is in correct format (e.g. checks against stocklevel for each condition); if statement uses correct comparisons (e.g. >= and <=); print statements in correct position (In demand for the range, Not in demand outside); print statements include string delimiters (e.g. speech marks) around both string outputs. Example: stocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\"); if stocklevel >= 5 and stocklevel <= 25 then print(\\"In demand\\") else print(\\"Not in demand\\") endif.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks both values (e.g. changes 'or' to 'and' when using inclusive range)","marks":1},{"criteria":"If statement in correct format — stocklevel referenced on both sides of the AND/OR","marks":1},{"criteria":"If statement uses correct comparison operators (>= and <=)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Print statements are in the correct position (In demand when in range, Not in demand otherwise)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Print statements include string delimiters (e.g. speech marks) around both string outputs","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-014.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-023.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-c.png"}},{"question_id":"5(d)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"function algorithm completion","marks":5,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Customers can use a discount code to reduce the price of their purchase. Valid discount codes and their value (in pounds) are stored in a global two-dimensional (2D) array with the identifier \`discount\`. The following table shows part of this 2D array."},{"type":"table","headers":["","0","1"],"rows":[["0","PVFC7","10"],["1","CPU5","5"],["2","BGF2","15"]]},{"type":"text","value":"For example, \`discount[2,0]\` holds discount code \`BGF2\` and \`discount[2,1]\` holds the discount of 15 pounds."},{"type":"text","value":"A function searches through the 2D array and applies the discount to the price. The price and discount code are passed in as parameters. The algorithm design is not complete."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the design for the algorithm."},{"type":"code","value":"function checkdiscount(price, code)\\n newprice = price\\n size = len(discount) - 1\\n for x = 0 to ____\\n  if discount[x,0] == ____ then\\n   newprice = ____ - discount[____]\\n  endif\\n next x\\n ____\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet point, in the correct place, to max 5. Gap 1 (loop upper bound): size (or len(discount) - 1). Gap 2 (comparison value): code. Gap 3 (subtract from): price (or newprice). Gap 4 (array index): [x,1] (or [x][1]). Gap 5 (after next x): return newprice (or checkdiscount = newprice).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Gap 1: size (or len(discount) - 1)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 2: code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 3: price (or newprice)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 4: [x,1] (or [x][1])","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 5: return newprice (or checkdiscount = newprice)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-d-i.png"}},{"question_id":"5(d)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"function variables identification","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify two variables used in this function design."},{"type":"text","value":"1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"2: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 2. Accept any two of: newprice, size, x. Also accept price, code, discount. Do not penalise capitalisation.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid variable identified (from: newprice, size, x, price, code, discount)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid variable identified (different from the first)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-d-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"5(d)(iii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"discount code program","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write a program that:"},{"type":"text","value":"- asks the user for an item price and discount code\\n- uses the checkdiscount() function from part (i) to calculate the price of the item after any discount has been applied\\n- repeats bullet points 1 and 2 until a price of 0 is entered\\n- outputs the total cost of all items entered, after any discounts have been applied."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet point to max 6. High-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required — do not accept pseudocode / natural language. Points: asks for price and discount code to be input; passes both to the checkdiscount() function as parameters; stores / uses the returned value; calculates a runni