ing_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-025.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-b.png"}},{"question_id":"7(c)","parent_question":7,"topic":"test plan completion","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pilot\'s flying experience is validated. An algorithm checks that the experience is between 0 and 20 years."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr","value":"exp = input(\\"Enter number of years\\")\\n\\nif exp >= 0 and exp <= 20 then\\n  print(True)\\nelse\\n  print(False)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete this test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Experience in years","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","True"],["20","Boundary",""],["32","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark each for: any value between 0 and 20, expected output True for 20, invalid/erroneous or sensible alternative for 32, and expected output False for 32.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Provides any normal value between 0 and 20","marks":1},{"criteria":"Expected output for 20 is True","marks":1},{"criteria":"Identifies 32 as invalid/erroneous or a sensible equivalent","marks":1},{"criteria":"Expected output for 32 is False","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-014.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-025.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-c.png"}},{"question_id":"7(d)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"pay calculation algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Pilots are paid a set amount each day. Pilots also get an additional payment for each mile they have flown that day. These payments are shown in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Flying experience","Pay per day","Pay per mile"],"rows":[["Fewer than 2 years","£120.00","£0.45"],["2 years to 5 years inclusive","£150.00","£0.65"],["More than 5 years","£180.00","£0.85"]]},{"type":"text","value":"For example, a pilot with 3 years\' experience who flies 100 miles in one day will receive £215.00 pay:\\n- £150.00 pay for the day\\n- £65.00 additional payment (£0.65 pay per mile x 100 miles)\\n- Add £150.00 and £65.00 together to get £215.00 total pay"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the algorithm to:\\n- Calculate the total pay for the pilot for that day\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr","value":"experience = input(\\"Enter years of experience\\")\\nmiles = input(\\"Enter miles flown\\")\\ntotalPay = 0"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Award marks for selection and correct calculations in each band. Follow through for the final band only where a reasonable attempt at calculating pay has been made."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr","value":"if experience < 2 then\\n  totalPay = 120 + (0.45 * miles)\\nelseif experience <= 5 then\\n  totalPay = 150 + (0.65 * miles)\\nelse\\n  totalPay = 180 + (0.85 * miles)\\nendif"}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Attempts to use selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for fewer than 2 years\' experience","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for 2 to 5 years inclusive","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for more than 5 years\' experience","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-015.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-026.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-d-i.png"}},{"question_id":"7(d)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"function refactoring","marks":2,"command_word":"refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The programmer decides to make a function to calculate the total pay for the pilot.\\n\\nThe function is called `calculatePay()`.\\n\\nThe function takes the values for experience and miles as two parameters and returns the total pay for the pilot."},{"type":"text","value":"Refine the algorithm to use this function and output the pay for the pilot."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr","value":"experience = input(\\"Enter years of experience\\")\\nmiles = input(\\"Enter miles flown\\")\\n____ = calculatePay( ____ , ____ )\\nprint(totalPay)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct completion: `totalPay = calculatePay(experience, miles)`. Accept the parameters in either order where logically valid.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Assigns the function return value to totalPay","marks":1},{"criteria":"Passes experience and miles as parameters to calculatePay()","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-011.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-026.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-d-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"7(e)","parent_question":7,"topic":"file write algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"design","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A text file is used to store pilot codes and dates of birth.\\n\\n\\nThe text file is named `pilots.txt`. The contents of some of this file are shown:"},{"type":"code","value":"JP670,17/05/1986\\nCC200,31/06/1980\\nSM720,01/04/1976\\nGC730,06/06/1978"},{"type":"text","value":"Design an algorithm to:\\n- ask the user to input a pilot code and a date of birth\\n- write these inputs to the `pilots.txt` text file."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"One mark per bullet point. Award the file-close mark for implicit closing, for example Python `with`. Allow data to be written either as both values or as one concatenated string separated by a comma."},{"type":"list","items":["Ask the user for two inputs and store/use them","Open `pilots.txt` for write or append","Write both inputs to the opened text file","Close the text file"]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Asks for and stores/uses pilot code and date of birth","marks":1},{"criteria":"Opens pilots.txt for write or append","marks":1},{"criteria":"Writes both inputs to the file","marks":1},{"criteria":"Closes the file or uses an implicit close","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-017.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-025.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-e.png"}},{"question_id":"7(f)","parent_question":7,"topic":"2D array pilot validation","marks":6,"command_word":"create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pilot is allowed to fly a maximum of 9 hours per day. The pilot code and the hours flown for each flight in one day are stored in a 2D array of strings with the identifier `journeys`.\\n\\nThe data in `journeys` is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","[0]","[1]"],"rows":[["[0]","\\"SM720\\"","\\"4.5\\""],["[1]","\\"GC730\\"","\\"3\\""],["[2]","\\"JP670\\"","\\"2\\""],["[3]","\\"GC730\\"","\\"3.5\\""],["[4]","\\"CC200\\"","\\"9\\""],["[5]","\\"RY320\\"","\\"12\\""]]},{"type":"text","value":"The value of `journeys[3,1]` is `\\"3.5\\"`."},{"type":"text","value":"Create a function, `pilotValid()`, that:\\n- takes a pilot code as a parameter\\n- uses the 2D array `journeys` to calculate the number of hours that the pilot has flown\\n- returns `valid` if the pilot has flown 9 hours or fewer or `warning` if the pilot has flown for more than 9 hours\\n- uses casting where needed.\\n\\nYou must use either OCR Exam Reference Language or a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"One mark per bullet point to max 6. Do not allow casting to integer where decimal journeys are present. Allow follow-through for the check and return marks if a reasonable calculation attempt has been made. Allow follow-through if values are output instead of returned."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr","value":"function pilotValid(pilotCode)\\n  total = 0\\n  for i = 0 to 5\\n    if journeys[i,0] == pilotCode then\\n      temp = float(journeys[i,1])\\n      total = total + temp\\n    endif\\n  next i\\n  if total > 9 then\\n    return \\"warning\\"\\n  else\\n    return \\"valid\\"\\n  endif\\nendfunction"}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function header pilotValid() with at least one parameter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks array element [i,0] for each item","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the total hours","marks":1},{"criteria":"Casts journey hours to float/real","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks whether the total is 9 hours or fewer","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns warning and valid correctly","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/question/page-018.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2023-practice/dev-qa/q-7-f.png"}}]'),XT={"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2023 (2).pdf"},YT={paper_id:$T,qualification:zT,component:WT,title:QT,year:VT,session:UT,total_marks:HT,questions:GT,maps:XT},KT="J277-01-2019-Practice",ZT="J277",eP="01",tP="Computer Systems",aP=2019,rP="Practice",nP=80,iP=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"character set definition","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The ASCII code for the character J is the denary number 74."},{"type":"text","value":"State what is meant by a character set."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a definition that explains a character set as all the different characters a computer can represent. Do not award 'the set of characters' as this repeats the question.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition e.g. all the different characters a computer can represent","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(a)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"ASCII character capacity","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"ASCII has 8 bits per character."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the maximum number of different characters that ASCII can represent."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only: 256.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct answer: 256","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(a)(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"file size calculation","marks":2,"command_word":"calculate","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A text file uses the ASCII character set. The text file has 2000 characters in it."},{"type":"text","value":"Calculate an estimate of the file size of the text file in Kilobytes. Show your working."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for working, 1 mark for answer. Example working: 2000 * 8 = 16000 bits; 16000 / 8 = 2000 bytes; 2000 / 1000 = 2 Kilobytes. Ignore any overheads e.g. adding 10%.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Working shown (e.g. 2000 * 8 = 16000 bits, then / 8 = 2000 bytes, then / 1000)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct answer: 2 Kilobytes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(a)(iv)","parent_question":1,"topic":"alternative character set","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one other character set."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming another character set. Accept: UNICODE.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"UNICODE (or Unicode)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)","parent_question":1,"topic":"ASCII binary conversion","marks":1,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write the 8-bit binary number for the ASCII character J in the following boxes:"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only (cao): 01001010.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct answer: 01001010","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(c)","parent_question":1,"topic":"hexadecimal conversion","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the hexadecimal number for the ASCII character K."},{"type":"text","value":"Show your working."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for adding 1 to J (binary 01001011) or showing the conversion from J to hexadecimal and adding 1. 1 mark for the final answer 4B. Allow 1 mark only for converting J to 4A without adding 1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Adds 1 to J (binary 01001011) / converts J to hex then adds 1","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct answer: 4B","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(d)","parent_question":1,"topic":"binary shift multiplication","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A binary shift can be performed on a binary integer."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify which shift will multiply a number by 8."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for left, 1 mark for 3 places. Ignore any reference to arithmetic/logical.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Left (shift direction)","marks":1},{"criteria":"3 places","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2","parent_question":2,"topic":"digital technology in medicine","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"*The use of digital technology in medicine is constantly evolving."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of digital technology on medicine including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- diagnosing patients\\n- treating patients\\n- storage of records."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Band 3 (6-8 marks): thorough knowledge and understanding of a wide range of considerations; material is accurate and detailed; applies knowledge directly and consistently to the context; weighs both sides of the discussion; well-developed, logically structured line of reasoning. Band 2 (3-5 marks): reasonable knowledge; material generally accurate but at times underdeveloped; applies knowledge directly but misses one or two opportunities; line of reasoning with some structure. Band 1 (1-2 marks): basic knowledge with limited understanding; unsupported assertions; unstructured communication. 0 marks: no attempt or response not worthy of credit. Indicative content (not prescriptive): Diagnosis - search records faster, identify range of possibilities based on symptoms, identify patterns, more accurate/automated/AI equipment, may miss some symptoms or suggest incorrect results. Treating - AI/automated surgery (can be controlled from another country), access to specialists who are not local, technology may be subject to hacking, software errors could have fatal consequences, may have little human interaction. Storage - centralised storage, all medical staff can access all information about patients from all people involved in care, concerns over privacy and security of data e.g. personal information may be leaked if storage is accessed.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic knowledge with limited understanding; unsupported assertions; unstructured communication","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable knowledge across a range of considerations; applies knowledge to context with one or two omissions; line of reasoning with some structure","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Thorough, detailed knowledge across diagnosis, treatment and storage; balanced discussion with weighing of both sides; well-developed, logically structured reasoning","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"CPU components identification","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table has five components of a computer, and four statements."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one or more boxes in each row to identify which component(s) each statement describes."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","MAR","MDR","Cache","Program Counter","RAM"],"rows":[["It stores a single address","","","","",""],["It stores frequently used instructions","","","","",""],["It is a register","","","","",""],["It stores all currently running data and instructions","","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for correct ticks and gaps on each row. If any extra ticks on a row, 0 marks for that row. Row 1 'It stores a single address': tick MAR only. Row 2 'It stores frequently used instructions': tick Cache only. Row 3 'It is a register': tick MAR, MDR and Program Counter. Row 4 'It stores all currently running data and instructions': tick RAM only.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 correct: MAR ticked only (for 'It stores a single address')","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 correct: Cache ticked only (for 'It stores frequently used instructions')","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 correct: MAR, MDR and Program Counter all ticked (for 'It is a register')","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 correct: RAM ticked only (for 'It stores all currently running data and instructions')","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"register name and purpose","marks":2,"command_word":"identify and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the name of one register not given in part (i) and describe its purpose."},{"type":"text","value":"Register: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Purpose: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming a valid register (e.g. Accumulator / ACC / Current Instruction Register / CIR). 1 mark for a matching description of its purpose (e.g. Accumulator stores the result of arithmetic operations; CIR stores the instruction currently being decoded/executed).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid register named (e.g. Accumulator / CIR)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of purpose (e.g. stores the result of arithmetic operations)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"clock speed and performance","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Computer A has a single core, 3.2 GHz processor."},{"type":"text","value":"Computer B has a single core, 1.2 GHz processor."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why Computer A will usually run faster than Computer B."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 2. Points: Computer A has a faster/higher clock speed; 3.2 GHz will run more Fetch-Execute (F-E) cycles per second; therefore more instructions can be executed per second (by calculation).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Computer A has a faster/higher clock speed, so it runs more Fetch-Execute cycles per second","marks":1},{"criteria":"Therefore more instructions can be executed per second","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"embedded systems","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following paragraph describes embedded systems."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the paragraph by selecting terms from the list and writing them in the correct places. Not all terms are used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: actuator, applications, change, functions, laptop, larger, lights, microprocessor, processor, range, smaller, washing machine"},{"type":"text","value":"Embedded systems have limited ____. They are often built into a ____ machine. Two examples of embedded systems are a ____ and automated ____ in a car."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed term. Gap 1: functions. Gap 2: larger. Gap 3: washing machine. Gap 4: lights.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Gap 1: functions","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 2: larger","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 3: washing machine","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 4: lights","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"image storage true or false","marks":2,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla is an artist. She draws images by hand. The image is then scanned and stored on a computer."},{"type":"text","value":"The table has four statements about the storage of images on a computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to identify if the statement is true or false."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","True","False"],"rows":[["Each colour has a unique binary code","",""],["Metadata stores the colour of each pixel in the image","",""],["A bitmap is made of pixels","",""],["The higher the colour depth, the smaller the number of different colours that can be displayed","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for 2 correct ticks; 2 marks for all 4 correct ticks. Two ticks in one row is incorrect. Correct answers: Each colour has a unique binary code = True; Metadata stores the colour of each pixel in the image = False; A bitmap is made of pixels = True; The higher the colour depth, the smaller the number of different colours that can be displayed = False.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"At least 2 of the 4 rows ticked correctly (True for 'unique binary code' and 'bitmap is made of pixels'; False for 'metadata stores the colour of each pixel' and 'higher colour depth, smaller number of colours')","marks":1},{"criteria":"All 4 rows ticked correctly","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"storage capacity calculation","marks":2,"command_word":"calculate","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla stores her images on a secondary storage device."},{"type":"text","value":"Each image has a fixed size of 1 MB. The storage device has a capacity of 3 GB."},{"type":"text","value":"Calculate how many images can be saved on the storage device. Show your working."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for working, 1 mark for answer. Working: 1000 * 3 (there are 1000 MB in 1 GB, so 3 GB = 3000 MB). Answer: 3000 images.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Working shown (e.g. 1000 * 3, or 3 GB = 3000 MB)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct answer: 3000 images","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"storage device selection","marks":3,"command_word":"identify and justify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla uses the images to make videos. These videos are stored on her computer's internal storage device."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the most appropriate type of storage device for Layla to use in her computer. Justify your choice."},{"type":"text","value":"Type of storage device: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Justification: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a suitable type: solid state or magnetic. Then 1 mark per bullet of justification to max 2, with points matching the chosen type. Solid state justifications: large enough capacity; can move computer without damaging storage; faster access speeds. Magnetic justifications: largest capacity; computer is not moved so moving parts do not matter; more reliable long-term.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Suitable type identified: solid state or magnetic","marks":1},{"criteria":"Justification point 1 matching the chosen type (e.g. solid state: faster access / not damaged when moved; magnetic: largest capacity / reliable long-term)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Justification point 2 matching the chosen type","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(iii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"sound storage true or false","marks":3,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The videos include sound. The table has statements about the storage of sound in a computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to identify if the statement is true or false."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","True","False"],"rows":[["The sample rate is the number of times the amplitude is recorded per second","",""],["The smaller the bit depth the smaller the range of sounds recorded","",""],["The larger the sample rate the larger the bit depth","",""],["The frequency and pitch of the sound wave are measured","",""],["Sound is stored using pixels","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for 2 correct ticks; 2 marks for 3 or 4 correct ticks; 3 marks for all 5 correct. Two ticks in one row is incorrect. Correct answers: sample rate is number of times amplitude is recorded per second = True; smaller the bit depth, smaller the range of sounds recorded = True; larger sample rate means larger bit depth = False; frequency and pitch of the sound wave are measured = False; sound is stored using pixels = False.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"At least 2 rows ticked correctly","marks":1},{"criteria":"At least 3 or 4 rows ticked correctly","marks":1},{"criteria":"All 5 rows ticked correctly (True, True, False, False, False in order)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"compression reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla uploads her images and videos to a website."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why Layla compresses the images and videos before uploading them."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 2. Points: reduces the file size; takes up less space on the server; faster upload to server; faster download for users.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reduces file size / takes up less space on the server","marks":1},{"criteria":"Faster upload to server / faster download for users","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"compression method choice","marks":3,"command_word":"identify and justify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla wants to reduce the file size of the images and videos by the largest amount possible."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the method of compression that would be most appropriate. Justify your choice."},{"type":"text","value":"Compression method: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Justification: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for lossy. 1 mark per bullet of justification to max 2. Justifications: lossy will most likely reduce the file size by a larger amount than lossless; lossy will remove data that is not noticeable, so changes allow further reduction without the user noticing. Award follow-through marks for justifying lossless appropriately to max 2.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compression method: lossy","marks":1},{"criteria":"Justification point 1 (e.g. lossy reduces file size by a larger amount than lossless)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Justification point 2 (e.g. lossy removes data that is not noticeable / user will not notice the changes)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"copyright legislation","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla wants to protect her images so they cannot be copied by other people."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify which legislation can help protect Layla's images."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming the relevant legislation: Copyright, Designs and Patents Act.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Copyright, Designs and Patents Act","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"network type identification","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir has a home network that includes two laptop computers, four mobile phones, and two televisions."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the type of network Amir has at home."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for LAN or Local Area Network.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"LAN / Local Area Network","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)(i)","parent_question":6,"topic":"switch vs router","marks":6,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The network uses a star topology with a central switch. The switch has an integrated wireless access point (WAP)."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the similarities and differences between a switch and a router."},{"type":"text","value":"Similarities: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Differences: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet. Maximum 4 marks for similarities and maximum 4 marks for differences, overall total 6. Similarities: they both connect devices; they receive data from the devices; they determine the correct destination for the data; they transmit the data to its destination. Differences: a switch uses MAC addresses; a router uses IPs; a switch connects nodes/computers; a router connects networks/Internet; a router stores the addresses of devices attached; a switch records the addresses as they are accessed (a switch has to look for the correct address before sending).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Similarity: both connect devices / receive data from devices","marks":1},{"criteria":"Similarity: determine the correct destination / transmit data to its destination","marks":1},{"criteria":"Difference: a switch uses MAC addresses, a router uses IP addresses","marks":1},{"criteria":"Difference: a switch connects nodes/computers within a network; a router connects networks / connects to the Internet","marks":1},{"criteria":"Difference: a router stores the addresses of devices attached to it","marks":1},{"criteria":"Difference: a switch records addresses as they are accessed / has to look up the correct address before sending","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)(ii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"star topology diagram","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Draw the star topology for Amir's home network. Clearly label each device."},{"type":"text","value":"[Student response: hand-drawn diagram — capture via canvas or upload]"}],"answer":{"guidance":"This is a hand-drawn diagram question — mark the diagram, not prose. 1 mark per bullet. Required: a clearly labelled switch at the centre; the correct set of devices (2 laptops, 4 phones, 2 TVs); every device connected to the switch and to nothing else.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Clearly labelled switch at the centre of the topology","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct devices shown: 2 laptops, 4 mobile phones, 2 televisions","marks":1},{"criteria":"All devices connected directly to the switch and nothing else (no other interconnections)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(c)","parent_question":6,"topic":"network security table","marks":6,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir wants to protect the computers on his network from threats such as unauthorised access."},{"type":"text","value":"The following incomplete table contains a form of attack, description and method of preventing each attack."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by writing the missing Forms of attack, Descriptions and Methods of prevention."},{"type":"table","headers":["Form of attack","Description of attack","Method of prevention"],"rows":[["","A program attempting all possible password combinations",""],["Data interception","",""],["","","Anti-virus"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed box (6 total). Row 1: Form of attack = Brute-force attack; Method of prevention = strong password / set number of password attempts / firewall. Row 2: Description = data transmission being read by an unauthorised user/program; Method of prevention = encryption. Row 3: Form of attack = Malware / Virus / Trojan (etc.); Description = software that damages/deletes data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 form of attack: Brute-force attack","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 1 method of prevention: strong password / set number of password attempts / firewall","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 description: data transmission being read by an unauthorised user/program","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 method of prevention: encryption","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 form of attack: Malware / Virus / Trojan (etc.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 description: software that damages or deletes data","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(a)","parent_question":7,"topic":"defragmentation benefits","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve runs the defragmentation program on her computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why Eve's computer is more efficient after the program has been run."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 3. Points: takes less time to read/access a file because the data/files/pages are contiguous; so the read head does not need to move as far to read the next piece of data/file/page; because it is in the next memory location; takes less time to save new data/files because there is larger free space together; so it does not need to split the data/file; and can store them in contiguous spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Takes less time to read/access a file because the data/files/pages are now contiguous","marks":1},{"criteria":"Read head does not need to move as far / data is in the next memory location","marks":1},{"criteria":"Takes less time to save new data because there is larger free space together / does not need to split files","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)","parent_question":7,"topic":"OS function categories","marks":5,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to identify which function of the Operating System deals with each action."},{"type":"table","headers":["Action","Memory management","Peripheral management","File management","User management"],"rows":[["Creating a new folder to store documents in","","","",""],["Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM","","","",""],["Renaming a file","","","",""],["Reading data from a scanner","","","",""],["Changing the password required to log on to the computer","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark p