ed, program counter incremented, instruction transferred to MDR, address placed in MAR)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid event in the fetch-execute cycle (different from the first)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"secondary storage definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Nina wants to transfer photos from a digital camera to an external secondary storage device."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by 'secondary storage'."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark only for a correct definition. Accept: long term / non-volatile storage of data / files; or external / auxiliary storage of data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Long term / non-volatile / external / auxiliary storage of data or files","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"storage types identification","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the three common types of storage Nina can choose from."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark only for each correct type, to a maximum of 3 marks. Accept: Optical, Magnetic, Solid state.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Optical","marks":1},{"criteria":"Magnetic","marks":1},{"criteria":"Solid state","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)","parent_question":2,"topic":"storage device characteristics","marks":4,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State four characteristics of secondary storage devices that Nina should consider when choosing a device."},{"type":"text","value":"1. ____"},{"type":"text","value":"2. ____"},{"type":"text","value":"3. ____"},{"type":"text","value":"4. ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct characteristic, to a maximum of 4 marks. Accept any four from: capacity / size, speed, portability, durability, reliability, cost.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid characteristic (e.g. capacity/size, speed, portability, durability, reliability, cost)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid characteristic (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third valid characteristic (different from the first two)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth valid characteristic (different from the first three)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)","parent_question":3,"topic":"RAM and ROM comparison","marks":3,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A satellite navigation system (Sat Nav) uses RAM and ROM."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show whether each of the statements is true for the RAM or ROM in a Sat Nav."},{"type":"table","headers":["","RAM","ROM"],"rows":[["Stores the boot up sequence of the Sat Nav.","",""],["The contents are lost when the Sat Nav is turned off.","",""],["Holds copies of open maps and routes.","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Award 1 mark for each correct tick. No marks should be awarded if ticks are in both boxes in a given row. Correct answers: 'Stores the boot up sequence of the Sat Nav' = ROM; 'The contents are lost when the Sat Nav is turned off' = RAM; 'Holds copies of open maps and routes' = RAM.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Stores the boot up sequence of the Sat Nav = ROM","marks":1},{"criteria":"The contents are lost when the Sat Nav is turned off = RAM","marks":1},{"criteria":"Holds copies of open maps and routes = RAM","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"embedded system definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The Sat Nav contains an embedded system. Define what is meant by an 'embedded system'."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a correct definition. Accept: a computer system that is built into another device.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A computer system that is built into another device","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(c)","parent_question":3,"topic":"embedded system examples","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify three devices, other than a Sat Nav, which contain embedded systems."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct example identified, to a maximum of 3 marks. Accept: dishwasher, MP3 player, washing machine, mobile phone, manufacturing equipment. There are many other examples of devices with embedded systems which may be acceptable.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid device with an embedded system (e.g. dishwasher, MP3 player, washing machine, mobile phone, manufacturing equipment)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid device with an embedded system (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third valid device with an embedded system (different from the first two)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"sound recording process","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computer records an audio file of someone playing a guitar."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe what happens when the computer converts the music into a file."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each bullet, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: the height of the wave is measured / sampled (at regular / set intervals); turned into / stored as binary.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"The height of the (sound) wave is measured / sampled (at regular/set intervals)","marks":1},{"criteria":"(The samples are) turned into / stored as binary","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"sample rate effects","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The sample rate is increased on the computer when recording the guitar."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two effects this will have on the recording."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each bullet, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: the quality will improve; the file size will increase.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"The (sound) quality will improve","marks":1},{"criteria":"The file size will increase","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"binary to denary conversion","marks":1,"command_word":"convert","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert the binary number 11001011 into denary."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only: 203.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct answer: 203","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"binary shift right","marks":1,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete a 2-place shift to the right on the binary number 11001011."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only: 00110010.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct answer: 00110010","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)","parent_question":5,"topic":"binary shift effect","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the effect of performing a 2-place shift to the right on the binary number 11001011."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct point, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: divides the number by 4; loses precision (the two bits shifted off the end are lost).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Divides the number by 4","marks":1},{"criteria":"Loses precision (bits shifted off the end are lost)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"ASCII representation","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table gives the ASCII code for the characters."},{"type":"table","headers":["Character","ASCII code"],"rows":[["L","76"],["M","77"],["N","78"],["O","79"],["P","80"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the word MOP will be represented in ASCII."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: each character from MOP has its ASCII code stored in the order written - 77 79 80 (MOP); ASCII code converted to 8-bit binary number.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Each character from MOP has its ASCII code stored in the order written (e.g. 77, 79, 80)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Each ASCII code is converted to (and stored as) an 8-bit binary number","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(a)","parent_question":7,"topic":"network protocol definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The owners of a large bakery have a Local Area Network (LAN) with a star topology. They order their supplies over the Internet. When data is transmitted from the bakery to the supplier, network protocols are used."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a 'network protocol'."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark only for a correct definition: a set of rules for communication.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A set of rules for communication (between devices on a network)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"protocol layer definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"TCP/IP is a set of protocols based on layers."},{"type":"text","value":"With regards to network protocols, define what is meant by a 'layer'."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a correct definition. Accept: a division of network functionality. Candidate's responses may differ from the given answer but must represent conceptually the same thing - e.g. 'a layer is where jobs / processes are split up' would receive the mark.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A division of network functionality (e.g. where jobs / processes are split up)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"protocol layer advantage","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe one advantage of using layers to construct network protocols."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for the correct identification and 1 mark for a valid description, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: it is self-contained (1) so it allows different developers to concentrate on one aspect of the network (1); a layer can be taken out and edited without affecting other layers (1) so it promotes interoperability between vendors and systems (1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies a valid advantage (e.g. layers are self-contained / a layer can be edited without affecting other layers)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Provides a valid description / consequence (e.g. allows developers to concentrate on one aspect, promotes interoperability between vendors and systems)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(c)","parent_question":7,"topic":"star topology reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why the bakery may use a star network topology for their LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct reason, to a maximum of 2 marks. Accept: it is easy to add a new node or device; fewer data collisions can occur; if a node or device fails it does not affect the rest of the network; a signal does not need to be transmitted to all computers in the network. Any valid comparisons to other topologies can be awarded marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid reason (e.g. easy to add a new node/device, fewer data collisions, node failure does not affect the rest of the network, signal not transmitted to all computers)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid reason (different from the first)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security methods","marks":6,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hospital stores patients' details on its computer network. The hospital is concerned about the security of its patients' details."},{"type":"text","value":"Staff already use strong passwords to protect systems. Explain, with reference to system security, three other ways that the hospital could protect the network system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark to be awarded for each correct type to a maximum of 3 marks (AO2 1a). 1 mark to be awarded for each correct explanation to a maximum of 3 marks (AO2 1b). Accept: Firewall - prevents unauthorised access; Anti-malware - removes viruses / spyware from infecting the system; Encryption - any intercepted data is rendered useless; User access levels - users have restricted access; Network policies - rules that define acceptable use.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First security method identified (e.g. firewall, anti-malware, encryption, user access levels, network policies)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explanation of the first method (e.g. firewall prevents unauthorised access)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second security method identified (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explanation of the second method","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third security method identified (different from the first two)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Explanation of the third method","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(b)","parent_question":8,"topic":"network security errors","marks":6,"command_word":"identify and outline","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify three errors that the hospital staff could make that may endanger the security of the network. Outline a procedure that could be put in place to prevent each error."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark to be awarded for each correct identification of an error to a maximum of 3 marks (AO2 1a). 1 mark to be awarded for each correct outlining of a procedure to a maximum of 3 marks (AO2 1b). Allow any reasonable combination of error and procedure to mitigate the risk. Accept: brings in files via any medium / not allowing or stopping external devices being used on the network; downloading infected files from the internet / blocking or restricting access to insecure websites; allowing physical access to the network / locking of doors, key cards or any physical security procedure; sending or sharing sensitive data with third parties / blocking or restricting access to USB ports, email, internet or printing.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First error identified (e.g. bringing in files via external media, downloading infected files, allowing physical access, sharing sensitive data with third parties)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure that mitigates the first error (e.g. block external devices, restrict insecure websites, lock doors / use key cards, restrict USB / email / printing)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second error identified (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure that mitigates the second error","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third error identified (different from the first two)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure that mitigates the third error","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(a)","parent_question":9,"topic":"disk fragmentation cause","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A restaurant has a computer-based ordering system which is running slowly. A technician has said that the hard disc drive is fragmented. The technician has suggested using utility software to defragment the drive."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain how the restaurant's hard disc could have become fragmented."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Up to a maximum of 4 marks. A maximum of three marks if there is no contextualisation. Allow a mark if candidates state that fragmentation increases access time. Accept: orders have been saved onto the system as they order food and then deleted once processed (1); once other orders have been made, new files are created (1) which may be bigger than the spaces left by the deleted files (1); the order files are split up (1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Order files are saved to the disc as customers order food and deleted once processed","marks":1},{"criteria":"As more orders are made, new files are created (and saved into the available spaces)","marks":1},{"criteria":"New files may be bigger than the spaces left by the deleted files","marks":1},{"criteria":"The (order) files are split up / stored in non-contiguous blocks across the disc","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(b)","parent_question":9,"topic":"defragmentation software","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how defragmentation software could overcome the issue of the slow computer system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Up to a maximum of 3 marks. Accept: files on the hard disk drive are moved (1); empty spaces collected together (1); files are moved to be stored together (1); fewer disc accesses are needed (1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Files on the hard disk drive are moved (rearranged)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Empty spaces are collected together / files belonging to the same file are stored together (contiguously)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fewer disc accesses are needed (so the system runs faster)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(a)","parent_question":10,"topic":"WAN definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A law company currently use a Local Area Network (LAN) linked to a Wide Area Network (WAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"They want to upgrade their system to utilise cloud storage."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a Wide Area Network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark only for a correct definition. Accept: the computers are geographically remote / distanced / more than a mile apart; communication medium is not owned by the law firm (e.g. the company doesn't own the infrastructure). Do not accept 'network over a wide area' or similar arrangement of wording.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Computers are geographically remote/distanced (more than a mile apart) and/or the communication medium/infrastructure is not owned by the organisation","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)","parent_question":10,"topic":"cloud storage advantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two advantages to the law company of storing their data in the Cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct advantage, with a mark for a discussion of the advantage related to the law firm, to a maximum of 2 advantages (4 marks total). Responses which are not contextualised will gain a maximum of 1 mark per advantage. Accept advantages such as: it would offer additional storage (1) so the company can take on more cases (1); it is a very efficient method of backing up data (1) and so saves the firm time and money (1); it would allow their employees to work from anywhere (1) so they can take cases from other countries (1); it is environmentally friendly (1); easy to increase availability of storage (1); you don't need specialist network skills (1) so the firm don't need to employ more staff (1); the third party provides security (1) so the company saves money on staff and software/hardware (1); the third party provides backup (1) so the company saves money on staff and software/hardware (1); cheaper as don't need own infrastructure (1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid advantage of cloud storage identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First advantage contextualised / discussed in relation to the law firm","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid advantage of cloud storage identified (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second advantage contextualised / discussed in relation to the law firm","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(c)","parent_question":10,"topic":"cloud storage disadvantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two disadvantages to the law company of storing their data in the Cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct disadvantage with a mark for a discussion of the disadvantage related to the law firm, to a maximum of 2 disadvantages (4 marks total). Responses which are not contextualised will gain a maximum of 1 mark per disadvantage. Accept: you need a constant internet connection (1) which lawyers who travel a lot may not always have (1); reliant on third party to carry out security procedures (1) but the firm are still legally responsible if things go wrong (1); reliant on third party for back up connection (1); data stored in the Cloud will be vulnerable to hacking and other threats (1) which the firm have no control over (1); issues regarding data ownership (1); implications of Data Protection Act (1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid disadvantage of cloud storage identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First disadvantage contextualised / discussed in relation to the law firm","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid disadvantage of cloud storage identified (different from the first)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second disadvantage contextualised / discussed in relation to the law firm","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(d)","parent_question":10,"topic":"legislation identification","marks":6,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 2 lists some actions that may take place in the law company's office."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show which legislation applies to each action."},{"type":"table","headers":["Action","Data Protection Act 2018","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988"],"rows":[["Using a picture for the law company's new logo without the original creator's permission.","","",""],["A secretary accessing a lawyer's personal email account without permission.","","",""],["Making a copy of the latest Hollywood blockbuster movie and sharing it with a client.","","",""],["Storing customer data insecurely.","","",""],["A lawyer installing a key logger on the secretary's computer.","","",""],["Selling client's personal legal data to a marketing company without their permission.","","",""]]},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 2"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each tick in the correct box, to a maximum of 6 marks. 0 marks for a row with more than one tick. Correct answers: 'Using a picture without permission' = Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988; 'A secretary accessing a lawyer's personal email account without permission' = Computer Misuse Act 1990; 'Making a copy of the latest Hollywood blockbuster movie and sharing it' = Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988; 'Storing customer data insecurely' = Data Protection Act 2018; 'A lawyer installing a key logger on the secretary's computer' = Computer Misuse Act 1990; 'Selling client's personal legal data to a marketing company without their permission' = Data Protection Act 2018.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Using a picture for the law company's new logo without the original creator's permission = Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988","marks":1},{"criteria":"A secretary accessing a lawyer's personal email account without permission = Computer Misuse Act 1990","marks":1},{"criteria":"Making a copy of the latest Hollywood blockbuster movie and sharing it with a client = Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988","marks":1},{"criteria":"Storing customer data insecurely = Data Protection Act 2018","marks":1},{"criteria":"A lawyer installing a key logger on the secretary's computer = Computer Misuse Act 1990","marks":1},{"criteria":"Selling client's personal legal data to a marketing company without their permission = Data Protection Act 2018","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11","parent_question":11,"topic":"smartphone upgrade impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"People often want to buy the most up-to-date smartphones, even though the smartphone they own still works."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of people wanting to upgrade to the latest smartphone."},{"type":"text","value":"In your answer, you might consider the impact on:"},{"type":"text","value":"- smartphone users\\n- cultural issues\\n- ethical issues\\n- environmental issues."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Levels-of-response question marked out of 8. Mark Band 3 - High Level (6-8 marks): the candidate demonstrates a thorough knowledge and understanding of a wide range of considerations in relation to the question; the material is generally accurate and detailed. The candidate is able to apply their knowledge and understanding directly and consistently to the context provided. Evidence/examples will be explicitly relevant to the explanation. The candidate is able to weigh up both sides of the discussion and includes reference to the impact on all areas showing thorough recognition of influencing factors. There is a well-developed line of reasoning which is clear and logically structured; information presented is relevant and substantiated. Mark Band 2 - Mid Level (3-5 marks): reasonable knowledge and understanding of a range of considerations; material is generally accurate but at times underdeveloped; applies knowledge to context with one or two opportunities missed; evidence/examples are mostly implicitly relevant; reasonable attempt to discuss most areas. There is a line of reasoning with some structure. Mark Band 1 - Low Level (1-2 marks): basic knowledge with limited understanding; material is basic with some inaccuracies; limited attempt to apply knowledge to the context; provides little more than unsupported assertion; basic, unstructured communication. 0 marks: no attempt to answer or response is not worthy of credit. Indicative content (not prescriptive or exhaustive): Smartphone users - can adversely affect people in this country and abroad (health issues, financially, socially, culturally). Cultural issues - desire/need to own newest device, to fit in with peers, may have new features required for work/leisure. Environmental issues - the type of devices that are disposed of, modern phones poorly designed for durability, hardware not upgradeable/replaceable, e-waste in landfill, equipment sent abroad, toxic waste released into land/ground water/air, waste of resources, precious metals in phones. Ethical issues - contributes to ill health, digital divide, social divide, problem of confidential data on devices, social pressure on parents/public to upgrade, bullying of those who cannot afford the latest technology, manufacturers intentionally designing fragile phones, high cost of new devices.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Mark Band 1 (1-2 marks): basic knowledge of considerations with limited understanding; material is basic with some inaccuracies; limited attempt to apply knowledge to the context; provides nothing more than unsupported assertion; communicated in an unstructured way","marks":2},{"criteria":"Mark Band 2 (3-5 marks): reasonable knowledge and understanding of a range of considerations; material generally accurate but at times underdeveloped; applies knowledge directly to the context with one or two opportunities missed; evidence/examples mostly implicitly relevant; reasonable attempt to discuss most areas with a line of reasoning that has some structure","marks":3},{"criteria":"Mark Band 3 (6-8 marks): thorough knowledge and understanding of a wide range of considerations; material accurate and detailed; knowledge applied directly and consistently to the context; weighs up both sides and references the impact on all areas (smartphone users, cultural, ethical, environmental); well-developed, logically structured line of reasoning that is relevant and substantiated","marks":3}]}}]`),qP={paper_id:_P,qualification:yP,component:wP,title:bP,year:kP,session:vP,total_marks:xP,questions:CP},SP="J277-02-Specimen",AP="J277",jP="02",OP="Computational Thinking Algorithms And Programming",TP=null,PP="Specimen",JP=80,IP=JSON.parse('[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"truth table NOT AND","marks":2,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the truth table in Fig. 1 for the Boolean statement P = NOT(A AND B)."},{"type":"table","headers":["A","B","P"],"rows":[["0","0","1"],["0","1","____"],["1","0","____"],["1","1","0"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 1"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct missing value in the truth table. We are looking for the two blank entries in row 2 (A=0, B=1) and row 3 (A=1, B=0). Accept \'True\' / \'T\' as alternatives to 1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row A=0, B=1 → P = 1","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row A=1, B=0 → P = 1","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-1-a.png"}},{"question_id":"1(b)","parent_question":1,"topic":"logic diagram identification","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box to identify the correct logic diagram for P = NOT(A AND B)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Logic diagram","Tick one box"],"rows":[[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/a.png","alt_text":"Option a logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/b.png","alt_text":"Option b logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/c.png","alt_text":"Option c logic-gate diagram."},""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only - option b, the NAND gate (an AND gate followed by a NOT gate, i.e. AND with a small circle on the output).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Selects option b, the NAND-gate diagram (AND followed by NOT)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-003.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-1-b.png"}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"pseudocode completion","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n• Input two numbers from the user\\n• Compare both numbers and output the largest number.\\n\\nComplete the pseudocode for this program."},{"type":"code","language":"pseudocode","value":"num1 = input(\\"enter first number\\")\\nnum2 = input(\\"enter second number\\")\\n.......... num1 > .......... then\\n   ..............................\\nelse\\n   ..............................\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow equivalent pseudocode expressions. Variable names must not be inside speech marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First gap completed with `if`","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second gap completed with `num2`","marks":1},{"criteria":"Then-branch outputs `num1` (e.g. `print(num1)`)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Else-branch outputs `num2` (e.g. `print(num2)`)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-004.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-2-a.png"}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"loop algorithm design","marks":5,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A second program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n• Input a number from the user\\n• Double the number input and print the result\\n• Repeat bullets 1 and 2 until the user enters a number less than 0.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm for this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Award 1 mark per bullet to a maximum of 5. Ignore non-initialisation of the value used in the loop condition.\\n\\nExample 1:\\n  store 10 in number\\n  while number is greater than or equal to 0 do the following:\\n    Take input from the user, store in number\\n    Multiply number by 2\\n    Output number\\n\\nExample 2:\\n  while number >= 0\\n    number = input()\\n    output(number * 2)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Uses a condition-controlled loop (while or do/until)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Loop condition checks the number is greater than or equal to 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Inputs a number from the user inside the loop (FT if