n the flowchart in Fig. 2."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/username-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Flowchart that builds a username from the first three letters of firstName and the first two letters of surname."},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 2\\n\\nFor example, using the process in Fig. 2, Tom Ward\'s username would be TomWa.\\n\\nState, using the process in Fig. 2, the username for Rebecca Ellis."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only. Accept any case.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"RebEl","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-021.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-4-a.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"teacher username generation","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The program design is updated to create usernames as follows:\\n• If the person is a teacher, their username is the last 3 letters of their surname and then the first 2 letters of their first name.\\n• If the person is a student, their username is the first 3 letters of their first name and then the first 2 letters of their surname.\\n\\nWhat would be the username for a teacher called Fred Biscuit using the updated process?"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer only. Accept any case.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"uitFr","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-021.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-4-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"username algorithm design","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm for the updated program design shown in question 4(b)(i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct bullet to a maximum of 6.\\n\\nExample:\\nAsk the user to input the data, store in variables firstname, surname and role. Check whether the role entered is teacher. If it is, join the right 3 most letters in surname with the left 2 letters in firstname. Store this in username. If it is not teacher, join the left 3 letters from firstname with the left 2 letters from surname. Store this in username. Output the value in username.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Takes firstname, surname and teacher-or-student as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks IF role is teacher/student using appropriate selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Teacher branch generates last 3 letters of surname using string manipulation","marks":1},{"criteria":"Teacher branch concatenates first 2 letters of first name onto the previous","marks":1},{"criteria":"Student branch correctly generates first 3 letters of first name + first 2 of surname","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct concatenation and output of the username","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-021.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-4-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"translation necessity","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computer game is written in a high-level programming language.\\n\\nState why the computer needs to translate the code before it is executed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Maximum 1 mark.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"To convert it to binary / machine code, because the processor can only understand machine code","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-5-a.png"}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"compiler vs interpreter","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Either a compiler or an interpreter can translate the code.\\n\\nDescribe two differences between how a compiler and an interpreter would translate the code."},{"type":"text","value":"Difference 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Difference 2: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for the correct identification and 1 mark for a valid description, up to a maximum of 4 marks. No more than 2 marks for answers relating only to interpreters and no more than 2 marks for answers relating only to compilers.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compiler translates all the code in one go, whereas an interpreter translates one line at a time","marks":2},{"criteria":"Compiler creates an executable, whereas an interpreter does not / executes one line at a time (or: compiler reports errors at the end, whereas an interpreter stops when it finds an error)","marks":2}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Specimen (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-specimen/dev-qa/q-5-b.png"}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"bubble sort steps","marks":4,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pr