,answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each completed gap: analogue, sampling, sample rate, unique, bit depth, higher.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"analogue",marks:1},{criteria:"sampling",marks:1},{criteria:"sample rate",marks:1},{criteria:"unique",marks:1},{criteria:"bit depth",marks:1},{criteria:"higher",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"2(i)",parent_question:2,topic:"sound sampling definition",marks:1,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"A musician uses a computer to make and record music."},{type:"text",value:"Tick one box to identify the correct description of sound sampling."},{type:"table",headers:["","The frequency of the wave is measured a set number of times each second.","The amplitude of the wave is measured at set intervals.","The digital sound wave is measured a set number of times each second.","The analogue sound wave's resolution is measured at set intervals."],rows:[["Select","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark. Correct answer: The amplitude of the wave is measured at set intervals.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Correct statement identified",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"2(ii)",parent_question:2,topic:"effects of changing bit depth",marks:2,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"Explain how changing the bit depth will affect the sound file."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to a maximum of 2. Changing the bit depth changes the number of bits per sample, changes the file size, and changes the accuracy or quality because a wider or smaller range of amplitudes can be recorded.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"The number of bits per sample or the file size changes",marks:1},{criteria:"The accuracy, quality, or range of amplitudes changes",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"3(i)",parent_question:3,topic:"analogue to digital sound conversion",marks:3,command_word:"describe",content:[{type:"text",value:"A student is creating a range of documents for a school project."},{type:"text",value:"The student records a podcast about computer science."},{type:"text",value:"Describe how an analogue sound wave is converted into digital form."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to a maximum of 3. The sound wave is sampled. Its amplitude or height is measured at regular intervals. Each sample is stored as a binary number, and the binary numbers are stored sequentially.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"The sound wave is sampled",marks:1},{criteria:"The amplitude is measured at set intervals",marks:1},{criteria:"Each sample is stored as a binary number",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"3(ii)",parent_question:3,topic:"effects of duration, sample rate, and bit depth changes",marks:3,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Tick one or more boxes on each row to identify the effect(s) that each change will have on the sound file."},{type:"table",headers:["Change","File size increases","File size decreases","Accuracy increases","Accuracy decreases"],rows:[["Duration changes from 10 minutes to 20 minutes","","","",""],["Sample rate changes from 44 kilohertz to 8 kilohertz","","","",""],["Bit depth changes from 8 bits to 16 bits","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each row. Duration from 10 to 20 minutes increases file size only. Sample rate from 44 kHz to 8 kHz decreases file size and decreases accuracy. Bit depth from 8 bits to 16 bits increases file size and increases accuracy.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Duration increase -> file size increases",marks:1},{criteria:"Sample rate decrease -> file size decreases and accuracy decreases",marks:1},{criteria:"Bit depth increase -> file size increases and accuracy increases",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"4(i)",parent_question:4,topic:"image storage capacity calculation",marks:2,command_word:"calculate",content:[{type:"text",value:"Each image has a fixed size of 1 MB. The storage device has a capacity of 3 GB."},{type:"text",value:"Calculate how many images can be saved on the storage device. Show your working."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the answer 3000 images.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Correct working is shown, such as 1000 x 3",marks:1},{criteria:"Correct answer: 3000 images",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"4(ii)",parent_question:4,topic:"secondary storage type for videos",marks:3,command_word:"identify and justify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Layla uses the images to make videos. These videos are stored on her computer's internal storage device."},{type:"text",value:"Identify the most appropriate type of storage device for Layla to use in her computer. Justify your choice."},{type:"text",value:"Type of storage device: ____"},{type:"text",value:"Justification: ____"}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for a suitable type such as solid state or magnetic. Up to 2 marks for justification. Solid state can be justified by sufficient capacity, durability, or faster access. Magnetic can be justified by high capacity or that moving parts do not matter if the computer is not being moved often.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"A suitable storage type is identified",marks:1},{criteria:"Justification point 1 matches the choice",marks:1},{criteria:"Justification point 2 matches the choice",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"4(iii)",parent_question:4,topic:"true and false statements about sound storage",marks:3,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Tick one box in each row to identify if the statement is true or false."},{type:"table",headers:["Statement","True","False"],rows:[["The sample rate is the number of times the amplitude is recorded per second","",""],["The smaller the bit depth the smaller the range of sounds recorded","",""],["The larger the sample rate the larger the bit depth","",""],["The frequency and pitch of the sound wave are measured","",""],["Sound is stored using pixels","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for 2 correct ticks, 2 marks for 3 or 4 correct ticks, and 3 marks for all correct. Statements 1 and 2 are true. Statements 3, 4, and 5 are false.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"At least 2 statements are correctly identified",marks:1},{criteria:"At least 4 statements are correctly identified",marks:1},{criteria:"All 5 statements are correctly identified",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"5",parent_question:5,topic:"sound sampled into a file",marks:2,command_word:"describe",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer records an audio file of someone playing a guitar."},{type:"text",value:"Describe what happens when the computer converts the music into a file."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to a maximum of 2. The height of the wave is measured or sampled at regular intervals, and the samples are turned into or stored as binary.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"The height of the wave is measured or sampled at regular intervals",marks:1},{criteria:"The samples are turned into or stored as binary",marks:1}]}}],lJ={paper_id:tJ,qualification:aJ,component:rJ,title:nJ,year:iJ,session:sJ,total_marks:oJ,questions:cJ},dJ="J277-TOPIC-1.2.9",uJ="J277",pJ="TOPIC",hJ="Compression",mJ=null,fJ=null,gJ=17,_J=[{question_id:"1(i)",parent_question:1,topic:"text compression method",marks:3,command_word:"identify and justify",content:[{type:"text",value:"A student has a text document and an image file that need to be compressed separately."},{type:"text",value:"The student needs to reduce the file size of both of these files as much as possible."},{type:"text",value:"Identify the most suitable type of compression for the text document. Justify your choice."},{type:"text",value:"Type of compression: ____"},{type:"text",value:"Justification: ____"}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for lossless. Up to 2 marks for justification. Valid points include that lossless removes no data, the file can be reconstructed exactly, and text files need all data to remain accurate and usable.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Correct type identified: lossless",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification about preserving all data",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification linked to text files remaining accurate and usable",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1(ii)",parent_question:1,topic:"image compression method",marks:3,command_word:"identify and justify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Identify the most suitable type of compression for the image file. Justify your choice."},{type:"text",value:"Type of compression: ____"},{type:"text",value:"Justification: ____"}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for lossy. Up to 2 marks for justification. Valid points include that lossy reduces file size more than lossless and that small losses in image quality may not be noticed by the user.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Correct type identified: lossy",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification about a larger reduction in file size",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification about image quality loss being less noticeable",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"2(i)",parent_question:2,topic:"compression benefits",marks:2,command_word:"give",content:[{type:"text",value:"A student compresses all their documents before emailing them to their teacher."},{type:"text",value:"Give two benefits of compressing the data before it is emailed."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to a maximum of 2. Valid benefits include reduced file size, faster transmission or upload/download, less storage space required, less bandwidth used, or less data sent.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"One valid benefit of compression is given",marks:1},{criteria:"A second valid benefit of compression is given",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"2(ii)",parent_question:2,topic:"lossy compression drawbacks",marks:2,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"Explain why lossy compression may not be appropriate to compress all of the student's files."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to a maximum of 2. Valid points include that data is permanently lost or not fully recoverable, text files cannot be compressed with lossy compression, or the teacher may need the original or high-quality image, video, or sound files.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Lossy compression permanently removes data or cannot fully recover the original",marks:1},{criteria:"Text files cannot use lossy compression, or original quality may be required",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"3(i)",parent_question:3,topic:"why compress media before upload",marks:2,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"Layla is an artist. She draws images by hand. The image is then scanned and stored on a computer."},{type:"text",value:"Layla uploads her images and videos to a website."},{type:"text",value:"Explain why Layla compresses the images and videos before uploading them."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to a maximum of 2. Compression reduces the file size, so the files take less space on the server and upload or download faster.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"The file size is reduced or less server space is needed",marks:1},{criteria:"Uploads or downloads are faster",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"3(ii)",parent_question:3,topic:"largest file-size reduction compression method",marks:3,command_word:"identify and justify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Layla wants to reduce the file size of the images and videos by the largest amount possible."},{type:"text",value:"Identify the method of compression that would be most appropriate. Justify your choice."},{type:"text",value:"Compression method: ____"},{type:"text",value:"Justification: ____"}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for lossy. Up to 2 marks for justification. Valid points include that lossy usually reduces file size by a larger amount than lossless and removes data that is less noticeable to the user.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Correct method identified: lossy",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification about a larger file-size reduction",marks:1},{criteria:"A valid justification about removing less noticeable data",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"4",parent_question:4,topic:"effects of increased sample rate",marks:2,command_word:"give",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer records an audio file of someone playing a guitar."},{type:"text",value:"The sample rate is increased on the computer when recording the guitar."},{type:"text",value:"Give two effects this will have on the recording."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each. Increasing the sample rate increases sound quality and increases file size.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Sound quality improves",marks:1},{criteria:"File size increases",marks:1}]}}],yJ={paper_id:dJ,qualification:uJ,component:pJ,title:hJ,year:mJ,session:fJ,total_marks:gJ,questions:_J},wJ="J277-TOPIC-1.3.1",bJ="J277",kJ="TOPIC",vJ="Networks and Topologies",xJ=null,CJ=null,qJ=77,SJ=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"LAN vs WAN","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A youth centre is creating a network with 20 computers in a single building that will be connected in a Local Area Network (LAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify two reasons why this network will be a LAN and not a Wide Area Network (WAN)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2 Small geographical location // single building No use of external infrastructure // All hardware will be owned/managed by youth centre Dedicated hardware // The hardware is not shared/used by anyone else/any other business It is not connecting networks (like a WAN does) Mark any two correct points as long as not contradicted.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid LAN-distinguishing reason from: small geographical location / single building; no use of external infrastructure (hardware owned/managed by the youth centre); dedicated hardware not shared with other businesses; it does not connect networks (as a WAN does).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second valid LAN-distinguishing reason from the same list (must be a distinct, non-contradictory point).","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"mesh topology definition","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe what is meant by a mesh topology."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Valid points: (Partial mesh) all devices are connected to one or more devices // (Full mesh) all devices are connected to every other device; No need for central management / switch / device / node // all devices are equal // decentralised; Multiple routes/paths (between devices). MP1 BOD each device is connected to other devices // all devices are connected. MP2 do not accept 'no central server'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid mesh-topology characteristic from: (partial mesh) all devices connected to one or more devices, OR (full mesh) all devices connected to every other device (BOD: each device is connected to other devices / all devices are connected); no need for central management/switch/device/node, all devices are equal, decentralised; multiple routes/paths between devices.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second valid mesh-topology characteristic from the same list. Do NOT accept 'no central server' for the decentralised point.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"mesh vs star topology","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe one benefit and one drawback of the network using a mesh topology instead of a star topology."},{"type":"text","value":"Benefit Drawback"}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2 for drawback max 2 for benefit e.g. Benefits: No purchase/cost of central switch / device … lower cost to install/maintain network // no need to setup / connect to the central device More connections/paths between devices // alternate routes … no single point of failure // if a connection breaks can still connect … more robust structure More scalable // can have more devices/connections/nodes/computers … easier to add devices … can add / remove nodes without impacting other nodes/speed Decrease in latency … fewer collisions Can be setup over a larger area // can provide a more stable connection … each device acts as repeater Drawbacks: Large number of / more connections/wires … can involve redundant connections … this is impractical Reduced central management/control/oversight of the network/transmission … less control over transfer/security of the network Less secure BOD example less secure … more routes/points/devices for data interception/attack/unauthorised access Difficult to find an error … because of the multiple routes where an error could occur Mesh network uses more power … more nodes are involved in each transmission for a point 1 for expansion/reason/benefit. Any of the MPs can be the benefit/drawback and can be the expansion as long as they follow on. Do not accept faster/slower transmission speeds – dependent on media and many other factors. MP3 and MP4 can be in a single statement e.g. ‘If one connection breaks the nodes can still connect through another route’ Accept: faster/slower transmission speeds – dependent on media and many other factors. MP3 and MP4 can be in a single statement e.g. ‘If one connection breaks the nodes can still connect through another route’","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a valid benefit of mesh over star from: no purchase/cost of central switch/device; more connections/paths between devices (alternate routes / no single point of failure / more robust); more scalable (easier to add devices, can add/remove without impacting others); decrease in latency / fewer collisions; can be set up over a larger area / more stable connection (each device acts as a repeater).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a matched expansion or second valid benefit (e.g. 'lower cost to install/maintain' expanding the no-switch point; 'if a connection breaks the nodes can still connect' expanding the alternate-routes point). MP3 and MP4 may be in a single statement.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a valid drawback of mesh from: large number of/more connections/wires (can involve redundant connections / impractical); reduced central management/control/oversight (less control over transfer/security); less secure (more routes/devices for interception/attack); difficult to find an error (multiple routes); mesh uses more power (more nodes per transmission).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a matched expansion or second valid drawback. Do NOT accept faster/slower transmission speeds (these depend on media and other factors).","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(c)","parent_question":1,"topic":"cloud storage drawbacks","marks":5,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The youth centre is going to store data about users and events using cloud storage."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the drawbacks to the youth centre of storing the data on the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 5 e.g. Can be expensive for large amounts of data / long term / hosting // may be ongoing subscription // no control over changing costs May be limited amount of storage No control over security // Need to rely on provider for security May be hacked/stolen/intercepted/affected by a virus May lose (personal) data // May lose access to data May go against DPA/legislation // Data may be misused (by provider) May not be clear where in the world the data is stored Limited/No control over access Company/cloud could go down No control over backup // May not have (automatic) backups … … data is not retrievable Need internet access // no internet access means no data … access speed depends on network/connection speed Concerns over ownership of the data Need to remember login details // may forget/lose login details Users may need training MP1 needs more than high cost, it needs identification of ongoing, cost for hosting etc. MP3 just poor/low security is NE Accept: data may be sold as example of misuse.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid cloud-storage drawback from: ongoing/expensive for large amounts/long-term/hosting (cost identification beyond just 'high cost'); limited amount of storage; no control over security / reliance on provider for security ('poor/low security' alone is NE); may be hacked/stolen/intercepted/affected by virus; may lose (personal) data or access to it; may go against DPA/legislation, data may be misused (accept: data may be sold); may not be clear where data is stored geographically; limited/no control over access; company/cloud could go down; no control over backup, may not have automatic backups, data not retrievable; requires internet access (access speed depends on connection); concerns over data ownership; need to remember login details; users may need training.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a fourth distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a fifth distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(d)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"internet connection device","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one device that will be required to allow the LAN to connect to the internet."}],"answer":{"guidance":"for router/modem","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"for router/modem","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(d)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"IPv4 address validation","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The computers will be allocated an IPv4 address."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box on each row to identify if the IPv4 address is valid or invalid."},{"type":"table","headers":["IPv4 address","Valid (✓)","Invalid (✓)"],"rows":[["192.154.21.2","",""],["258.0.0.3","",""],["56.1.2.66.1","",""],["251.58.3.7","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"for each row. IPv4 address Valid (✓) Invalid (✓) 192.154.21. ✓ 258.0.0.3 ✓ 56.1.2.66.1 ✓ 251.58.3.7 ✓ Do not award rows with 2 ticks. Allow Xs in place of ticks in either/both columns.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (192.154.21.2): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Allow X in place of tick.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (258.0.0.3): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (first octet 258 is greater than 255).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (56.1.2.66.1): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (5 octets — IPv4 must have exactly 4).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (251.58.3.7): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Do not award rows with 2 ticks.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(d)(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"DNS resolution steps","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"When a user wants to access a website they enter the URL into the address bar of a web browser."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps that take place to convert the URL into the website’s IP address."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 4 Browser checks cache for matching IP address URL/domain is sent to DNS DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP …in table/database/files/lists (of URL/domain and IPs) If not found the request is transferred to higher-level DNS DNS returns IP address to computer/browser An error is returned/sent if the IP address cannot be found MP3 – award for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP MP5 accept another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own. Accept: another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for any one of: browser checks cache for matching IP address; URL/domain is sent to DNS.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP (in a table/database/files/lists). MP3 awarded for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: if not found, the request is transferred to a higher-level DNS (accept: another/larger DNS).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS returns IP address to computer/browser (do not award 'user' on its own); OR an error is returned/sent if the IP cannot be found.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"star topology diagram","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Draw a diagram to show how the five computers, switch and two printers are connected in a star topology. You must include labels."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for drawing showing: (1) 5 computers, 2 printers and 1 switch all clearly labelled; (2) all devices directly connected to the switch (or all computers to switch and each printer to a switch/computer); (3) only 8 devices and no additional connections other than to the switch. Allow any type of computer e.g. PC, laptop. Do not accept 'client' for computer. MP1 requires at least 5 computers, at least 2 printers, and at least 1 switch.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: 5 computers, 2 printers, and 1 switch all clearly labelled. Allow any computer type (PC, laptop). Do NOT accept 'client' for computer. There must be at least 5 computers, at least 2 printers, and at least 1 switch.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: all devices directly connected to the switch (or all computers connected to the switch and each printer to a switch/computer).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: only 8 devices and no additional connections other than to the switch (or central device, or printers connected to only one computer each).","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"star vs mesh topology","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one benefit and one drawback of the office using a star topology instead of a mesh topology."},{"type":"text","value":"Benefit Drawback"}],"answer":{"guidance":"for benefit e.g. Easier to add new nodes // easier to setup BOD Central device can monitor/control transmissions Faster data transmission Fewer data collisions One connection/computer breaks the network still works Less cost of cables for drawback e.g. Switch fails the network fails // reliant on a central device (working) // single point of failure Extra cost of central device/switch Speed, cheaper etc. on its own is NE Server is irrelevant. Read whole benefit and award a valid benefit. Read whole drawback and award a valid drawback. Do not award contradictory statements.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid benefit of star over mesh from: easier to add new nodes / easier to setup (BOD); central device can monitor/control transmissions; faster data transmission; fewer data collisions; if one connection/computer breaks the network still works; less cost of cables. ('Speed', 'cheaper' on their own are NE.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid drawback of star vs mesh from: switch/central-device failure brings the network down (single point of failure); extra cost of central device/switch. Do not award contradictory statements.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(iii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"switch role in network","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the role of the switch in the star topology."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 3 e.g. Connects the devices together in the network // allows devices to communicate in the network Receives data from (all) devices in the star topology Record/register/store the address of devices connected to it … … in a table Uses MAC address of devices Direct data to destination … if address not recorded transmit to all devices","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for any one of: connects the devices together in the network / allows devices to communicate; receives data from (all) devices in the star topology.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: records/registers/stores the addresses of devices connected to it (in a table); uses MAC addresses of devices.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: directs data to the destination (and if the address is not recorded, transmits to all devices).","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"client-server client","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the computer that is acting as the client in this scenario and justify your choice."},{"type":"text","value":"Client computer Justification"}],"answer":{"guidance":"for identification: Artist's computer // computer uploading the images // BOD The artist each for justification to max 2: e.g. Sends the files/data for storage/to the host/web server // the files are stored on the web server Performs the user's actions ….. and sends the results to the web server Sends a request to the web server… … to store/upload its files It does not store data for others to access Confirmation of upload/error is received (from server) for display Incorrect computer, do not award justification. Be careful the justification is talking about the upload of images to the web server, not the download. Accept: host for web server. If 'user's computer' is given for identification, this is NE – read on for justification. If 'user viewing the website' or similar is given this is incorrect.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for identification: artist's computer / computer uploading the images (BOD: 'the artist'). 'User's computer' is NE; 'user viewing the website' is incorrect — read on for justification but do not award if computer is incorrect.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid justification from: sends the files/data for storage to the host/web server (files are stored on the web server); performs the user's actions and sends results to the web server; sends a request to the web server to store/upload its files; does not store data for others to access; receives confirmation of upload/error from server for display. (Accept 'host' for web server.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct justification from the same list. The justification must concern UPLOAD of images, not download.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"client-server server","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the computer that is acting as the server in this scenario and justify your choice Server computer Justification"}],"answer":{"guidance":"for identification: Web server each for justification to max 2: e.g. The images/data are stored on / uploaded to / sent to / hosted on the web server Web server receives a request (from the artist's computer to upload the images) Web server executes/responds to the request // Web server is doing the processing/handling the (request to) upload Web server returns confirmation/error of the processing/upload If computer is incomplete or inaccurate e.g. server/website instead of web server. Do not award computer, but award justification. Allow FT in justification if the same inaccurate term is used, for example 'website' is given as computer (NE), but justification is: 'images are sent to the website' (FT for website instead of web server). Incorrect computer, do not award justification.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for identification: web server. If incomplete/inaccurate (e.g. 'server' or 'website' instead of 'web server'), do not award the identification mark — but allow follow-through on justification if the same inaccurate term is used consistently.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid justification from: images/data are stored on / uploaded to / sent to / hosted on the web server; web server receives a request (from the artist's computer to upload the images); web server executes/responds to the request (does the processing/handling of the upload); web server returns confirmation/error of the processing/upload.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct justification from the same list. Incorrect computer = do not award justification.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network performance factors","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A library has a LAN (Local Area Network)."},{"type":"text","value":"A LAN allows access by both wired and wireless devices."},{"type":"text","value":"Users have reported that the network sometimes runs very slowly."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the number of devices using the network at the same time can affect the performance of the network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 3. More devices mean more data is being transmitted at a time. Bandwidth will be split between all the devices. This means less bandwidth for each device. Devices have to wait longer before they can transmit (increased latency). Slower transmission of data / less data can be transmitted at the same time. Central device has to handle more requests. More collisions likely, more data has to be retransmitted.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"More devices = more data being transmitted","marks":1},{"criteria":"Bandwidth split between devices / less bandwidth per device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Slower transmission / increased latency / devices wait longer / more collisions","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network performance factor","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one other factor that can affect the performance of the network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. E.g. Bandwidth, interference, wired/wireless/transmission medium, type/amount of data being transmitted, central hardware performance (e.g. router/switch), error rate, distance between nodes, topology. Do not award number of users.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid factor identified (e.g. bandwidth, interference, transmission medium, hardware performance, distance, topology)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"accessing websites terms","marks":7,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Users can access websites from library computers."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the description of accessing websites using the given list of terms. Not all terms will be used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: 0, 1, 127, 128, 255, 256, Colon, Domain Name Server, Embedded systems, File server, Full stop, Hyphen, Internet protocol, MAC address, Router, Uniform Resource Locator, Web server, Clients"},{"type":"text","value":"A website is hosted on a ____. The computers that access the websites are called ____."},{"type":"text","value":"The user enters the ____ into a web browser. The web browser sends a request to the ____ for the matching IP (Internet Protocol) address. If found the IP address is returned. A request is then sent to this IP address."},{"type":"text","value":"An IPv4 address is made of 4 groups of digits. Each group can be between the denary values ____ and ____. The groups of digits are separated by a ____."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed term. Web server. Clients. Uniform Resource Locator. Domain Name Server. 0. 255 (accept in either order). Full stop.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Web server","marks":1},{"criteria":"Clients","marks":1},{"criteria":"Uniform Resource Locator","marks":1},{"criteria":"Domain Name Server","marks":1},{"criteria":"0 (lower bound of IPv4 group)","marks":1},{"criteria":"255 (upper bound of IPv4 group)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Full stop (separator)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"router tasks","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A network has several routers."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify three tasks carried out by a router."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 3. Receive packets. Forward/send/transmit packets. Maintain a routing table. Identify the most efficient path to the destination. Assign IP addresses to nodes/devices. Convert packets from one protocol to another. Note: question asks for tasks, not the purpose/use of a router.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Task 1 (e.g. receive packets, forward packets, maintain routing table, assign IP addresses)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Task 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Task 3","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"network type identification","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir has a home network that includes two laptop computers, four mobile phones, and two televisions."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the type of network Amir has at home."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: LAN / Local Area Network.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"LAN / Local Area Network identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"switch vs router","marks":6,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The network uses a star topology with a central switch. The switch has an integrated wireless access point (WAP)."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the similarities and differences between a switch and a router."},{"type":"table","headers":["Similarities","Differences"],"rows":[["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per valid point to max 6. Similarities include that both connect devices, receive data, determine the destination, and transmit data. Differences include that a switch uses MAC addresses while a router uses IP addresses, a switch connects nodes/devices on a LAN, and a router connects networks/the internet.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid similarity 1 (e.g. both connect devices / both receive data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid similarity 2 (e.g. both determine destination / both transmit data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 1 (switch uses MAC addresses, router uses IP addresses)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 2 (switch connects nodes/devices, router connects networks/the internet)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid difference 3","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid additional comparison point","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"star topology diagram","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Draw the star topology for Amir's home network. Clearly label each device."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet. Clearly labelled switch. 2 laptops, 4 phones, and 2 TVs. All devices connected to the switch and nothing else. Connections can be wired or identifiable wireless connections.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Switch clearly labelled","marks":1},{"criteria":"2 laptops, 4 phones, and 2 TVs shown","marks":1},{"criteria":"All devices connected only to the switch","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(i)","parent_question":6,"topic":"cloud storage benefits","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve stores her computer program on the cloud whilst working on it."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits to Eve of storing the program on the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per point to max 3. She can access the program from anywhere. She does not need to carry a storage device. Security / backup is likely managed for her, so she does not need to back up the work manually.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Can access the program from anywhere","marks":1},{"criteria":"Does not need to carry a storage device","marks":1},{"criteria":"Security / backup managed for her","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(ii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"cloud storage drawbacks","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve stores her computer program on the cloud whilst working on it."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the drawbacks to Eve of storing the program on the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per point to max 3. If there is no internet access she cannot access her work. Transmission may not be secure, so the work could be intercepted. Security is out of her control, so it may not be backed up or kept safe.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"No internet access means she cannot access the work","marks":1},{"criteria":"Transmission may not be secure / work could be intercepted","marks":1},{"criteria":"Security / backup is out of her control","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_question":7,"topic":"star topology reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why the bakery may use a star network topology for their LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid reason to max 2. It is easy to add a new node or device. Fewer data collisions can occur. If one node or device fails it does not affect the rest of the network. A signal does not need to be transmitted to all computers in the network.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid reason 1 (e.g. easy to add devices / fewer collisions / one failure does not affect others)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Valid reason 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(a)","parent_question":8,"topic":"WAN definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A law company currently use a Local Area Network (LAN) linked to a Wide Area Network (WAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"They want to upgrade their system to utilise cloud storage."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a Wide Area Network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a correct definition, such as computers being geographically remote / separated over a large area, or communication media that is not owned by the law company.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Computers are geographically remote / over a large area, or the communication medium is not owned by the company","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(b)","parent_question":8,"topic":"cloud storage advantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two advantages to the law company of storing their data in the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Two contextualised advantages, worth up to 2 marks each. Advantages include extra storage so the company can take on more cases, efficient backup saving time and money, remote access for employees, reduced need for specialist network skills, third-party security/backup, or lower infrastructure costs. Each advantage must be linked to the law company for full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Advantage 1 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 1 contextualised to the law company","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Advantage 2 contextualised to the law company","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8(c)","parent_question":8,"topic":"cloud storage disadvantages","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain two disadvantages to the law company of storing their data in the cloud."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Two contextualised disadvantages, worth up to 2 marks each. Disadvantages include needing a constant internet connection, reliance on a third party for security or backup, data being vulnerable to hacking, uncertainty over data ownership, and data protection implications. Each disadvantage must be linked to the law company for full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Disadvantage 1 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 1 contextualised to the law company","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Disadvantage 2 contextualised to the law company","marks":1}]}}]`),AJ={paper_id:wJ,qualification:bJ,component:kJ,title:vJ,year:xJ,session:CJ,total_marks:qJ,questions:SJ},jJ="J277-TOPIC-1.3.2",OJ="J277",TJ="TOPIC",PJ="Wired and Wireless Networks, Protocols and Layers",JJ=null,IJ=null,EJ=50,RJ=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(a)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"Wi-Fi connection benefits","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the benefits to the youth centre of also providing Wi-Fi connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 3 e.g. Easier to add more devices/users Can have more devices/connections/users // more scalable … without cost of purchase/install additional wired connections Devices are not fixed to one spot // devices can be moved // can connect from anywhere in (wireless) range …allowing people to use them where they are needed // can use where there is no physical connection Reduces risk of (additional) trailing wires Less potential damage to equipment/wires Connect wider range / more types of device // by example e.g. mobile phones/tablets … that don’t have ports / wired connections // that only have wireless connections It allows a backup network if the wired network fails (Due to each node acting as a repeater in the mesh) the network will have a greater range MP1 is for easier to add more devices to the network. MP2 is there can be more connections/nodes/devices than on a wired. ‘more devices can be added easily’ is MP1 on its own.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid wireless benefit from: easier to add more devices/users (more devices/connections, more scalable, no cost of installing additional wired connections); devices not fixed to one spot (can be moved, used anywhere in wireless range, used where there is no physical connection); reduces risk of trailing wires, less potential damage to equipment/wires; connects wider range/more types of device (e.g. mobile phones/tablets without ports); allows a backup network if wired network fails; greater range (each node acts as a repeater in a mesh).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct benefit from the same list. ('More devices can be added easily' covers MP1 alone; MP2 is the separate point about more devices/connections than wired.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(a)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"network performance factors","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The decision to use wired and/or wireless connections can affect the performance of the network."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by explaining how each factor can affect the performance of a network."},{"type":"table","headers":["Factor","How it affects performance"],"rows":[["Bandwidth",""],["Number of users accessing the network at the same time",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"per bullet to max 2 for each factor Factor How it affects the performance Bandwidth More bandwidth = faster network/transmission BOD better performance …because more data is transferred simultaneously/per second Number of users accessing the network at the same time More users = slower network/transmission BOD worse performance …because more data is transferred simultaneously // more traffic // the bandwidth is being shared/used // more bandwidth used // BOD more congestion / collisions Allow reverse of each. Do not award references to Internet speed alone. Points need to reference more/less data in correct context.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Bandwidth: Award 1 mark for: more bandwidth = faster network/transmission (BOD better performance). Allow reverse. Do not award references to internet speed alone.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Bandwidth: Award 1 mark for the linked reason: ...because more data is transferred simultaneously / per second.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Number of users: Award 1 mark for: more users = slower network/transmission (BOD worse performance). Allow reverse.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Number of users: Award 1 mark for the linked reason: ...because more data is transferred simultaneously / more traffic / bandwidth is shared / more congestion / more collisions.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"internet connection device","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one device that will be required to allow the LAN to connect to the internet."}],"answer":{"guidance":"for router/modem","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"for router/modem","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"IPv4 address validation","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The computers will be allocated an IPv4 address."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box on each row to identify if the IPv4 address is valid or invalid."},{"type":"table","headers":["IPv4 address","Valid (✓)","Invalid (✓)"],"rows":[["192.154.21.2","",""],["258.0.0.3","",""],["56.1.2.66.1","",""],["251.58.3.7","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"for each row. IPv4 address Valid (✓) Invalid (✓) 192.154.21.2 ✓ 258.0.0.3 ✓ 56.1.2.66.1 ✓ 251.58.3.7 ✓ Do not award rows with 2 ticks. Allow Xs in place of ticks in either/both columns.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (192.154.21.2): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Allow X in place of tick.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (258.0.0.3): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (first octet 258 is greater than 255).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (56.1.2.66.1): Award 1 mark for ticking INVALID (5 octets — IPv4 must have exactly 4).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (251.58.3.7): Award 1 mark for ticking VALID. Do not award rows with 2 ticks.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(b)(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"DNS resolution steps","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"When a user wants to access a website they enter the URL into the address bar of a web browser."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps that take place to convert the URL into the website’s IP address."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 4 Browser checks cache for matching IP address URL/domain is sent to DNS DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP …in table/database/files/lists (of URL/domain and IPs) If not found the request is transferred to higher-level DNS DNS returns IP address to computer/browser An error is returned/sent if the IP address cannot be found MP3 – award for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP MP5 accept another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own. Accept: another/larger for higher-level MP6 needs to be computer/browser, do not award user on its own.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for any one of: browser checks cache for matching IP address; URL/domain is sent to DNS.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS looks for URL/domain to find matching IP (in a table/database/files/lists). MP3 awarded for the use of the DNS converting URL to IP.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: if not found, the request is transferred to a higher-level DNS (accept: another/larger DNS).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for: DNS returns IP address to computer/browser (do not award 'user' on its own); OR an error is returned/sent if the IP cannot be found.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"IP address examples","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one valid example of an IPv4 address and one valid example of an IPv6 address."},{"type":"text","value":"IPv4 IPv6"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid IP. v4: 4 groups of denary numbers between 0 and 255 separated by full stops (example v4: 123.16.46.72). v6: 8 groups of hex numbers between 0 and FFFF separated by colons. Double colon can appear once and replaces any number of groups of consecutive 0000 (example v6: 0252:5985:89ab:cdde:a57f:89ad:efcd:00fe) (example v6: F513:8C:2A::999:0000 expanded would be F513:8C:2A:0000:0000:0000:999:0000). Each hex number can be between 1 and 4 digits.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a valid IPv4 example: 4 groups of denary numbers between 0 and 255 separated by full stops (e.g. 123.16.46.72).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a valid IPv6 example: 8 groups of hex numbers between 0 and FFFF separated by colons; double colon may appear once and replaces any number of consecutive 0000 groups (e.g. 0252:5985:89ab:cdde:a57f:89ad:efcd:00fe; or F513:8C:2A::999:0000 expanded as F513:8C:2A:0000:0000:0000:999:0000). Each hex group can be 1-4 digits.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"MAC address format","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the format of a MAC address."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2 (usually presented in) hexadecimal / denary / binary of numbers // 12 (hex) numbers … each group has paired/2-digit (hex) numbers / 8 bit binary number long Separated by colons/hyphens (The first half/part) contains the manufacturer ID // (first half/part) identifies the manufacturer (The second half/part) contains the serial number // (second half/part) identifies the device MP1 'numbers' is NE Allow both marks for a valid example. NB '6 pairs of numbers' gets MP2 and MP3. '4 pairs of numbers' gets MP3","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid MAC-address characteristic from: (usually presented in) hexadecimal/denary/binary; 6 groups of numbers / 12 hex digits (each group is paired/2-digit hex / 8-bit binary); 48 bits long; separated by colons/hyphens; first half/part contains/identifies the manufacturer ID; second half/part contains/identifies the serial number/device. ('Numbers' alone for MP1 is NE.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct characteristic from the same list. Both marks may be awarded for a valid worked example. NB '6 pairs of numbers' awards MP2 and MP3; '4 pairs of numbers' awards MP3 only.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"wired network benefits","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two benefits to the airport of using wired connections in their network."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each for benefit 1 for application to max 4 e.g. Fast connection/speed // high bandwidth // consistent bandwidth … e.g. reduce delays at check in // by example for airport Secure // unlikely to have unauthorised access/hacked // data transmissions are likely to be safe … e.g. so that data about passengers/staff/aeroplanes is not intercepted // by example for airport Little interference // little chance of data loss // reliable … e.g. flight status is received without delay // by example for airport Long range transmission … e.g. airport has a large floor area/terminals // by example for airport Mark in pairs. Mark each benefit space to the candidates' benefit. An expansion/application for a benefit can be awarded in the other answer space. and 1 expansion for each answer space. Max 2 marks per answer space. Max 3 marks if expansions have no direct application to the airport and its computers connecting using wired connections. If the second expansion is not applied, annotate with ^ NOT cost The question is not a comparison to wireless, but accept answers worded in this way. Fast on its own is NE. 'faster to connect' is NE because this couldbe to set up the connection as opposed to the bandwidth. Accept: answers worded in this way. Fast on its own is NE. 'faster to connect' is NE because this couldbe to set up the connection as opposed to the bandwidth.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Answer space 1 - benefit: Award 1 mark for one valid benefit of wired connections from: fast connection/speed / high or consistent bandwidth; secure / unlikely to be hacked / data transmissions safe; little interference / little chance of data loss / reliable; long-range transmission. ('Fast' alone is NE; 'faster to connect' is NE - could refer to setup time.) NOT cost.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Answer space 1 - application: Award 1 mark for an expansion/application contextualised to the airport (e.g. 'reduce delays at check-in', 'so data about passengers/staff/aeroplanes is not intercepted', 'flight status received without delay', 'airport has a large floor area/terminals'). If expansion is not applied to the airport, annotate ^ (counts toward the max-3 cap).","marks":1},{"criteria":"Answer space 2 - benefit: Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid benefit from the same list as space 1.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Answer space 2 - application: Award 1 mark for a matching expansion/application contextualised to the airport. Max 2 marks per answer space; max 3 marks total if expansions have no direct airport application.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"wireless network reasons","marks":3,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the reasons why the airport should also allow the network to be accessed using a wireless connection."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 3 e.g. Staff do not need to be in one-place // movement of staff // can work whilst moving to another part of the airport // can be accessed from any location (in range) Staff can be more responsive to customers/requests Allows a larger number of connections/devices // more scalable … … without the disruption/cost of installing more cables Some devices do not allow physical/wired connection // allow wider range of type of device (or by example such as vehicles/mobile devices/aeroplanes) Easier to add/connect more devices Do not need to find/use a physical connection/wire // can allow you to connect in a place where there isn't a cable/connection For use as a backup if the wired connection fails Do not award cost on its own. Do not award range on its own. Allow explanation of how a wireless network will benefit the passenger as well as the airport and staff. Allow in reverse if clear, for example wired restricts staff to one location.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid reason from: staff not fixed to one place / movement of staff / can work whilst moving / can be accessed from any location in range; staff can be more responsive to customers/requests; allows a larger number of connections/devices / more scalable (without disruption/cost of installing more cables); some devices do not allow physical/wired connection / wider range of device types (e.g. vehicles, mobile devices, aeroplanes); easier to add/connect more devices; do not need to find/use a physical connection / can connect where there is no cable; for use as a backup if wired connection fails. Do not award 'cost' or 'range' on their own.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid reason from the same list. Allow explanations of how wireless benefits passengers as well as airport/staff. Allow reverse if clear (e.g. 'wired restricts staff to one location').","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct valid reason from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"network protocols","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by identifying the most appropriate protocol for each of the tasks the student is performing."},{"type":"table","headers":["Task","Protocol"],"rows":[["Requesting to view a news webpage from a web server",""],["Entering a username and password to access their bank\\naccount",""],["Downloading a text document from a web server",""],["Checking for new emails in their inbox",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"for each protocol Task Protocol Requesting a webpage from a web server HTTP // HTTPS Entering a username and password to access their bank account HTTPS Downloading a text document from a web server FTP // HTTP // HTTPS Checking for new emails in their inbox IMAP // POP Mark first answer in each box. Allow full name to be written e.g. file transfer (protocol). Accept: POP3 or any other version","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (requesting a webpage from a web server): Award 1 mark for HTTP or HTTPS. Mark first answer in the box. Allow full name (e.g. 'hypertext transfer protocol').","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (entering a username and password to access bank account): Award 1 mark for HTTPS only.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (downloading a text document from a web server): Award 1 mark for FTP, HTTP, or HTTPS. Allow 'file transfer protocol'.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (checking for new emails in inbox): Award 1 mark for IMAP or POP. Accept POP3 or any other version.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(a)(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"protocol layers","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Some protocols have layers."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why protocols have layers."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2: Each layer is independent // layers are not reliant on other layers One layer can be changed without affecting the others // a layer can function without needing/changing/impacting any other layer // self-contained Separates tasks so they can be developed independently A developer can focus on only one layer // developer can specialise Allows for standards for individual tasks/layers to be developed // for compatibility Manufacturers can develop hardware to fit into one particular layer To group together similar protocols Max 1 in each answer space","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid reason from: each layer is independent / not reliant on others; one layer can be changed without affecting others (self-contained); separates tasks so they can be developed independently; a developer can focus on/specialise in one layer; allows standards for individual layers (compatibility); manufacturers can develop hardware to fit one layer; groups together similar protocols. Max 1 mark in each answer space.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct valid reason from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"LAN characteristics","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"One characteristic of a LAN is that they are set up over a small geographical area."},{"type":"text","value":"Give one other characteristic of a LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"wireless LAN benefits","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits of the student changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 4: e.g. Allows more devices to connect … …for example televisions, mobile phones Easy to connect (devices) // Easier to setup (wireless connections) // By example e.g. easier for guests to connect their devices Home is likely small area … so short distance wireless is sufficient Devices can move around // can use devices in different areas // can connect from anywhere in the house // can use where wires don't reach // can access from a larger area (than wired) … by example e.g. student is using a laptop so does not need to be tied to one place // by example e.g. they don't have to disconnect before moving // e.g. they can stay connected whilst moving Cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices // no cost for (new/replacement) wires/hardware …because no additional/fewer wires are needed Fewer trip hazards from trailing wires // reduce risk of damage to cables // fewer cables to damage More compatible // some devices only have wireless connections Easier/cheaper on their own is NE","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid benefit from: allows more devices to connect (e.g. televisions, mobile phones); easy to connect/setup wireless devices (e.g. easier for guests); home is likely a small area, so short-distance wireless is sufficient; devices can move around / connect from anywhere in the house / used where wires don't reach; cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices (no cost for replacement wires); fewer trip hazards / less cable damage; more compatible - some devices only have wireless connections. ('Easier'/'cheaper' alone are NE.)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a third distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a fourth distinct benefit from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(b)(iii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"wireless LAN drawbacks","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State two drawbacks of changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"each to max 2: e.g. Prone to interference // by example Limited range of signal Slower rate of transmission // less bandwidth // reduced network performance// increased latency // BOD slower connection // more users reduces rate of transmission / bandwidth /performance etc. Increased risk of security concerns // by example e.g. A hacker could connect to the wireless connection Less stable connection Higher chance of collisions // Higher error rate MP3 needs to say what is slower / decreased e.g. It's slower, is NE Mark first drawback in each answer space. Less reliable is TV on its own for MP 5","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for one valid drawback from: prone to interference (or by example); limited range of signal; slower rate of transmission / less bandwidth / reduced network performance / increased latency (must specify what is slower - 'it's slower' alone is NE); increased risk of security concerns (e.g. hacker could connect to wireless); less stable connection; higher chance of collisions / higher error rate. Mark first drawback in each answer space. 'Less reliable' on its own is TV.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Award 1 mark for a second distinct drawback from the same list.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"Ethernet as a standard","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Ethernet is considered a standard."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why Ethernet is a standard."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Ethernet is used by (mostly) all manufacturers / used in many devices. To allow compatibility with other devices. Has high bandwidth. Has inbuilt security. Is a proven/reliable connection. Is low cost for purchase/installation/maintenance.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ethernet is widely used / used by most manufacturers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Allows compatibility between devices, OR has specific benefits (high bandwidth, reliable, low cost)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(b)","parent_question":4,"topic":"encryption reasons","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A library does not use encryption when data is transmitted through its network."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two reasons why the library should use encryption."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Data cannot be understood if intercepted / data will be meaningless. Only authorised users can access confidential material / protects personal/user/library data. To follow legislation/DPA. Note: question is about transmission, not storage.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reason 1 (e.g. data meaningless if intercepted, protects confidential data, follows legislation)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Reason 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"network protocols","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Protocols are used to transmit data through the network and over the internet."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one protocol that can be used to perform each of the following tasks:"},{"type":"text","value":"Send an email: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Access a website securely: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each. Send email: SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol). Access website securely: HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure). Mark first answer in each line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Send email: SMTP","marks":1},{"criteria":"Access website securely: HTTPS","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"network protocol definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The owners of a large bakery have a Local Area Network (LAN) with a star topology. They order their supplies over the Internet. When data is transmitted from the bakery to the supplier, network protocols are used."},{"type":"text","value":"Define what is meant by a network protocol."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: a set of rules for communication.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A set of rules for communication","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"protocol layer definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"TCP/IP is a set of protocols based on layers."},{"type":"text","value":"With regards to network protocols, define what is meant by a layer."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: a division of network functionality.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A division of network functionality","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"protocol layer advantage","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"TCP/IP is a set of protocols based on layers."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one advantage of using layers to construct network protocols."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for identifying an advantage and 1 mark for description. A layer is self-contained, allowing different developers to focus on one aspect of the network. A layer can be edited or replaced without affecting other layers, promoting interoperability between vendors and systems.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Layers are self-contained / can be worked on separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"A layer can be changed without affecting others / promotes interoperability","marks":1}]}}]`),DJ={paper_id:jJ,qualification:OJ,component:TJ,title:PJ,year:JJ,session:IJ,total_marks:EJ,questions:RJ},MJ="J277-TOPIC-1.4",NJ="J277",LJ="TOPIC",BJ="Network Security",FJ=null,$J=null,zJ=26,WJ=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1(i)","parent_question":1,"topic":"characteristics of a LAN","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student's computer is part of their home Local Area Network (LAN). The LAN currently only has wired connections."},{"type":"text","value":"One characteristic of a LAN is that they are set up over a small geographical area."},{"type":"text","value":"Give one other characteristic of a LAN."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark from: Uses dedicated/own/internal hardware // no external/third party hardware/infrastructure // computers use MAC addresses to communicate within the LAN.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid characteristic given (e.g. uses own/internal hardware, no third-party infrastructure, devices use MAC addresses)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(ii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"benefits of wireless LAN","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the benefits of the student changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 4. e.g. Allows more devices to connect (e.g. televisions, mobile phones). Easier to connect/setup (e.g. easier for guests to connect). Home is likely a small area so short distance wireless is sufficient. Devices can move around / can connect from anywhere in the house / can use where wires don't reach (e.g. student using a laptop does not need to be tied to one place). Cheaper to purchase/install/setup for new devices because no additional/fewer wires are needed. Fewer trip hazards from trailing wires / fewer cables to damage. More compatible — some devices only have wireless connections. 'Easier'/'cheaper' on their own is NE.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Benefit 1 (e.g. more/wider range of devices can connect)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 2 (e.g. easier to connect/setup, or devices can move around the house)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 3 (e.g. cheaper since fewer wires needed, fewer trip hazards)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Benefit 4 (e.g. more compatibility with wireless-only devices)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1(iii)","parent_question":1,"topic":"drawbacks of wireless LAN","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State two drawbacks of changing their home LAN to include wireless connections."},{"type":"text","value":"1. ____"},{"type":"text","value":"2. ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. e.g. Prone to interference. Limited range of signal. Slower rate of transmission / less bandwidth / reduced network performance / increased latency (must specify what is slower — 'it's slower' is NE). Increased risk of security concerns (e.g. a hacker could connect to the wireless connection). Less stable connection. Higher chance of collisions / higher error rate. Mark first drawback in each answer space. 'Less reliable' on its own is TV.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Drawback 1 (e.g. interference, limited range, slower transmission, security risk, less stable, more collisions)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Drawback 2","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)","parent_question":2,"topic":"threat prevention methods","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one or more boxes on each row to identify all of the methods that can help to prevent each threat."},{"type":"table","headers":["Threat","Anti-malware","Penetration testing","Encryption","Firewall"],"rows":[["Spyware","","","",""],["Brute-force attack","","","",""],["Data interception","","","",""],["SQL injection","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per row. Each row has a primary required tick (✓) and may have additional optional ticks ((✓)) that are accepted but not required. Award the mark if the primary tick is present; do not penalise extra optional ticks or their absence. Spyware: required = Anti-malware; optional = Firewall. Brute-force attack: required = Firewall; optional = Penetration testing. Data interception: required = Encryption only (firewall and penetration testing do NOT prevent data interception in transit — do not award them). SQL injection: required = Penetration testing; optional = Firewall.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Spyware row: Anti-malware ticked (required). Firewall accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Penetration testing, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Brute-force attack row: Firewall ticked (required). Penetration testing accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Anti-malware, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"Data interception row: Encryption ticked (required, sole correct answer). Any other tick (Anti-malware, Penetration testing, Firewall) is wrong.","marks":1},{"criteria":"SQL injection row: Penetration testing ticked (required). Firewall accepted as optional extra; do not penalise if also ticked or if omitted. Other ticks (Anti-malware, Encryption) are wrong.","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"other computer system threats","marks":3,"command_word":"name and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Name and describe one threat to a computer system that is not Spyware, Brute Force attack, Data Interception or SQL Injection."},{"type":"text","value":"Threat: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Description: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for threat, 1 mark each to max 2 for description. Valid threats include: Social engineering (deception to manipulate users to gain personal data); Shoulder surfing (watching a person enter a password, then using it); Phishing (fake emails / clicking link in fake email; person gives away personal data); Pharming (software/fake website that redirects user; person enters personal data); Denial of Service / DOS / DDOS (multiple/flood of requests to a server; more than server can process / uses all bandwidth; server cannot respond / crashes / denies access); Hacker (unauthorised access to system/account; to delete/damage/access data); Virus/Malware (software that replicates/spreads; fills disk space; deletes/corrupts data / allows unauthorised access); Trojan (malware disguised as legitimate software; once installed acts as a virus); Worm (software that replicates across a network; uses up all bandwidth); Ransomware (encrypts/locks access to data; cannot access without paying a fee — 'ransom' alone is NE); Physical threat (damage to hardware; deletes/corrupts data). If threat is clearly wrong, do not FT.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Valid threat correctly named (not spyware, brute-force, data interception, or SQL injection)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description point 1 matching the threat","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description point 2 matching the threat","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3","parent_question":3,"topic":"forms of attack and prevention","marks":6,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Amir has a home network that includes two laptop computers, four mobile phones, and two televisions. Amir wants to protect the computers on his network from threats such as unauthorised access."},{"type":"text","value":"The following incomplete table contains a form of attack, description and method of preventing each attack. Complete the table by writing the missing Forms of attack, Descriptions and Methods of prevention."},{"type":"table","headers":["Form of attack","Description of attack","Method of prevention"],"rows":[["","A program attempting all possible password combinations",""],["Data interception","",""],["","","Anti-virus"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each completed box (6 total). Row 1: Form = Brute-force attack; Prevention = Strong password / set number of password attempts / firewall. Row 2: Description = Data transmission being read by unauthorised user/program; Prevention = Encryption. Row 3: Form = Malware / Virus / Trojan etc.; Description = Software that damages/deletes data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 form: Brute-force attack","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 1 prevention: Strong password / limit password attempts / firewall","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 description: Data transmission being read by unauthorised user/program","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 prevention: Encryption","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 form: Malware / Virus / Trojan","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 description: Software that damages/deletes data","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"human errors and preventative procedures","marks":6,"command_word":"identify and outline","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hospital stores patients' details on its computer network. The hospital is concerned about the security of its patients' details."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify three errors that the hospital staff could make that may endanger the security of the network. Outline a procedure that could be put in place to prevent each error."},{"type":"text","value":"Error 1: ____\\nProcedure 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Error 2: ____\\nProcedure 2: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Error 3: ____\\nProcedure 3: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct identification (max 3, AO2 1a) and 1 mark for each correct procedure (max 3, AO2 1b). Allow any reasonable combination of error and procedure. Examples: Bringing in files via any medium → not allowing/stopping external devices being used on the network. Downloading infected files from the internet → blocking/restricting access to insecure websites. Allowing physical access to the surgery's network → locking of doors / key cards / physical security procedure. Sending/sharing sensitive data with third parties → blocking/restricting access to USB ports / email / internet / printing.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Error 1 identified (e.g. brings in files via external media, downloads infected files, allows physical access, shares sensitive data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 1 mitigates Error 1 (e.g. block external devices, restrict insecure sites, lock doors/key cards, restrict USB/email)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Error 2 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 2 mitigates Error 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Error 3 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Procedure 3 mitigates Error 3","marks":1}]}}]`),QJ={paper_id:MJ,qualification:NJ,component:LJ,title:BJ,year:FJ,session:$J,total_marks:zJ,questions:WJ},VJ="J277-TOPIC-1.5.1",UJ="J277",HJ="TOPIC",GJ="Operating Systems",XJ=null,YJ=null,KJ=14,ZJ=[{question_id:"1.5.1-1",parent_question:1,topic:"operating system functions",marks:5,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Tick one box on each row to identify the computer operating system function that will manage each task."},{type:"table",headers:["Operating system function","Memory management","Peripheral management","User management","File management"],rows:[["Multitasking","","","",""],["Renaming a folder","","","",""],["Creation of user accounts","","","",""],["Installation of a printer driver","","","",""],["Transfer of data to and from RAM","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each correct row. Multitasking and transferring data to and from RAM are managed by memory management. Renaming a folder is file management. Creating user accounts is user management. Installing a printer driver is peripheral management.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Multitasking = memory management",marks:1},{criteria:"Renaming a folder = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Creation of user accounts = user management",marks:1},{criteria:"Installation of a printer driver = peripheral management",marks:1},{criteria:"Transfer of data to and from RAM = memory management",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.1-2",parent_question:2,topic:"operating system functions",marks:4,command_word:"complete",content:[{type:"text",value:"The table contains operating system functions and a task that each function performs."},{type:"text",value:"Complete the table by writing the two missing function names and a task performed by the two given functions."},{type:"table",headers:["Function","Task"],rows:[["","Moves data from secondary storage to RAM"],["Peripheral management",""],["","Allows the user to create, name and delete folders"],["User interface",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each missing function or task. The first function is memory management. A valid peripheral-management task includes receiving data from input devices, sending data to output devices, using device drivers, or enabling communication with peripherals. The third function is file management. A valid user-interface task includes receiving input from the user, outputting data to the user, or allowing the user to interact with a GUI or command prompt.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Memory management identified",marks:1},{criteria:"Valid peripheral-management task described",marks:1},{criteria:"File management identified",marks:1},{criteria:"Valid user-interface task described",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.1-3",parent_question:3,topic:"operating system functions",marks:5,command_word:"identify",content:[{type:"text",value:"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{type:"text",value:"Tick one box in each row to identify which function of the Operating System deals with each action."},{type:"table",headers:["Action","Memory management","Peripheral management","File management","User management"],rows:[["Creating a new folder to store documents in","","","",""],["Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM","","","",""],["Renaming a file","","","",""],["Reading data from a scanner","","","",""],["Changing the password required to log on to the computer","","","",""]]}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each correct row. Creating a new folder and renaming a file are file-management actions. Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM is memory management. Reading data from a scanner is peripheral management. Changing the logon password is user management. No mark should be awarded for a row with more than one box ticked.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Creating a new folder to store documents in = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Moving data from Virtual Memory to RAM = memory management",marks:1},{criteria:"Renaming a file = file management",marks:1},{criteria:"Reading data from a scanner = peripheral management",marks:1},{criteria:"Changing the password required to log on to the computer = user management",marks:1}]}}],eI={paper_id:VJ,qualification:UJ,component:HJ,title:GJ,year:XJ,session:YJ,total_marks:KJ,questions:ZJ},tI="J277-TOPIC-1.5.2",aI="J277",rI="TOPIC",nI="Utility Software",iI=null,sI=null,oI=21,cI=[{question_id:"1.5.2-1(i)",parent_question:1,topic:"utility software purpose",marks:1,command_word:"state",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer has utility software."},{type:"text",value:"State the purpose of utility software in a computer."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark. Utility software performs housekeeping or maintenance tasks, helps the computer run smoothly, or monitors, manages, configures, diagnoses, or fixes the system. Do not award a specific example on its own.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Valid general purpose of utility software stated",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-1(ii)",parent_question:1,topic:"encryption software",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A computer has utility software."},{type:"text",value:"Explain the purpose and function of encryption software."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark each to max 3. Encryption software scrambles or encodes data. It uses an algorithm or key to encrypt and decrypt the data. If the encrypted data is intercepted or accessed without authorisation, it is meaningless and cannot be understood.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Data is scrambled, encoded, or turned into ciphertext",marks:1},{criteria:"An algorithm or key is used to encrypt and decrypt the data",marks:1},{criteria:"Encrypted data is meaningless if intercepted or accessed without authorisation",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-2",parent_question:2,topic:"utility software description",marks:6,command_word:"complete",content:[{type:"text",value:"Complete the description of utility system software using the words provided in the box. Not all words are used."},{type:"text",value:"Terms: access, amount, apart, compression, consecutive, defragmentation, deleted, encryption, key, lock, quantity, separate, speed, understood"},{type:"text",value:"____ software changes data using a ____. If the changed data is intercepted, it cannot be ____. This software does not stop the data from being intercepted. ____ software analyses the data on a disk to find files that have been split and stored in separate locations. The split files are moved to be ____ in storage and the free space is moved together. This does not provide more storage space on the disk, instead it makes the ____ of the data faster because the read head does not have to move as far to access the next part of the file."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark for each completed term. Encryption software changes data using a key. If the changed data is intercepted, it cannot be understood. This software does not stop the data from being intercepted. Defragmentation software analyses the data on a disk to find files that have been split and stored in separate locations. The split files are moved to be consecutive in storage, making access to the data faster.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Encryption",marks:1},{criteria:"Key",marks:1},{criteria:"Understood",marks:1},{criteria:"Defragmentation",marks:1},{criteria:"Consecutive",marks:1},{criteria:"Access",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-3",parent_question:3,topic:"utility software purpose",marks:1,command_word:"state",content:[{type:"text",value:"The computer has an operating system and utility software."},{type:"text",value:"State the need for utility software in a computer."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark. Utility software performs housekeeping tasks, helps to monitor, manage, maintain, or diagnose the system, or helps the computer run smoothly and efficiently. Do not award an example on its own.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Valid need for utility software stated",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-4",parent_question:4,topic:"defragmentation benefits",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{type:"text",value:"Eve runs the defragmentation program on her computer."},{type:"text",value:"Explain why Eve's computer is more efficient after the program has been run."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 3. Files are stored contiguously, so they take less time to read or access. The read head does not need to move as far to reach the next piece of data. Free space is grouped together, so saving new files is faster and less likely to split them up.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Files are contiguous so reading or accessing files is faster",marks:1},{criteria:"The read head does not need to move as far to read the next part of a file",marks:1},{criteria:"Free space is grouped together so saving new files is faster or less likely to fragment them",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-5(a)",parent_question:5,topic:"disk fragmentation cause",marks:4,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A restaurant has a computer-based ordering system which is running slowly."},{type:"text",value:"A technician has said that the hard disc drive is fragmented. The technician has suggested using utility software to defragment the drive."},{type:"text",value:"Explain how the restaurant's hard disc could have become fragmented."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 4. Order files are saved to the system and then deleted once processed. New order files are created later. Some new files are larger than the gaps left by deleted files. Those files are split across separate locations on the disk.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Order files are saved and then deleted once processed",marks:1},{criteria:"New order files are created later",marks:1},{criteria:"Some new files are larger than the empty spaces left behind",marks:1},{criteria:"Files are split across the disk into separate locations",marks:1}]}},{question_id:"1.5.2-5(b)",parent_question:5,topic:"defragmentation software",marks:3,command_word:"explain",content:[{type:"text",value:"A restaurant has a computer-based ordering system which is running slowly."},{type:"text",value:"A technician has said that the hard disc drive is fragmented. The technician has suggested using utility software to defragment the drive."},{type:"text",value:"Explain how defragmentation software could overcome the issue of the slow computer system."}],answer:{guidance:"1 mark per point to max 3. Files on the hard disk are moved. Empty spaces are collected together. Files are stored together, so fewer disk accesses are needed when they are read.",marks_breakdown:[{criteria:"Files on the hard disk are moved",marks:1},{criteria:"Empty spaces are collected together",marks:1},{criteria:"Files are stored together so fewer disk accesses are needed",marks:1}]}}],lI={paper_id:tI,qualification:aI,component:rI,title:nI,year:iI,session:sI,total_marks:oI,questions:cI},dI="J277-TOPIC-1.6.1",uI="J277",pI="TOPIC",hI="Ethical, Legal, Cultural and Environmental Impact",mI=null,fI=null,gI=74,_I=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1.6.1-1","parent_question":1,"topic":"tablet computer upgrade impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A company designs and builds tablet computers. The company wants to reduce the cost of manufacturing their tablet computers."},{"type":"text","value":"The company also decides to stop releasing software updates for their older tablet computers. This means customers will need to change their devices more often."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of these decisions on the company and the customers."},{"type":"text","value":"- ethical issues\\n- environmental issues\\n- benefits and drawbacks"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Top-band responses include developed benefits and drawbacks, at least one ethical issue and one environmental issue, and clear reference to both the company and the customers. Indicative content includes increased profit or sales for the company, lower purchase cost for some customers, possible damage to reputation, customers being forced to replace devices more often, more e-waste, recycling or reuse of old devices, fairness to customers, and whether customers are clearly told about the decision.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about benefit, drawback, ethical, or environmental impact with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some required areas with some linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion covering benefits and drawbacks with ethical and environmental issues, linked to both company and customers","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-2","parent_question":2,"topic":"data protection legislation","marks":4,"command_word":"identify and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A youth centre is creating a network with 20 computers in a single building that will be connected in a Local Area Network (LAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one piece of legislation that the youth centre will need to follow when storing users' data and describe the steps they need to take to meet the requirements of this legislation."},{"type":"table","headers":["Section","Your answer"],"rows":[["Legislation",""],["Steps the youth centre needs to follow",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming the Data Protection Act 2018, DPA, GDPR, or data-protection legislation. 1 mark each to max 3 for valid steps such as keeping data secure, not sharing data without consent, only collecting relevant data, keeping it accurate and up to date, deleting it when no longer needed, only using it for the stated purpose, or allowing users to access their data.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Data Protection Act 2018, DPA, GDPR, or equivalent data-protection legislation identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"First valid compliance step described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid compliance step described","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third valid compliance step described","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-3","parent_question":3,"topic":"open source vs proprietary","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computer programmer has developed a computer game that they want to release for users to download over the internet."},{"type":"text","value":"The programmer needs to decide whether to release the game as open source or proprietary software."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the features, benefits and drawbacks of each type of licence for this program and make a recommendation to the programmer."},{"type":"text","value":"- features of each licence\\n- legal and ethical issues of each licence\\n- benefits and drawbacks of each licence"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Top-band responses cover both open source and proprietary software, address legal and ethical issues, benefits and drawbacks, and give a justified recommendation. Indicative content includes open source usually being free with editable source code and redistribution rights, proprietary software being sold with restricted access to the code, copyright applying to both, wider access and easier bug fixing for open source, limited financial gain for open source, and greater profit and control for proprietary software.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic facts about open source and/or proprietary software","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some required features, issues, benefits, or drawbacks","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed comparison of both licence types with justified recommendation","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-4(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"proprietary software benefits","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An artist is working with a programmer on the development of a new piece of software."},{"type":"text","value":"The software will allow users to edit images on devices such as mobile telephones. They are considering releasing the software as open source instead of proprietary."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two benefits to the artist and programmer of releasing the software as proprietary."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for valid benefits or linked developments. Valid points include earning money from sales or licences, preventing users from seeing or editing the source code, reducing unauthorised copying or reselling, and keeping more control over what users can do with the software. Benefits must be to the artist and programmer, not the user.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Can earn money by selling the software or charging licence fees","marks":1},{"criteria":"Users cannot access or edit the source code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Unauthorised copying, sharing, or reselling is reduced","marks":1},{"criteria":"The creators keep more control over how the software is used","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-4(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"open source benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An artist is working with a programmer on the development of a new piece of software."},{"type":"text","value":"The software will allow users to edit images on devices such as mobile telephones. They are considering releasing the software as open source instead of proprietary."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one benefit to the users of releasing the software as open source."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a valid user benefit and 1 mark for linked development. Valid points include users being able to view or edit the source code, tailor the software to their needs, fix errors, learn how it works, access it for free, or redistribute it with changes.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Users can access or edit the source code, or use the software for free","marks":1},{"criteria":"Linked benefit such as adapting it, fixing errors, learning from it, or redistributing changes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-5","parent_question":5,"topic":"facial recognition impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A shopping centre has a security system that includes CCTV cameras to record activities in the centre."},{"type":"text","value":"The security system is being upgraded to include the use of facial recognition to identify, track the movements of and record individuals throughout the shopping centre."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative impacts of this upgrade including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- ethical issues\\n- privacy issues\\n- legal issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes legal requirements under the Data Protection Act, informing customers, keeping data secure, using evidence to identify criminals, customers feeling safer, people feeling watched or tracked, privacy concerns about storing movement data, and the fact that the centre is private property. Stronger answers give a balanced discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one impact with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion of most required areas with some explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-6","parent_question":6,"topic":"AI and social networking","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"*Social networking websites use artificial intelligence (AI) to monitor posts from users."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the positive and negative uses of AI by social networking websites including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- legal issues\\n- ethical issues\\n- privacy issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes checking posts for plagiarism or illegal material, complying with data-protection rules, users agreeing through terms and conditions, concerns about users being monitored or posts being blocked incorrectly, safer use of the website, and privacy concerns about users feeling watched. Stronger answers balance positives and negatives across legal, ethical, and privacy issues.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one issue with limited development","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion of most required areas with some explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Balanced, developed discussion across legal, ethical, and privacy issues","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-7","parent_question":7,"topic":"legislation identification","marks":5,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box on each row to identify the legislation that would cover each of the given events."},{"type":"table","headers":["Event","The Data Protection Act (2018)","Computer Misuse Act (1990)","Copyright Designs and Patents Act (1988)"],"rows":[["A company transmits personal data to another company without the individual's permission.","","",""],["A school accidentally publishes their students' addresses on the school website.","","",""],["The interface for a piece of software is replicated by a rival company.","","",""],["A user leaves a computer logged on and another person leaves them a message on their desktop.","","",""],["A student guesses their teacher's password and accesses their computer account.","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct row. Transmitting personal data without permission and publishing student addresses are covered by the Data Protection Act. Replicating a software interface is covered by the Copyright Designs and Patents Act. Leaving a message on another user's logged-in desktop and guessing a password to access an account are covered by the Computer Misuse Act.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Transmitting personal data without permission = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Publishing student addresses = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Replicating a software interface = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Leaving a message on another user's desktop = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Guessing a teacher's password and accessing the account = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-8","parent_question":8,"topic":"copyright legislation","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Layla is an artist. She draws images by hand. The image is then scanned and stored on a computer."},{"type":"text","value":"Layla wants to protect her images so they cannot be copied by other people."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify which legislation can help protect Layla's images."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. Copyright Designs and Patents Act.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Copyright Designs and Patents Act identified","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-9(i)","parent_question":9,"topic":"open source licence benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve uses a computer to write a computer game. She wants people to be able to download her program online."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve is choosing between an open source and proprietary licence."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two benefits to the customers of Eve choosing an open source licence."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid benefit. Customers may get the software free of charge, and they can adapt it or add features.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Free of charge","marks":1},{"criteria":"Customers can adapt it or add features","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-9(ii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"proprietary licence benefits","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Eve's computer has system software including an Operating System and Utility Software."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve uses a computer to write a computer game. She wants people to be able to download her program online."},{"type":"text","value":"Eve is choosing between an open source and proprietary licence."},{"type":"text","value":"Give two benefits to Eve of choosing a proprietary licence."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each valid benefit. She can charge customers or earn a profit, and she can restrict what users can do with the software so they cannot edit it.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"She can charge customers or earn a profit","marks":1},{"criteria":"She can restrict what users can do or prevent them editing it","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-10","parent_question":10,"topic":"digital technology in medicine","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The use of digital technology in medicine is constantly evolving."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of digital technology on medicine including:"},{"type":"text","value":"- diagnosing patients\\n- treating patients\\n- storage of records"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes diagnosis becoming faster through searching records, pattern finding, or AI-assisted equipment; treatment using automation or remote specialist control; and centralised storage of records being easier for medical staff to access. Drawbacks include incorrect diagnoses, hacking, fatal software errors, reduced human interaction, and privacy or security risks if centralised records are leaked.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about diagnosis, treatment, or record storage","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some of the required areas with linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed discussion across diagnosis, treatment, and record storage with balanced impacts","marks":3}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-11","parent_question":11,"topic":"legislation identification","marks":6,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A law company currently use a Local Area Network (LAN) linked to a Wide Area Network (WAN)."},{"type":"text","value":"They want to upgrade their system to utilise cloud storage. Fig. 2 lists some actions that may take place in the law company's office."},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to show which legislation applies to each action."},{"type":"table","headers":["Action","Data Protection Act 2018","Computer Misuse Act 1990","Copyright Designs and Patents Act 1988"],"rows":[["Using a picture for the law company's new logo without the original creator's permission.","","",""],["A secretary accessing a lawyer's personal email account without permission.","","",""],["Making a copy of the latest Hollywood blockbuster movie and sharing it with a client.","","",""],["Storing customer data insecurely.","","",""],["A lawyer installing a key logger on the secretary's computer.","","",""],["Selling client's personal legal data to a marketing company without their permission.","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct row. Using a picture without permission and copying and sharing a movie are covered by the Copyright Designs and Patents Act. Unauthorised access to email and installing a key logger are covered by the Computer Misuse Act. Storing customer data insecurely and selling personal legal data without permission are covered by the Data Protection Act. No mark for a row with more than one tick.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Using a picture without permission = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Accessing a lawyer's email without permission = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Copying and sharing a movie = Copyright Designs and Patents Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Storing customer data insecurely = Data Protection Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Installing a key logger = Computer Misuse Act","marks":1},{"criteria":"Selling personal legal data without permission = Data Protection Act","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1.6.1-12","parent_question":12,"topic":"smartphone upgrade impact","marks":8,"command_word":"discuss","content":[{"type":"text","value":"* People often want to buy the most up-to-date smartphones, even though the smartphone they own still works."},{"type":"text","value":"Discuss the impact of people wanting to upgrade to the latest smartphone."},{"type":"text","value":"In your answer, you might consider the impact on:\\n- smartphone users\\n- cultural issues\\n- ethical issues\\n- environmental issues"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Banded mark scheme. Indicative content includes users wanting the newest devices to fit in or gain new features, financial pressure on users or parents, the digital divide, social pressure and possible bullying, devices being designed for short lifespans, e-waste, landfill, toxic waste, wasted precious metals, and the environmental impact of disposal in the UK or abroad.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Band 1 (1-2 marks): Basic point about one impact on users, ethics, culture, or the environment","marks":2},{"criteria":"Band 2 (3-5 marks): Reasonable discussion covering some of the required areas with linked explanation","marks":3},{"criteria":"Band 3 (6-8 marks): Developed discussion across users, cultural, ethical, and environmental impacts","marks":3}]}}]`),yI={paper_id:dI,qualification:uI,component:pI,title:hI,year:mI,session:fI,total_marks:gI,questions:_I},wI="J277-TOPIC-2.1.1",bI="J277",kI="TOPIC",vI="Computational Thinking",xI=null,CI=null,qI=61,SI=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1","parent_question":1,"topic":"computational-thinking-techniques","marks":3,"command_word":"draw","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Computational thinking techniques were used to design a security system.\\n\\nDraw one line to match each computational thinking technique to its correct example."},{"type":"matching","left_heading":"Computational thinking technique","right_heading":"Example","prompts":[{"id":"abstraction","label":"Abstraction"},{"id":"decomposition","label":"Decomposition"},{"id":"algorithmic-thinking","label":"Algorithmic thinking"}],"options":[{"id":"A","label":"The system is broken down into modules for security and payments."},{"id":"B","label":"There is an increase in the amount of RAM in the computer running the system."},{"id":"C","label":"The user's identity, PIN and password are required. All other data is ignored."},{"id":"D","label":"The step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed."}]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct link for abstraction to the third example ('The user's identity, PIN and password are required. All other data is ignored.'). Correct link for decomposition to the first example ('The system is broken down into modules for security and payments.'). Correct link for algorithmic thinking to the bottom example ('The step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed.'). No mark is awarded for more than one connection from a box.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct link for abstraction to third example (user's identity, PIN and password required; all other data ignored)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct link for decomposition to first example (system is broken down into modules for security and payments)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct link for algorithmic thinking to bottom example (step-by-step process for users to securely pay online is designed)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-1.png"}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"data-types","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Students take part in a sports day. The students are put into teams.\\n\\nStudents gain points depending on their result and the year group they are in. The points are added to the team score.\\n\\nThe team with the most points at the end of the sports day wins.\\n\\nData about the teams and students is stored in a sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify the most appropriate data type for each variable used by the program.\\n\\nEach data type must be different."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Example","Data type"],"rows":[["teamName","\\"Super-Team\\"",""],["studentYearGroup","11",""],["javelinThrow","18.2",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept alternative equivalent correct data types (e.g. single/double/decimal for BP3). Do not accept char for BP1. Examiner's Comments: This question was completed very well by the vast majority of candidates, showing that the use of data types are now well understood by centres.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"teamName = String","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentYearGroup = Integer","marks":1},{"criteria":"javelinThrow = Real / Float (accept single/double/decimal)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-002.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-a-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"linear-search-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student names for a team are stored in an array with the identifier \`theTeam\`.\\n\\nAn example of the data in this array is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Data","Ali","Eve","Ling","Nina","Sarah","Tom"]]},{"type":"text","value":"A linear search function is used to find whether a student is in the team. The function:\\n\\n- takes a student name as a parameter\\n- returns \`True\` if the student name is in the array\\n- returns \`False\` if the student name is not in the array.\\n\\nComplete the design of an algorithm for the linear search function."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"function linearSearch(studentName)\\n   for count = 0 to ____\\n     if theTeam[____] == ____ then\\n       return ____\\n     endif\\n   next count\\n   return False\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept 6 // theTeam.length() for BP1 (Python). Accept alternative length functions e.g. len(). Accept count = 5 (and equivalents) for BP1. Accept \\"True\\" for BP4. Do not allow obvious spaces in variable names. Ignore capitalisation. Examiner's Comments: This was a challenging question revolving around the use of an array that is iterated through to implement a linear search. Many candidates achieved two marks for the first and last points, understanding that the loop would repeat from indexes 0 to 5 and that the value True would be returned if the item was found. As usual, allowance was given for off-by-one errors with this loop because of the prevalence of Python in centres and how count controlled loops are handled in this language. The most challenging mark was certainly the use of count as the index of the theTeam array, with very few candidates correctly identifying this as the missing element.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First blank: theTeam.length() - 1 // 5 (accept 6 // theTeam.length() for Python)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second blank (array index): count","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third blank (comparison value): studentName","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth blank (return value): True","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-003.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-a-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"trace-table","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm calculates the number of points a student gets for the distance they throw in the javelin:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 javelinThrow = input(\\"Enter distance\\")\\n02 yearGroup = input(\\"Enter year group\\")\\n03 if javelinThrow >= 20.0 then\\n04   score = 3\\n05 elseif javelinThrow >= 10.0 then\\n06   score = 2\\n07 else\\n08   score = 1\\n09 endif\\n10 if yearGroup != 11 then\\n11   score = score * 2\\n12 endif\\n13 print(\\"The score is\\", score)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm when a student in year 10 throws a distance of 14.3\\n\\nYou may not need to use all the rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","javelinThrow","yearGroup","score","Output"],"rows":[["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example trace: Line 01: javelinThrow = 14.3. Line 02: yearGroup = 10. Line 06: score = 2. Line 11: score = 4. Line 13: Output \\"The score is 4\\". Answer may include lines where no changes or output happens (i.e. lines 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12). Where variable doesn't change, current value may be repeated on subsequent lines. Max 3 if in wrong order or additional (incorrect) changes. Penalise line numbers once then FT. Allow FT for BP4 for current value of score. BP4 must not include comma. Ignore superfluous spaces. Ignore quotation marks. Treat any entry in output column as an output, even if \\"x\\", \\"-\\" or \\"0\\". Examiner's Comments: Trace tables have appeared multiple times in J277 examination papers now and candidates are hopefully familiar with the expectations, which are consistent across series. Candidates should be encouraged to simply leave boxes blank if no output is given on a particular line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"javelinThrow set to 14.3 on line 01 and yearGroup set to 10 on line 02","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 2 on line 06","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 4 on line 11","marks":1},{"criteria":"\\"The score is 4\\" output on line 13 with no additional outputs (allow input statements)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-003.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-b.png"}},{"question_id":"2(c)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"validation-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated.\\n\\nThe height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \`VALID\` or \`NOT VALID\` depending on the height input.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (checking for valid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nExample 2 (checking for invalid input):\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif\\n\\nAnswers using AND/OR for BP2 and BP3 must be logically correct e.g. if height >=40 and height <=180. Do not accept if height >=40 and <=180. Answers using OR will reverse output for BP4. BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3. Allow FT for BP4 if reasonable attempt at validating (must include at least one boundary). Ignore conversion to int on input. input cannot be used as a variable name. Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for a high-level language / ERL. Do not accept => (wrong way around) or the mathematical >= symbol (not available on keyboard). No obvious spaces in variable names. Penalise once and then FT.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses it","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 // < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 // > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-005.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-c-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(c)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"test-data","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests.\\n\\nComplete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Normal: Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive), e.g. 50. Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0. Erroneous: Any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value. No need to include decimals, e.g. accept 50. Ignore cm if given. Answer must be actual data (e.g. 50) and not description of data (e.g. \\"a value between 40 and 180\\"). If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for BP3.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: any value between 40 and 180 inclusive (e.g. 50)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 or 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: any value less than 40, any value greater than 180, or any non-numeric value","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-005.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-c-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(d)","parent_question":2,"topic":"sql-query","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database.\\n\\nThe database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct answer: SELECT StudentID, TeamName FROM TblResult WHERE YearGroup = 11. Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1. Spelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments). Examiner's Comments: A number of candidates struggled with this question. Problems included spaces in field names, misspelling of the table name (such as TblResults plural when TblResult singular was given) or misunderstanding of the WHERE clause.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE keyword used","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11 condition","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-d.png"}},{"question_id":"2(e)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"abstraction-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design). Examples: 'focus on student names and events', 'ignore data such as students' favourite subjects', 'store year groups instead of ages or DOB', 'shows student IDs instead of full student details'. Must be applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is. Give BOD (benefit of the doubt) where this is unclear. Examiner's Comments: Where the context of the sports day was used, candidates were generally successful in achieving this mark. No marks were given by examiners for generic definitions of what the term abstraction means.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data in the design, applicable to the sports day program context","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-e-i.png"}},{"question_id":"2(e)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"decomposition-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or any example of breaking down the database into tables. Examples: 'splits the program up into different events', 'separates the validation routines into subroutines', 'breaks the database down into a table per event'. Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description of what decomposition is. Give BOD where this is unclear. Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields (e.g. split into StudentID, YearGroup, etc). BOD if answer discusses one table but suggests other tables could be used. Do not give answers relating simply to data being split into smaller groups unless this clearly relates to how data is decomposed into tables in the DB. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines OR breaking down the database into tables, applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-e-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"2(f)","parent_question":2,"topic":"algorithm-design","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score).\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1:\\nhighscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nExample 2 (alternative):\\nscores = []\\nteams = []\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  scores.append(score)\\n  teams.append(team)\\nendwhile\\nhighscore = max[scores]\\nhighteam = teams[scores.index(highscore)]\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nFor BP3, allow \\"stop\\" to be entered for either team name or score (or both). Allow third input (e.g. \\"do you wish to stop?\\"). Allow use of break (or equivalent) to exit loop for BP3. Allow use of recursive function(s) for BP2/3. Initialisation of variables not needed - assume variables are 0 or empty string if not set. Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score, assume only one team has the highest score. BP4/5 could be done in many ways. Allow any logically valid method. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists. FT for BP6 if attempt made at calculating highest score/name. If answer simply asks for multiple entries (not using iteration), BP2 and 3 cannot be accessed but all others available. For minor syntax errors (e.g. missing quotation marks or == for assignment, spaces in variable names) penalise once then FT. input cannot be used as a variable name.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store / use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"Iterates to enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-2-f.png"}},{"question_id":"3","parent_question":3,"topic":"computational-thinking-definitions","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the description of computational thinking using the given list of terms.\\n\\nNot all terms will be used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: abstraction, algorithm, computation, decomposition, evaluation, flowchart, origin, program, pseudocode, research, sequence, thinking"},{"type":"text","value":"Computational thinking is the process of analysing problems so that they can be solved in a logical way.\\n\\nThe process of ____ breaks down a problem into smaller, more manageable parts.\\n\\nThe process of ____ removes unnecessary detail from the problem, so that the main components can be focused on.\\n\\nAlgorithmic ____ identifies the main steps needed to solve the problem and the ____ that the steps are completed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Answers in order: decomposition, abstraction, thinking, sequence. Ignore minor misspellings.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First blank: decomposition","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second blank: abstraction","marks":1},{"criteria":"Third blank: thinking","marks":1},{"criteria":"Fourth blank: sequence","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-3.png"}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"algorithm-design-game","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to play a game with the following rules.\\n\\n- the player is asked 3 addition questions\\n- each question asks the player to add together two random whole numbers between 1 and 10 inclusive\\n- if the player gets the correct answer, 1 is added to their score\\n- at the end of the game their score is displayed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 6. Example answer:\\nscore = 0\\nfor count = 1 to 3\\n  num1 = random(1, 10)\\n  num2 = random(1, 10)\\n  ans = input(\\"What is \\" + num1 + \\" + \\" + num2 + \\"?\\")\\n  if ans = num1 + num2 then\\n    score = score + 1\\n  end if\\nnext count\\nprint(\\"You scored \\" + score)\\n\\nNo need to cast data to string/integer. If random numbers chosen, BP3 must use these. If no random numbers chosen, allow manually setting values. BP6 can be awarded for either a loop repeating 3 times or the same code written out 3 times. BP5 can be given FT if sensible attempt at BP4. Do not award BP6 if same numbers used for every question. Must pick new values each time. Do not penalise potential off by 1 errors for looping (Python) or random number generation.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Initialise / declare score (to zero) before use, outside of any loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Generates 2 random numbers between 1 and 10","marks":1},{"criteria":"Inputs answer from user displaying suitable numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if input is correct answer","marks":1},{"criteria":"If correct adds 1 to score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeats BP2 to 5 three times (for bullet points attempted); outputs score after reasonable attempt at counting","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-008.png"],["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-024.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-4.png"}},{"question_id":"5","parent_question":5,"topic":"high-vs-low-level-languages","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The table contains four statements about programming languages.\\n\\nTick one box in each row to identify whether each statement describes a low-level programming language or a high-level programming language."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","Low-level","High-level"],"rows":[["The same language can be used on computers that use different hardware","",""],["It allows the user to directly manipulate memory","",""],["It allows the user to write English-like words","",""],["It always needs to be translated into object code or machine code","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per row. No mark if more than 1 tick for that row. Allow other indications of choice (e.g. cross) as long as clear."},{"type":"table","headers":["Statement","Answer"],"rows":[["The same language can be used on computers that use different hardware","High-level"],["It allows the user to directly manipulate memory","Low-level"],["It allows the user to write English-like words","High-level"],["It always needs to be translated into object code or machine code","High-level"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Examiner's Comments: The strongest responses showed a good understanding of the difference between low-level and high-level languages. Incorrect responses tended to be on the first row related to portability. A high-level language such as Python is portable, with translators available for many different types of processors. A low-level language is specific to one type of processor."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Row 1 (same language used on different hardware): High-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 2 (directly manipulate memory): Low-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (write English-like words): High-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 4 (always needs to be translated into object code or machine code): High-level","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-026.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-5.png"}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"computational-thinking-techniques","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State the name of each of the following computational thinking techniques."},{"type":"text","value":"Breaking a complex problem down into smaller problems.\\n\\n____"},{"type":"text","value":"Hiding or removing irrelevant details from a problem to reduce the complexity.\\n\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Decomposition (for breaking a complex problem down into smaller problems). Abstraction (for hiding or removing irrelevant details). Correct answer only. Ignore spelling errors. Examiner's Comments: This question was answered well by the majority of candidates.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Decomposition","marks":1},{"criteria":"Abstraction","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-010.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-6.png"}},{"question_id":"7(a)","parent_question":7,"topic":"flowchart-trace","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program creates usernames for a school. The first design of the program is shown in the flowchart in Fig. 2."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/username-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Flowchart that builds a username from the first three letters of firstName and the first two letters of surname."},{"type":"text","value":"For example, using the process in Fig. 2, Tom Ward's username would be TomWa.\\n\\nState, using the process in Fig. 2, the username for Rebecca Ellis."}],"answer":{"guidance":"RebEl. Correct Answer Only (allow any case).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"RebEl (allow any case)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-011.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-a.png"}},{"question_id":"7(b)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"string-manipulation","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The program design is updated to create usernames as follows:\\n\\n- If the person is a teacher, their username is the last 3 letters of their surname and then the first 2 letters of their first name.\\n- If the person is a student, their username is the first 3 letters of their first name and then the first 2 letters of their surname.\\n\\nWhat would be the username for a teacher called Fred Biscuit using the updated process?"}],"answer":{"guidance":"uitFr. Correct Answer Only (allow any case). (Last 3 letters of 'Biscuit' = 'uit', first 2 letters of 'Fred' = 'Fr'.)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"uitFr (allow any case)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-011.png"],["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"7(b)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"algorithm-design-string","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm for the updated program design shown in (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example: Ask the user to input the data, store in variables firstname, surname and role. Check whether the role entered is teacher. If it is, join the right 3 most letters in surname with the left 2 letters in firstname. Store this in username. If it is not teacher, join the left 3 letters from firstname with the left 2 letters from surname. Store this in username. Output the value in username. 1 mark for each correct bullet to a maximum of 6. If used, a flowchart should represent the bulleted steps in the answer column.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Taking firstname, surname and teacher or student as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checking IF role is teacher/student (using appropriate selection)","marks":1},{"criteria":"For teacher: generating last 3 letters of surname using appropriate string manipulation","marks":1},{"criteria":"For teacher: generating first 2 letters of firstname and adding to previous","marks":1},{"criteria":"For student: correctly calculating (first 3 letters of firstname + first 2 letters of surname)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct concatenation and output","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-027.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-7-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"8(i)","parent_question":8,"topic":"abstraction-definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven.\\n\\nEach car is given a star rating of 1 to 5, based on the age of the car and the number of miles it has been driven. This rating is recorded in the computer system.\\n\\nDefine the term abstraction."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Hiding / ignoring / removing detail // focussing on certain parts of a problem.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Hiding / ignoring / removing detail, or focussing on certain parts of a problem","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-i.png"}},{"question_id":"8(ii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"abstraction-in-context","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one example of how abstraction has been used in the design of this star rating system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Focus on age / number of miles. Ignore other factors (such as make, model, etc). Allow other examples of factors to ignore / remove.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Focus on age / number of miles OR ignore other factors such as make, model, etc.","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"8(iii)","parent_question":8,"topic":"defensive-design-authentication","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how authentication could be used as part of the defensive design considerations for this computer system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ensures only certain users can access the system. Using password / other example of authentication technique. Allow other examples of authentication for BP2.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ensures only certain users can access the system","marks":1},{"criteria":"Using password / other example of authentication technique (e.g. biometric, PIN, 2FA)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/question/page-013.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/topics/2.1.1/markscheme/page-014.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/topics/2.1.1/dev-qa/q-8-iii.png"}}]`),AI={"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/topics/ExamBuilder/2.1.1 Computational thinking.pdf"},jI={paper_id:wI,qualification:bI,component:kI,title:vI,year:xI,session:CI,total_marks:qI,questions:SI,maps:AI},OI="J277-TOPIC-2.1.2",TI="J277",PI="TOPIC",JI="Designing, Creating and Refining Algorithms",II=null,EI=null,RI=221,DI=JSON.parse('[{"question_id":"1","parent_question":1,"topic":"pin-validation-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"As part of a security system, the user is asked to choose a 4-digit value for their PIN. This PIN cannot be 1234 or 4321.\\n\\nCreate an algorithm that:\\n\\n- takes the PIN as input as a string\\n- checks that the PIN is 4 characters in length\\n- checks that the PIN is not either \\"1234\\" or \\"4321\\"\\n- outputs `\\"VALID PIN\\"` if the PIN meets all of the criteria\\n- outputs `\\"INVALID PIN\\"` if the PIN does not meet all of the criteria.\\n\\nYou do not need to check that the digits entered are numeric."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example code:"},{"type":"code","value":"pin = input(\\"Enter PIN\\")\\nif pin.length == 4 and pin != \\"1234\\" and pin != \\"4321\\" then\\n  print(\\"VALID PIN\\")\\nelse:\\n  print(\\"INVALID PIN\\")"},{"type":"list","items":["If diagram/flowchart given, ignore shapes if incorrect and mark text inside as algorithm.","Note - | or || means OR in some languages. AND is &.","BOD input and use/comparison of PIN as integer.","No need for quotation marks around 1234 / 4321.","Allow equivalent outputs.","BP3 and 4 can be combined or as separate checks. If combined, must logically work.","BP3 and 4 can be checks for positive or negative. Check must refer to variable for both comparisons.","BP5 given if invalid output (once or multiple times) for all invalid PINs even if valid also output.","BP6 only given if valid is output as the only output.","If sensible attempt at BP3 and BP4 (but incorrect), FT to BP5 and 6.","Section A so candidates can respond in pseudocode, structured English, flowcharts, etc. However, must not simply be a repeat of the question."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and storage/use of PIN","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at selection / condition controlled loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for 4 digits","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for both 1234 and 4321","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"INVALID PIN\\" after sensible attempt at all three checks","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"VALID PIN\\" after sensible attempt at all three checks, only when PIN is fully valid and no contradictory output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"ocr-reference-language-input","marks":1,"command_word":"Tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Rockfest is a yearly music festival. Visitors buy tickets to attend and watch the music acts.\\n\\nA computer system is created to sell tickets and store information about visitors and the music acts.\\n\\nVisitors are asked to choose a password. The password is entered twice and stored in the variables passA and passB.\\n\\nTick (✓) one box to identify the correct OCR Exam Reference Language statements for the design of this task."},{"type":"table","headers":["OCR Exam Reference Language to enter two passwords","Tick (✓) one box"],"rows":[["`passA and passB = input(\\"enter password twice\\")`",""],["`input(\\"enter your password\\") = passA`\\n`input(\\"enter your password again\\") = passB`",""],["`passA = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`passB = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""],["`\\"passA\\" = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`\\"passB\\" = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"3rd box ticked. The correct OCR Exam Reference Language uses the format: passA = input(\\"enter your password\\") and passB = input(\\"enter your password again\\").\\n\\nPleasingly, the vast majority of candidates were able to identify the correct syntax for code to store data in two distinct variables."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"3rd box ticked (passA = input(...); passB = input(...))","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"password-match-algorithm","marks":3,"command_word":"Design","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Design an algorithm that:\\n\\n- checks if the passwords stored in `passA` and `passB` are the same\\n- outputs `\\"passwords match\\"` if they are the same\\n- outputs `\\"passwords do not match\\"` if they are not the same."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example code:"},{"type":"code","value":"if passA == passB:\\n  print(\\"passwords match\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"passwords do not match\\")\\nend if"},{"type":"list","items":["Design so answer can be flowchart, etc. If flowchart, decision (diamond) box needed for BP1, True/False labels essential for BP2 and 3. Output (parallelogram) box needed for BP2 and 3.","Allow alternative messages. Ignore superfluous code (including inputs or overwriting variables) as long as it does not affect outcome. Could be done as separate if statements.","Do not allow elseif/elif etc by itself for BP1 unless complete logical comparison (e.g. elif passA != passB). FT to BP2 and 3.","BP2 and 3 require a sensible attempt at comparing variables in BP1 to be given. passA and passB must be correct with no spaces. Ignore case."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks if passA and passB are equal","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if True (\\"passwords match\\")","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if False (\\"passwords do not match\\")","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"function-definition-calculateprice","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tickets to the festival are £60 each. Visitors can buy multiple tickets. For example, a visitor buying 4 tickets would pay £240.\\n\\nWrite a function, `calculatePrice()` that:\\n\\n- takes the number of tickets to be purchased as a parameter\\n- calculates and returns the total price to pay.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\nfunction calculatePrice(num)\\n    price = num * 60\\n    return price\\nendfunction\\n```\\n\\nSection B so must be high level code. Do not accept x for multiplication. Do not accept spaces in function, variable or parameter names if clearly obvious.\\n\\nAll code must be within function definition (if present). BP3 – if no parameter present, allow multiplying by sensible value (e.g. input value). FT overwriting parameter.\\n\\nBP4 must be return. Do not credit output/print. Value returned can be with or without £ sign and other text. FT for BP4 if sensible attempt made at calculation.\\n\\nAccept multiple parameters passed in. Accept returning by assigning to function name (e.g. calculatePrice = price), some languages such as VB do this.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function definition for calculatePrice()","marks":1},{"criteria":"...with parameter that is not overwritten","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculate price based on multiplying parameter (if present) by 60","marks":1},{"criteria":"...always return calculated price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"function-call-calculateprice","marks":2,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:\\n\\n- uses the function `calculatePrice()` to find the price for a visitor buying 3 tickets\\n- stores the result in the variable `totalPrice`\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\ntotalPrice = calculatePrice(3)\\n```\\n\\nDo not accept == for assignment. Allow FT to BP2 only if sensible attempt at BP1 (e.g. missing/wrong parameter).\\n\\nAllow multiple values passed in as long as 3 is one of the values. Do not accept definitions of a function – must be calling the existing function. Penalise spaces in variable / function name only if clearly obvious. Ignore superfluous code. Ignore case.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"calculatePrice(3) called","marks":1},{"criteria":"...return value assigned to totalPrice","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"trace-table-for-loop","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The number of different music acts on each of the three stages is stored in the array `actNumbers`.\\n\\nEach index of the array represents one stage."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2"],"rows":[["Data","10","14","6"]]},{"type":"text","value":"`actNumbers`"},{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm adds up the total number of acts on all three stages."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 count = 0\\n02 for x = 0 to 2\\n03 count = count + actNumbers[x]\\n04 next x\\n05 print(count)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm. You may not need to use all rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","count","x","Output"],"rows":[["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""],["","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore line numbers - not required. Mark order of values. Values can be repeated in count and x columns, look at changes.\\n\\nFT for BP4 if incorrect answer calculated but output. Allow last value of count calculated to be output for the mark. Give BOD for \\"-\\", \\"nothing\\", etc as extra in output column.\\n\\nTrace: count initially 0; count increases to 10, 24, 30; x goes from 0, 1, 2 (, 3); 30 output on final line.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Count initially set to 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"count increases to 10, 24, 30 and no other changes","marks":1},{"criteria":"x goes from 0, 1, 2 (, 3) and no other changes","marks":1},{"criteria":"30 output and no other output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"refining-loop-for-new-array","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The array `actNumbers` is changed to include data for two more stages. The new array is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4"],"rows":[["Data","10","14","6","9","11"]]},{"type":"text","value":"`actNumbers`"},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm from part (i) needs refining to work for the new array. The algorithm is repeated here:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 count = 0\\n02 for x = 0 to 2\\n03 count = count + actNumbers[x]\\n04 next x\\n05 print(count)"},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the line number that will need to be changed for the new array and write the updated line of code."},{"type":"text","value":"Line number ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Updated code ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must use x as counter variable. Accept rewrites in other languages.\\n\\nBOD 0 to 5 / range(5) etc. Accept other changes that implement the change successfully e.g. using length of actNumbers.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 02 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"For x = 0 to 4 (updated code)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(c)(iii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"refining-output-concatenation","marks":1,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Refine line 05 in the algorithm to output `\\"The total act count is \\"` and then the content of the variable `count`."},{"type":"text","value":"For example:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"The total act count is 50"},{"type":"text","value":"New line 05 ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nprint(\\"The total act count is \\" + count)\\n```\\n\\nAllow other concatenation operators e.g. & (VB), dot (PHP, Rust), double dot (Lua), etc. Allow comma. Must have some form of separator. Ignore spacing. Ignore case. Ignore casting.\\n\\nAllow other commands that are equivalent to print (e.g. echo, output, etc). Allow multiple print statements. Allow string interpolation or language specific examples e.g. f strings in Python, $ before variables in PHP.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"print(\\"The total act count is \\" + count) or equivalent with separator","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(d)","parent_question":2,"topic":"ticket-booking-loop","marks":6,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 500 tickets available for the music festival. The number of tickets currently available is stored in the variable `tickets`.\\n\\nComplete the algorithm to:\\n\\n- prompt the user to enter how many tickets they would like and take this value as input\\n- output `\\"Tickets booked\\"` and reduce the value stored in `tickets` if there are enough tickets available\\n- output `\\"Not enough tickets\\"` if there are not enough tickets available\\n- repeat the steps until there are no tickets left.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"tickets = 500"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\n\\n```\\ntickets = 500\\nwhile tickets > 0\\n  num = input(\\"enter num tickets\\")\\n  if tickets >= num then\\n    print(\\"tickets booked\\")\\n    tickets = tickets - num\\n  else\\n    print(\\"not enough tickets\\")\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\n```\\n\\nAllow any suitable confirmation message for BP3 (booked / not enough) but must be right way around. BP2 must allow tickets to be booked when exact number remain.\\n\\nBP3 and 4 dependent on BP2; if BP2 incorrect / not given but reasonable attempt (e.g. checking if tickets bigger than 0) allow FT for BP3/4. Any attempt at loop means giving BP5.\\n\\nBP6 must not repeatedly reset tickets to 500. BP6 must include repeated input (if present).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"input and store/use number of tickets needed","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if enough tickets left","marks":1},{"criteria":"sensible confirmation message output correctly for both booked and no tickets available","marks":1},{"criteria":"tickets variable updated correctly","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"repeat all above while tickets > 0 / != 0 / until <= 0","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"range-validation-if","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program asks a user to enter a number. Only integer values between 1 and 100 (inclusive) are accepted. The program is tested.\\n\\nAn algorithm is written for the program.\\n\\nComplete the algorithm for the program."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"num = input(\\"Enter a number between 1 and 100\\")\\nif num ____ AND ____ then\\n  print(\\"accepted\\")\\n____\\n  print(\\"not accepted\\")\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow check either way around (e.g. could check higher boundary first) for BP1 and BP2. BP2 must include reference to num variable.\\n\\nBP3 Do not allow elseif / elif unless full correct logical comparison included (e.g. elseif num < 1 or num > 100). Section A so allow \\"less than or equal to\\" etc.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":">= 1 // > 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"num <= 100 // num < 101","marks":1},{"criteria":"else","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"identify-variable","marks":1,"command_word":"Give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the identifier of one variable used in the algorithm in part (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"num - the only variable identifier used in the algorithm. Do not allow any other code around variable identifier. Ignore capitalisation. Ignore minor spelling errors.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"num","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(iii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"identify-boolean-operator","marks":1,"command_word":"Give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one Boolean operator used in the algorithm in part (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"AND - the Boolean operator joining the two comparisons. Do not allow any other code around operator. Ignore capitalisation. Ignore minor spelling errors.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"AND","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"flowchart-iteration-trace","marks":2,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This flowchart uses iteration.\\n\\nComplete the table by identifying how many times \\"OK\\" will be output for when each value is input."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/iteration-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Iteration flowchart that repeatedly outputs OK and subtracts 2 from x until x is less than 0."},{"type":"table","headers":["Input value","Number of times \\"OK\\" is output"],"rows":[["3",""],["10",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"This question involved following a flowchart to determine the outcome produced. For input value of 3, OK is output 2 times. For input value of 10, OK is output 6 times.\\n\\nMost students successfully determined this for input 3 but fewer for input 10, due to confusion over the use of the \'less than\' operator when x was set to zero.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"2 (for input 3)","marks":1},{"criteria":"6 (for input 10)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"data-types","marks":3,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Students take part in a sports day. The students are put into teams.\\n\\nStudents gain points depending on their result and the year group they are in. The points are added to the team score.\\n\\nThe team with the most points at the end of the sports day wins.\\n\\nData about the teams and students is stored in a sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify the most appropriate data type for each variable used by the program.\\n\\nEach data type must be different."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Example","Data type"],"rows":[["`teamName`","\\"Super-Team\\"",""],["`studentYearGroup`","11",""],["`javelinThrow`","18.2",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept alternative equivalent correct data types (e.g. single/double/decimal for BP3). Do not accept char for BP1.\\n\\nThis question was completed very well by the vast majority of candidates, showing that data types are now well understood.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"teamName: String","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentYearGroup: Integer","marks":1},{"criteria":"javelinThrow: Real / Float","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"linear-search-function","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student names for a team are stored in an array with the identifier `theTeam`. An example of the data in this array is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Data","Ali","Eve","Ling","Nina","Sarah","Tom"]]},{"type":"text","value":"A linear search function is used to find whether a student is in the team. The function:\\n- takes a student name as a parameter\\n- returns True if the student name is in the array\\n- returns False if the student name is not in the array.\\n\\nComplete the design of an algorithm for the linear search function."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"function linearSearch(studentName)\\n  for count = 0 to ____\\n    if theTeam[____] == ____ then\\n      return ____\\n    endif\\n  next count\\n  return False\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept 6 // theTeam.length() for BP1 (Python). Accept alternative length functions e.g. len(). Accept count = 5 (and equivalents) for BP1.\\n\\nAccept \\"True\\" for BP4. Do not allow obvious spaces in variable names. Ignore capitalisation.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"theTeam.length() - 1 // 5","marks":1},{"criteria":"count (as index)","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentName","marks":1},{"criteria":"True","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"trace-table-javelin-score","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm calculates the number of points a student gets for the distance they throw in the javelin:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 javelinThrow = input(\\"Enter distance\\")\\n02 yearGroup = input(\\"Enter year group\\")\\n03 if javelinThrow >= 20.0 then\\n04 score = 3\\n05 elseif javelinThrow >= 10.0 then\\n06 score = 2\\n07 else\\n08 score = 1\\n09 endif\\n10 if yearGroup != 11 then\\n11 score = score * 2\\n12 endif\\n13 print(\\"The score is\\", score)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm when a student in year 10 throws a distance of 14.3. You may not need to use all the rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","javelinThrow","yearGroup","score","Output"],"rows":[["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if in wrong order or additional (incorrect) changes. Penalise line numbers once then FT.\\n\\nAllow FT for BP4 for current value of score. BP4 must not include comma. Ignore superfluous spaces. Ignore quotation marks. Treat any entry in output column as an output, even if \\"x\\", \\"-\\" or \\"0\\".\\n\\nAnswer may include lines where no changes or output happens (i.e. lines 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12). Where variable doesn\'t change, current value may be repeated on subsequent lines.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"javelinThrow set to 14.3 on line 01 and yearGroup set to 10 on line 02","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 2 on line 06","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 4 on line 11","marks":1},{"criteria":"\\"The score is 4\\" output on line 13 with no additional outputs (allow input statements)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"height-validation-algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated.\\n\\nThe height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \\"VALID\\" or \\"NOT VALID\\" depending on the height input.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (checking for valid input):\\n\\n```\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nExample 2 (checking for invalid input):\\n\\n```\\nh = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nAnswers using AND/OR for BP2 and BP3 must be logically correct. Do not accept if height >=40 and <=180. Answers using OR will reverse output for BP4. BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3. Ignore conversion to int on input. input cannot be used as a variable name. Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for a high-level language / ERL.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 // < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 // > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(c)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"test-data-plan","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests.\\n\\nComplete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow FT for BP4 if reasonable attempt at validating (must include at least one boundary). No need to include decimals, e.g. accept 50. Ignore cm if given.\\n\\nAnswer must be actual data (e.g. 50) and not description of data. If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for BP3.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: Any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"sql-where-clause","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database.\\n\\nThe database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n________________"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1.\\n\\nSpelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"abstraction-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design) that is applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is.\\n\\nExamples: \\"focus on student names and events\\"; \\"ignore data such as students\' favourite subjects\\"; \\"store year groups instead of ages or DOB\\"; \\"shows student IDs instead of full student details\\".","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data in context of sports day","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"decomposition-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables. Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description.\\n\\nExamples: \\"splits the program up into different events\\"; \\"separates the validation routines into subroutines\\"; \\"breaks the database down into a table per event\\". Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking the program into sections/subroutines OR database into tables (in context)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(f)","parent_question":5,"topic":"highest-score-iteration","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score).\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example 1:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"highscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)"},{"type":"list","items":["For BP3, allow \\"stop\\" for either team name or score (or both).","Allow use of break to exit loop.","Allow recursive function(s) for BP2 and BP3.","Initialisation of variables is not needed.","Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score; assume only one team has the highest score.","BP4 and BP5 could be done in many ways. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists.","FT for BP6 if an attempt is made at calculating highest score/name.","If an answer simply asks for multiple entries without iteration, BP2 and BP3 cannot be accessed, but all other marks remain available.","`input` cannot be used as a variable name."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store/use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"...to enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"flowchart-odd-even","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","response_style":"annotate-image","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm decides if a number is odd or even.\\n\\nAn odd number divided by 2 will give the remainder 1.\\n\\nThe flowchart statements have been written for the algorithm, but the flowchart is incomplete.\\n\\nComplete the flowchart."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/topics/ocr/2.1.2_q6_odd_even_flowchart.png","alt_text":"Incomplete flowchart for the odd or even number algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"No need for arrows – lines are acceptable. BOD for correct answers that include a loop back to the start.\\n\\nThe majority of candidates recognised the correct flowchart shapes for a decision and many used the parallelogram shape for inputs and outputs. Fewer correctly connected the boxes so all paths started and ended at appropriate points.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct shape for all three inputs AND outputs (parallelogram)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct shape for decision (diamond)","marks":1},{"criteria":"True and False // Yes and No labelled correctly (True/Yes linking to \\"Even\\")","marks":1},{"criteria":"All lines joined up correctly and link to End","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_question":7,"topic":"identify-inputs-outputs","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program allows users to search for and watch videos. Users give a rating to the videos they watch.\\n\\nIdentify one input and one output for the program."},{"type":"text","value":"Input ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Output ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a suitable input, 1 mark for a suitable output. Allow input / print pseudocode statements if meets mark point(s). Does not have to be valid pseudocode. Do not allow examples of inputs (e.g. \\"music videos\\").\\n\\nInput e.g.: Name/keyword for video (to be searched for) // search text; Controls for watching video (e.g. play/pause); Rating given to video.\\n\\nOutput e.g.: Video to be watched // audio; Results of search; (total/overall/average) rating of video; Number of views (of video); Confirmation of data entry / data validity; Messages to user (e.g. \\"enter a rating\\", \\"your rating has been saved\\") in quotation marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Suitable input (e.g. search keyword, controls, rating)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Suitable output (e.g. video/audio, search results, rating, message)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8","parent_question":8,"topic":"refine-search-count","marks":4,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An array stores a collection of words. The array has the identifier `data`."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"data = [\\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\", \\"when\\", \\"and\\", \\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"A searching algorithm is used to find a word in the array `data`. The algorithm used to search for a word is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"word = input(\\"Enter a word to find\\")\\nfound = False\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == word then\\n    found = True\\n  endif\\nnext i\\nprint(found)"},{"type":"text","value":"Some words appear in the array more than once.\\n\\nRefine the algorithm to:\\n- count the number of times a word appears in the array\\n- output this number.\\n\\nFor example, the algorithm should output 3 if the input is \\"and\\"."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example answer:\\n\\n```\\nsearch = input(\\"Enter a word\\")\\ncount = 0\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == search then\\n    count = count + 1\\nnext i\\nprint(count)\\n```\\n\\nAllow answe