 07","marks":1},{"criteria":"HK900 output on line 08","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(a)","parent_question":10,"topic":"pseudocode-sum","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The variables num1 and num2 store integers.\\n\\nWrite pseudocode to add the integers stored in num1 and num2. Store the result in a variable with the identifier total."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other logically valid responses that result in total storing the correct value. Accept other suitable assignment operators.\\n\\nExamples: total = sum(num1, num2); total = num2 + num1; x = num1 + num2 then total = x. Ignore any values given to the variable. Ignore capitalisation and minor misspelling.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"total = num1 + num2 (or logically valid equivalent)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(i)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator-power","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Three incomplete pseudocode algorithms are given with a description of the purpose of each algorithm.\\n\\nWrite the missing arithmetic operator for each algorithm.\\n\\nOutputting 12 to the power of 2."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"print(12 ____ 2)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept ** or other sensible operator that indicates raising to a power. If pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. pow), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"^ (or ** / pow etc)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(ii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Working out if a number is odd or even."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"number = 53\\nif number ____ 2 == 0 then\\n  print(\\"Even number\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"Odd number\\")\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept % or other sensible operator that indicates modulus. If pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. modulo), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"MOD (or % / modulo)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(iii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"arithmetic-operator-subtraction","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding the difference between two measurements."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"measurement1 = 300\\nmeasurement2 = 100\\ndifference = measurement1 ____ measurement2"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept other sensible operator that indicates subtraction. If a pseudocode operator given, must be a single word/symbol (e.g. minus), not containing spaces.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"- (minus)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(c)","parent_question":10,"topic":"trace-table-do-until","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Read the following pseudocode algorithm:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 start = 3\\n02 do\\n03 print(start)\\n04 start = start - 1\\n05 until start == -1\\n06 print(\\"Finished\\")"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following trace table for the given algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","start","Output"],"rows":[["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore lines 02 and 05 in answer unless these change or output any values. Candidate may repeat start value when unchanged, this is acceptable.\\n\\nPenalise incorrect or missing line numbers or additional output once only then FT. -1 must not be output for BP2. Penalise missing or incorrect output once only for BP1 and FT for missing or incorrect output for BP2.\\n\\nFinished may be with or without quotes. Ignore case or minor spelling error. Max 2 marks if any incorrect output or changes to start. Do not accept calculated values of start (e.g. 3-1).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Start is set to 3 on line 01 and 3 is output on line 03","marks":1},{"criteria":"2, 1 and 0 are output on next 3 iterations with start updated to 2, 1, 0, -1 on correct line numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"Finished is output on line 06","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11","parent_question":11,"topic":"increment-variable","marks":1,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write pseudocode to increment a value held in a variable score by one."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other logically correct answers that result in score increasing by one and being overwritten. Do not accept score + 1 / score = +1.\\n\\nAccept valid structured English answers that refer to score increasing and overwriting the existing value by one. e.g. \\"score becomes/equals score plus one\\". Ignore any superfluous code that does not affect the outcome.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"score = score + 1 // score += 1 // score++","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12","parent_question":12,"topic":"total-and-average-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Jack is writing a program to add up some numbers. His first attempt at the program is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"a = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nb = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nc = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nd = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\ne = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\nf = (a + b + c + d + e)\\nprint(f)"},{"type":"text","value":"Jack decides to improve his program. He wants to be able to input how many numbers to add together each time the algorithm runs, and also wants it to calculate and display the average of these numbers.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- ask the user to input the quantity of numbers they want to enter and read this value as input\\n- repeatedly take a number as input, until the quantity of numbers the user input has been entered\\n- calculate and output the total of these numbers\\n- calculate and output the average of these numbers."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nnum = input(\\"Enter how many numbers\\")\\nfor x = 1 to num\\n  temp = input(\\"Enter a number\\")\\n  total = total + temp\\nnext x\\nprint(total)\\nprint(total / num)\\n```\\n\\nIf flow chart used, correct shapes needed. Allow tolerance of 1 with number of loops for BP3 with for loops. BP1 requires input with a message (can be two statements, e.g. print and then input or combined). Input must be stored or used.\\n\\nBP3, 4, 5 must be logically correct to be credited. Ignore non-initialisation of total. BP5 can be given as FT as long as an attempt has been made at working out a total within the loop. BP6 can be given as FT long as attempt made at total and average (not necessarily in a loop).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"input and stores/uses value with message","marks":1},{"criteria":"attempt at repeating","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly repeats number of times given as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly take number as input within loop and calculates total of these numbers","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly calculate an average (total/num)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output both total and average","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(a)(i)","parent_question":13,"topic":"hotel-booking-validation","marks":5,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Customers at a hotel can stay between 1 and 5 (inclusive) nights and can choose between a basic room or a premium room.\\n\\nWhen a new booking is recorded, the details are entered into a program to validate the values. The following criteria are checked:\\n- firstName and surname are not empty\\n- room is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"\\n- nights is between 1 and 5 (inclusive).\\n\\nIf any invalid data is found \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\nIf all data is valid \\"ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\n\\nComplete the following program to validate the inputs.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"firstName = input(\\"Enter a first name\\")\\nsurname = input(\\"Enter a surname\\")\\nroom = input(\\"Enter basic or premium\\")\\nnights = input(\\"Enter between 1 and 5 nights\\")\\nstayComplete = False"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nvalid = True\\nif firstname == \\"\\" or surname == \\"\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nend if\\nif room != \\"basic\\" and room != \\"premium\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif nights < 1 or nights > 5 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif valid then\\n  print(\\"ALLOWED\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"NOT ALLOWED\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nMust have some attempt at all three checks to give output mark(s). Check for nights must check both upper and lower limits. Iteration can be used as validation if input repeatedly asked for until valid answer given.\\n\\nDo not accept logically incorrect Boolean conditions such as \'if firstname or surname == \\"\\"\'. BP1 to 3 can check for valid or invalid inputs. Pay particular attention to use of AND / OR. Only give marks for output if these work together correctly.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks that both firstname and surname are not empty","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that room is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that nights is between 1 and 5 (inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) if any of the 3 checks are invalid","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) only if all three checks are valid","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(a)(ii)","parent_question":13,"topic":"test-plan-nights","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan to check whether the number of nights is validated correctly."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (number of nights)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["2","","ALLOWED"],["","Boundary","ALLOWED"],["","Erroneous / Invalid","NOT ALLOWED"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other descriptions that mean normal (e.g. valid / typical / acceptable).\\n\\nTest data 2: Normal, ALLOWED.\\nTest data 1 // 5: Boundary, ALLOWED (not 0 or 6 as says allowed).\\nTest data e.g. 7: Erroneous/Invalid, NOT ALLOWED (any numeric value except 1 to 5 // any non-numeric input e.g. \\"bananas\\").","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal (type for test data 2)","marks":1},{"criteria":"1 or 5 (boundary test data, not 0 or 6)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Any numeric value except 1 to 5 // non-numeric (erroneous test data)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(b)(i)","parent_question":13,"topic":"newprice-function","marks":4,"command_word":"Create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A Basic room costs £60 each night. A Premium room costs £80 each night.\\n\\nCreate a function, `newPrice()`, that takes the number of nights and the type of room as parameters, calculates and returns the price to pay.\\n\\nYou do not have to validate these parameters.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nfunction newPrice(nights, room)\\n  if room == \\"basic\\" then\\n    price = 60 * nights\\n  elseif room == \\"premium\\" then\\n    price = 80 * nights\\n  endif\\n  return price\\nendfunction\\n```\\n\\nBP1 must be clear that a new function is being defined. E.g. function / def keyword. Allow FT for subsequent marks if not present. Ignore any code outside attempt at function definition. Ignore additional parameters. Ignore inputs or additional code as long as these do not overwrite parameters. If inputs used instead of parameters, FT for BP3.\\n\\nAllow use of else for second room type in BP3. Must return (not output) value. Return can be done e.g. in VB by assigning to function name (e.g. newPrice = price).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function header for newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"...taking (at least) two parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates price based on parameters within function","marks":1},{"criteria":"...returns this calculated price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(b)(ii)","parent_question":13,"topic":"newprice-function-call","marks":3,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write program code, that uses `newPrice()`, to output the price of staying in a Premium room for 5 nights.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nprint(newPrice(\\"premium\\", 5))\\n```\\n\\nor\\n\\n```\\nx = newPrice(5, \\"premium\\")\\nprint(x)\\n```\\n\\nOrder of parameters not important. \\"premium\\" must use string delimiters (e.g. quotes). Do not allow function definitions for BP1. Ignore capitalisation of newPrice.\\n\\nCandidate could store returned value in a variable and then print this, or store parameters in variables before passing in. Ignore any superfluous code given. Do not credit answers where newPrice is overwritten prior to use. Ignore spaces. Allow function call if brackets missing.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Call function newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"...with (\\"premium\\", 5) as parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output returned value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(c)","parent_question":13,"topic":"refine-logic-errors","marks":2,"command_word":"Describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hotel has nine rooms that are numbered from room 0 to room 8.\\n\\nThe number of people currently staying in each room is stored in an array with the identifier room.\\n\\nThe index of room represents the room number."},{"type":"text","value":"Array `room`"},{"type":"table","headers":["Index","0","1","2","3","4","5","6","7","8"],"rows":[["Data","2","1","3","2","1","0","0","4","1"]]},{"type":"text","value":"The following program counts how many people are currently staying in the hotel."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"for count = 1 to 8\\n  total = 0\\n  total = total + room[count]\\nnext count\\nprint(total)"},{"type":"text","value":"When tested, the program is found to contain two logic errors.\\n\\nDescribe how the program can be refined to remove these logic errors."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow loop changed to 0 to 8 or 0 to 9 (Python). Do not accept moving total outside loop if moved to after loop (would still be a logic error). Do not accept move to top of loop.\\n\\nAccept corrected code shown. Accept reference to count variable limits for BP1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"For loop changed to include 0 (e.g. 0 to 8)","marks":1},{"criteria":"total = 0 moved to before loop starts / removed","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(d)","parent_question":13,"topic":"car-park-pricing-loop","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hotel car park charges £4 per hour. If the car is electric, this price is halved to £2 per hour.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- take as input the number of hours the user has parked and whether their car is electric or not\\n- calculate and output the total price\\n- repeat continually until the user enters 0 hours.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nwhile hours != 0\\n  hours = input(\\"Enter hours\\")\\n  electric = input(\\"enter Y for electric or N\\")\\n  if electric == \\"Y\\" then\\n    price = hours * 2\\n  elseif electric == \\"N\\" then\\n    price = hours * 4\\n  endif\\n  print(price)\\nendwhile\\n```\\n\\nInitialisation of price and hours not necessary, but if present hours must be non-zero for BP6 to be given. BP5 must include all points attempted.\\n\\nBP6 can be given as FT even if BP5 (loop) is in the wrong place. BP6 could be achieved as repeated function calls / recursion.\\n\\nInitial input outside of loop that is then also included within loop is fine. Do not accept while hours > 0 (could be -1). Do not penalise answers where 0 is output when loop exits.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs hours AND electric (two separate inputs), storing or using these","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if car is electric (IF/Select statement)","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 2 // price / 2) for electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"...correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 4 // electric price * 2) for non-electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at repetition of above","marks":1},{"criteria":"...until 0 hours entered","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(a)","parent_question":14,"topic":"truth-table-nand","marks":2,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the truth table in Fig. 1 for the Boolean statement P = NOT(A AND B)."},{"type":"table","headers":["A","B","P"],"rows":[["0","0","1"],["0","1","____"],["1","0","____"],["1","1","0"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each correct answer in table. \'True\' or \'T\' are also credit worthy.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"P = 1 for A=0, B=1","marks":1},{"criteria":"P = 1 for A=1, B=0","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(b)","parent_question":14,"topic":"logic-diagram-nand","marks":1,"command_word":"Tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box to identify the correct logic diagram for P = NOT(A AND B).\\n\\nP = NOT(A AND B)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Logic diagram","Tick one box"],"rows":[[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/a.png","alt_text":"Option a logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/b.png","alt_text":"Option b logic-gate diagram."},""],[{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/yearpapers/specimen/1b/c.png","alt_text":"Option c logic-gate diagram."},""]]}],"answer":{"auto_mark":{"type":"single_tick_table","correct_key":"1:1"},"guidance":"Correct Answer Only. The correct logic diagram is the NAND gate equivalent (AND followed by NOT).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct logic diagram identified (NAND / AND-NOT)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(a)","parent_question":15,"topic":"pseudocode-max-of-two","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n- Input two numbers from the user\\n- Compare both numbers and output the largest number.\\n\\nComplete the pseudocode for this program."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"num1 = input(\\"enter first number\\")\\nnum2 = input(\\"enter second number\\")\\n____ num1 > ____ then\\n  ____\\nelse\\n  ____\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow equivalent pseudocode expressions. Variables must not have speech marks around them. Correct completed code is:\\n\\n```\\nif num1 > num2 then\\n  print(num1)\\nelse\\n  print(num2)\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nEach blank is position-locked and must be marked against its intended position only: blank 1 (before `num1 >`) must be `if`; blank 2 (after `num1 >`) must be `num2`; blank 3 (inside the if branch) must be `print(num1)`; blank 4 (inside the else branch) must be `print(num2)`. Do not award a mark for a correct item placed in the wrong blank.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Blank 1 (before `num1 >`): if","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 2 (after `num1 >`): num2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 3 (if branch): print(num1)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Blank 4 (else branch): print(num2)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(b)","parent_question":15,"topic":"doubling-loop-algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A second program needs to perform the following tasks:\\n- Input a number from the user\\n- Double the number input and print the result\\n- Repeat bullets 1 and 2 until the user enters a number less than 0.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm for this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example 1 (structured English):\\n\\n```\\nstore 10 in number\\nwhile number is greater than or equal to 0\\ndo the following:\\n  Take input from the user, store in number\\n  Multiply number by 2\\n  Output number\\n```\\n\\nExample 2 (pseudocode):\\n\\n```\\nwhile number >= 0\\n  number = input()\\n  output(number * 2)\\n```\\n\\nIgnore non-initialisation of value used in condition for loop.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"use of condition controlled loop (while or do/until)","marks":1},{"criteria":"checking condition of number larger than or equal to 0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Input number from user within loop (FT if no loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"multiply number input by 2","marks":1},{"criteria":"output value in number","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"16","parent_question":16,"topic":"complex-boolean-cinema","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A cinema uses the following criteria to decide if a customer is allowed to see a film that has a 15 rating:\\n\\nCustomers have to be 15 years of age or older to see the film. They also need to either have a ticket or have the money to buy a ticket.\\n\\nThe table shows the inputs to the system that will output whether the customer can watch the film."},{"type":"table","headers":["Input","Criteria (True / False)"],"rows":[["A","The customer is 15 or over"],["B","The customer has a ticket"],["C","The customer has the money to buy a ticket"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following algorithm to output whether the customer is allowed to see the film or not."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"A = input(\\"Is the customer 15 or over?\\")\\nB = input(\\"Does the customer have a ticket?\\")\\nC = input(\\"Does the customer have money to buy a ticket?\\")"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nif A AND (B OR C) then\\n  print(\\"allowed\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not allowed\\")\\nendif\\n```\\n\\nAccept answers where inputs are given as strings e.g.:\\nif A == \\"Yes\\" AND (B == \\"Yes\\" OR C == \\"Yes\\") then...","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Logically compares A AND // correct nested IF","marks":1},{"criteria":"...B OR C // correct sequential IF","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output in both cases (with attempt at selection)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(i)","parent_question":17,"topic":"car-validation-algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven.\\n\\nThe car dealership only sells cars that have fewer than 10 000 miles and are 5 years old or less.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm that will:\\n- ask the user to enter the number of miles and the age of a car\\n- validate the input to check that only sensible values that are in the given range are entered\\n- output True if valid data has been entered or False if invalid data has been entered."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\n\\n```\\nmiles = input(\\"enter miles driven\\")\\nage = input(\\"enter age of car\\")\\nvalid = True\\nif miles > 10000 or miles < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nelseif age > 5 or age < 0 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nprint(valid)\\n```\\n\\nBP2 and 3 must check for both ends of range – must check that input data is not negative. Allow FT for BP4 if already penalised under BP2 and/or 3 and output is otherwise correct.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Miles and age input separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid mileage","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid age","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks both are greater than / greater than equal to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"correctly outputs both True and False","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(ii)","parent_question":17,"topic":"car-validation-test-data","marks":3,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The validation routine from part (i) must be tested with normal, erroneous and boundary test data.\\n\\nIdentify suitable test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["","Miles","Age"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Erroneous","",""],["Boundary","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Specific data must be given, not a description.\\n\\nNormal: miles (0 – 9,999), age (0 - 5). e.g. Normal 7,000 / 3.\\nErroneous: miles (less than 0, larger than 9,999), age (less than 0 / more than 5) // non-numeric data. e.g. Erroneous 12,000 / 7.\\nBoundary: miles (-1/0 / 9,999 / 10,000), age (-1/0 / 5/6). e.g. Boundary 10,000 / 5.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: miles 0-9999, age 0-5","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: miles out of range, age out of range or non-numeric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: miles -1/0/9999/10000, age -1/0/5/6","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)(iii)","parent_question":17,"topic":"iterative-testing","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify when iterative testing is performed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Iterative testing is performed during the development process while writing the program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"During development // whilst writing the program // before development is complete","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(b)","parent_question":17,"topic":"battery-charge-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership sells electric cars, which require charging before they can be driven. Charging the battery by 1% takes 10 minutes.\\n\\nFor example, a battery has 80% charge. It would take 200 minutes, or 3 hours and 20 minutes to charge to 100%.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm that:\\n- asks the user for the current battery charge percentage\\n- outputs \\"full\\" for a battery currently at 100%\\n- calculates how long this battery would take to charge\\n- outputs this in hours and minutes."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\ncharge = input(\\"enter battery charge\\")\\nif charge == 100 then\\n  print(\\"full\\")\\nelse\\n  time = (100-charge) * 10\\n  hours = time DIV 60\\n  mins = time MOD 60\\n  print(hours, mins)\\nendif\\n\\nAllow output of 0 hours 0 minutes if full. Allow answers referencing decimal parts (e.g. 0.8 = 80%). BP5 can be attempted in many ways (e.g. DIV and MOD, repeated division, etc). Allow FT for BP6 if reasonable attempt at conversion for BP5 has been given.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs the current battery charge percentage","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"full\\" if 100%","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the amount to charge","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the time in minutes","marks":1},{"criteria":"converts to hours and minutes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs the time in hours and minutes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(a)(i)","parent_question":18,"topic":"sale-price-test-plan","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Tech is an online shop that sells electronics such as TVs and game consoles.\\n\\nThe following flowchart shows an algorithm to calculate the price of an item during a sale period."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/sale-price-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Sale-price flowchart that reduces prices over 200 by 40 and prices over 100 by 20 before output."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Price input","Test type","Expected price output"],"rows":[["50","Normal",""],["100","Boundary",""],["150","Normal",""],["200","Boundary",""],["250","Normal",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark if two correct, two marks if four correct, three marks if all correct.\\n\\nExpected outputs:\\n50 Normal -> 50\\n100 Boundary -> 100\\n150 Normal -> 130\\n200 Boundary -> 180\\n250 Normal -> 210","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Two correct outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"Four correct outputs","marks":1},{"criteria":"All five correct outputs (50, 100, 130, 180, 210)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(a)(ii)","parent_question":18,"topic":"sale-price-rewrite","marks":6,"command_word":"Rewrite","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewrite the algorithm above.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nprice = input(\\"enter price\\")\\nif price > 200 then\\n  price = price - 40\\nelseif price > 100 then\\n  price = price - 20\\nendif\\nprint(price)\\n\\nHigh-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural language. BP3 and BP5 only to be given if sensible check for price being over the appropriate threshold. BP4 must check that price is both larger than 100 and not larger than 200; do not give mark for simply checking price is larger than 100. This may be implicit (e.g. using elseif).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and store price","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 200","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if true, reduce price by 40","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if price is > 100 and not > 200","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if true, reduce price by 20","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"18(b)","parent_question":18,"topic":"refine-stock-program","marks":5,"command_word":"Refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An item is classified as \\"In demand\\" if OCR Tech have between 5 and 25 inclusive in stock.\\n\\nA program is written that allows the user to input the current stock level and output whether the item is in demand or not."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"stocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel >= 5 or =< 25 then\\n  print(Not in demand)\\nelse\\n  print(In demand)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"The program contains syntax and logic errors.\\n\\nRefine the program to correct the errors and write the refined version of the program.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nstocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel >= 5 and stocklevel <= 25 then\\n  print(\\"In demand\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"Not in demand\\")\\nendif\\n\\nAlternative:\\nstocklevel = input(\\"Enter stock level\\")\\nif stocklevel < 5 or stocklevel > 25 then\\n  print(\\"Not in demand\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"In demand\\")\\nendif\\n\\nHigh-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural language. As a matter of principle, a candidate who refines the program to work fully but in a different format should gain full marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"checking both values (e.g. or changed to and if appropriate)","marks":1},{"criteria":"if statement in correct format (e.g. checking against stocklevel for each condition)","marks":1},{"criteria":"if statement uses correct comparisons (e.g. >= and <=)","marks":1},{"criteria":"print statements in correct position","marks":1},{"criteria":"print statements include string delimiters around both string outputs","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"19(a)","parent_question":19,"topic":"structure-diagram","marks":4,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school uses a mobile phone app to allow parents to book appointments for parents\' evenings.\\n\\nParents must log in before they can use the system. They then choose to book a new appointment, view all appointments already made or update their personal details. If parents choose to view their appointments, they can either view them on-screen or print them off.\\n\\nA structure diagram has been used to design the mobile phone app.\\n\\nWrite one letter from the following table in each space to complete the structure diagram."},{"type":"table","headers":["Letter","Task"],"rows":[["A","Book new appointment"],["B","Check attendance of child"],["C","Update personal details"],["D","View appointments on-screen"],["E","Log out of the system"],["F","Print a paper copy of appointments"]]},{"type":"structure-diagram","title":"Structure diagram for the mobile phone app","root":{"id":"root","label":"Mobile phone app","children":[{"id":"login","label":"Login","children":[]},{"id":"manage","label":"Manage appointments","children":[{"id":"book","label":"","answerSlot":{"id":"box-a","correctAnswer":"A","placeholder":"?"}},{"id":"view","label":"View appointments","children":[{"id":"view-screen","label":"","answerSlot":{"id":"box-d","correctAnswer":"D","markSchemeAnswer":"D / F","placeholder":"?"}},{"id":"print","label":"","answerSlot":{"id":"box-f","correctAnswer":"F","markSchemeAnswer":"F / D","placeholder":"?"}}]}]},{"id":"update","label":"","answerSlot":{"id":"box-c","correctAnswer":"C","placeholder":"?"}}]}}],"answer":{"guidance":"D, F may be swapped around. Expected order: C, A, D/F, F/D.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"C (Update personal details)","marks":1},{"criteria":"A (Book new appointment)","marks":1},{"criteria":"D (View appointments on-screen)","marks":1},{"criteria":"F (Print paper copy of appointments)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"19(b)(i)","parent_question":19,"topic":"define-logic-error","marks":1,"command_word":"Define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"At the parents\' evening, each parent can book up to five appointments with teachers.\\n\\nAppointments for one student are stored in a one-dimensional array with the identifier appointments.\\n\\nIn the array, each element is either the name of a teacher or an empty string where no appointment has been made.\\n\\nAn example for one student is shown:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"array appointments = [\\"Miss E\\", \\"\\", \\"Mr C\\", \\"Mr B\\", \\"\\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"The following code shows an algorithm to count up how many empty slots remain in the array and output this value."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 for i = 0 to 4\\n02  empty = 0\\n03  if appointments[i] == \\"\\" then\\n04   empty = empty + 1\\n05  endif\\n06 next i\\n07 print(\\"empty\\")"},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm contains logic errors.\\n\\nDefine the term logic error."}],"answer":{"guidance":"A logic error is an error that does not cause the program to crash but produces unexpected output.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"An error that does not cause the program to crash // produces unexpected output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"19(b)(ii)","parent_question":19,"topic":"identify-logic-errors","marks":4,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the line number of two logic errors in the code above and explain why each is an error."},{"type":"text","value":"Logic error 1"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Explanation ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Logic error 2"},{"type":"text","value":"Line number ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Explanation ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Mark in pairs.\\n\\nError 1: Line 02 // empty = 0. Explanation: Will reset empty to 0 on each iteration of the loop.\\n\\nError 2: Line 07 // print(\\"empty\\"). Explanation: Will print out the string \\"empty\\" instead of the value held in the variable.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 02 // empty = 0 identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Will reset empty to 0 on each iteration of the loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Line 07 // print(\\"empty\\") identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Will print out the string \\"empty\\" instead of the value held in the variable","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(a)(i)","parent_question":20,"topic":"invalid-input-validation","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The program should only allow values from 0 to 300 inclusive as valid inputs. If the data entered breaks this validation rule, an error message is displayed.\\n\\nComplete the following program to output \\"Invalid input\\" if the data does not meet the validation rule.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"mins = input(\\"Enter minutes played: \\")\\nif mins < 0 ____ mins ____ then\\n  ____ (\\"Invalid input\\")\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"High-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural English. MP2 do not accept \'greater than\', must use the HLL syntax > or >=. MP3 must be a suitable output command word that could be found in a HLL e.g. print (Python), console.writeline (VB), cout (C++).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"or","marks":1},{"criteria":"> 300 // >= 301","marks":1},{"criteria":"print (or equivalent HLL output)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(a)(ii)","parent_question":20,"topic":"test-plan-boundary","marks":3,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the program in (i)."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data","Test type","Expected result"],"rows":[["25","Normal","Value accepted"],["","Invalid","Invalid input message displayed"],["","Boundary",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Mark test data first, both must meet different criteria. Then mark output for each.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Suitable invalid test data (i.e. > 300, e.g. 350)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Suitable boundary test data (e.g. 0, 300)","marks":1},{"criteria":"\\"Value accepted\\" or equivalent if boundary data 0 or 300 // \\"Invalid input displayed\\" or equivalent if boundary data -1 or 301","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(b)","parent_question":20,"topic":"trace-table-while-condition","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following program uses a condition-controlled loop."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"x = 15\\ny = 0\\nwhile x > 0\\n  y = y + 1\\n  x = x - y\\nendwhile\\nprint(y)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table to test this program."},{"type":"table","headers":["x","y","output"],"rows":[["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""],["","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"One mark for first row, one mark for rows 2 and 3, one mark for rows 4, 5 and 6, one mark for the correct output (the only value in the output column, in any position).\\n\\nTrace: x=15, y=0; y=1, x=14; y=2, x=12; y=3, x=9; y=4, x=5; y=5, x=0; output 5.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First row correct (x=15, y=0)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Rows 2 and 3 correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"Rows 4, 5, 6 correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output value (5)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(c)","parent_question":20,"topic":"test-data-if-else","marks":4,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A teacher writes an algorithm to store the name of the game a student plays each night (for example \\"OCR Zoo Simulator\\").\\n\\n`variable.length` returns the number of characters in variable.\\n`variable.upper` returns the characters in variable in upper case."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"valid = false\\nwhile(valid == false)\\n  gameName = input(\\"Enter the game name\\")\\n  if (gameName.length > 0) AND (gameName.length < 20)\\n    gamesPlayed = gameName.upper\\n    valid = true\\n    print(\\"Valid game name\\")\\n  else\\n    print(\\"Game name is not valid\\")\\n  endif\\nendwhile"},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm needs testing to make sure the IF-ELSE statement works correctly.\\n\\nIdentify two different pieces of test data that can be used to test different outputs of the algorithm. Give the output from the program for each piece of test data."},{"type":"text","value":"Test data 1 ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Expected output ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Test data 2 ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Expected output ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Mark test data first, both must meet different criteria. Then mark output for each.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Test data either 0 or less characters, or 20 or more characters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Stating correct output (\\"Game name is not valid\\")","marks":1},{"criteria":"Test data between 1 and 19 characters (inc)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Stating correct output (\\"Valid game name\\")","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(d)(i)","parent_question":20,"topic":"identify-input-output","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A teacher researches the length of time students spend playing computer games each day.\\n\\nThe teacher asks students how long they spend completing homework. Students answer in minutes and hours (for example 2 hours 15 minutes).\\n\\nThe teacher would like to create an algorithm that will display students\' inputs in minutes only.\\n\\nIdentify the input and output required from this algorithm."},{"type":"text","value":"Input ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Output ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Input: Number of hours and minutes. Output: Number of minutes.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input: Number of hours and minutes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output: Number of minutes","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(d)(ii)","parent_question":20,"topic":"function-with-parameters","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program is created to convert hours and minutes into a total number of minutes.\\n\\nThe teacher wants to create a sub program to perform the calculation.\\n\\nThe program has been started but is not complete.\\n\\nComplete the design for the program."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"hours = input(\\"Please enter number of hours played\\")\\nminutes = input(\\"Please enter number of minutes played\\")\\nfinalTotal = ____\\nprint(finalTotal)\\nfunction ____\\n  ____\\n  ____\\n  ____\\n  ____\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nhours = input(\\"Please enter number of hours played\\")\\nminutes = input(\\"Please enter number of minutes played\\")\\nfinalTotal = totalMins(hours, minutes)\\nprint(finalTotal)\\nfunction totalMins(hours, minutes)\\n  total = hours * 60 + minutes\\n  return total\\nendfunction\\n\\n1. Parameters named in function must be used within the function itself.\\n2. Does not matter if function uses different names to those declared in main program.\\n3. Return must be included with the correct local variable for total.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Program calls function correctly using hours and minutes variables","marks":1},{"criteria":"Parameters used appropriately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculation is computed accurately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Final total is returned suitably","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"20(d)(iii)","parent_question":20,"topic":"flowchart-to-program","marks":4,"command_word":"Rewrite","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following flowchart outputs a message depending on how long each person has spent playing computer games."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/games-time-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Flowchart that outputs one of two messages depending on whether minutes played is greater than 120."},{"type":"text","value":"Rewrite the flowchart as a program.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nminutes = input(\\"Enter minutes played\\")\\nif minutes > 120\\n  print \\"You played games for too long!\\"\\nelse\\n  print \\"You are under your time limit!\\"\\nendif\\n\\nHigh-level programming language / OCR Exam Reference Language response required. Do not accept pseudocode / natural English. Accept alternative (but suitable) output messages. Accept logical comparison of input less than or equal to 120 and appropriate True/False statements.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Takes input from the user","marks":1},{"criteria":"Compares if input is larger than 120","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if true, outputs \\"You played games for too long!\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"...if false, outputs \\"You are under your time limit!\\"","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"21(a)","parent_question":21,"topic":"file-handling-algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm is written to read numbers from the external text file data.txt\\n\\nEach number is checked when it is read from the external text file. The number is output to the user if it is not 0.\\n\\nComplete the algorithm using the OCR Exam Reference Language statements in the box.\\n\\nNot all statements are used."},{"type":"text","value":"Terms: data, for, if, myFile.close(), myFile.readLine(), input(), myFile.writeLine(), open, print, temp, txt, x"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"myFile = ____ (\\"data.txt\\")\\nwhile NOT myFile.endOfFile()\\n  temp = ____\\n  if int (____) != 0\\n    ____(temp)\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept readLine for BP2 / close for BP5. Ignore spelling errors / case / minor differences as long as clear which option has been selected. Accept MyFile.readLine() as 3rd missing option (instead of temp) only if not used on line above. Only allow one choice per gap. Mark incorrect if multiple options chosen.\\n\\nCompleted algorithm:\\nmyFile = open(\\"data.txt\\")\\nwhile NOT myFile.endOfFile()\\n  temp = myFile.readLine()\\n  if int(temp) != 0\\n    print(temp)\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nmyFile.close()","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"open","marks":1},{"criteria":"myFile.readLine()","marks":1},{"criteria":"temp","marks":1},{"criteria":"print","marks":1},{"criteria":"myFile.close()","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"21(b)","parent_question":21,"topic":"identify-casting","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify how casting is used in this algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not allow generic \\"change to different data type\\". Casting converts one data type to another data type. In this algorithm, a value (temp) is converted to an integer (on the 4th line).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Convert data to an integer / int (in context)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"22(a)","parent_question":22,"topic":"flowchart-design","marks":5,"command_word":"Design","response_style":"drawing","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A fast food restaurant offers half-price meals if the customer is a student or has a discount card. The offer is not valid on Saturdays.\\n\\nThe restaurant needs an algorithm designing to help employees work out if a customer can have a half price meal or not. It should:\\n- input required data\\n- decide if the customer is entitled to a discount\\n- output the result of the calculation.\\n\\nDesign the algorithm using a flowchart."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Question asks for a flowchart. Answers as pseudocode, high level language or other forms are not acceptable (NAQ).\\n\\nGuidance for correct outputs:\\n- Not Saturday and (either a student or has a discount card) -> Half price\\n- Saturday or (not a student and doesn\'t have a discount card) -> Full price\\n\\nBP 4 and 5 only to be awarded if all decisions ensure correct output and clear what the decisions are. FT for incorrect shapes used or no inputs as long as decisions are logically correct. Must attempt all three decisions. Allow calculation of half price / full price instead of message but this must still be output. Inputs / decisions may be presented as individual or combined boxes but must still store as three variables.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Start and end/stop with all boxes connected, no orphan boxes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Input three variables using parallelogram shape","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks all three criteria (day, student, discount card) using diamond shape(s) with two lines from each","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"full price\\" with correct conditions using parallelogram shape","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"half price\\" with correct conditions using parallelogram shape","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"22(b)","parent_question":22,"topic":"identify-additional-inputs","marks":2,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The restaurant adds a service charge to the cost of a meal depending on the number of people at a table. If there are more than five people 5% is added to the total cost of each meal.\\n\\nCustomers can also choose to leave a tip, this is optional and the customer can choose between a percentage of the cost, or a set amount.\\n\\nIdentify all the additional inputs that will be required for this change to the algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Ignore additional inputs that would be sensible, such as cost of the meal. Accept inputs in form of pseudocode / high-level language. Max 1 if other irrelevant inputs given. \\"Whether to leave a tip or not\\" or \\"Amount of tip\\" NE for BP2. Must address both the percentage and value of tip if asked for. BOD \\"type of tip\\" for BP2.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Number of people (at the table) // whether there are more than 5 people or not","marks":1},{"criteria":"Choice between percentage and value // actual value of both percentage, value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"23(a)","parent_question":23,"topic":"username-from-flowchart","marks":1,"command_word":"State","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program creates usernames for a school. The first design of the program is shown in the flowchart in Fig. 2.\\n\\nFor example, using the process in Fig. 2, Tom Ward\'s username would be TomWa.\\n\\nState, using the process in Fig. 2, the username for Rebecca Ellis."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/username-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Flowchart that builds a username from the first three letters of firstName and the first two letters of surname."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only (allow any case).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"RebEl","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"23(b)(i)","parent_question":23,"topic":"username-teacher-rule","marks":1,"command_word":"State","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The program design is updated to create usernames as follows:\\n- If the person is a teacher, their username is the last 3 letters of their surname and then the first 2 letters of their first name.\\n- If the person is a student, their username is the first 3 letters of their first name and then the first 2 letters of their surname.\\n\\nWhat would be the username for a teacher called Fred Biscuit using the updated process?"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only (allow any case). Last 3 letters of Biscuit = \'uit\', first 2 letters of Fred = \'Fr\'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"uitFr","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"23(b)(ii)","parent_question":23,"topic":"username-algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm for the updated program design shown in (i)."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nAsk the user to input the data, store in variables firstname, surname and role.\\nCheck whether the role entered is teacher.\\nIf it is, join the right 3 most letters in surname with the left 2 letters in firstname. Store this in username.\\nIf it is not teacher, join the left 3 letters from firstname with the left 2 letters from surname. Store this in username.\\nOutput the value in username.\\n\\n1 mark for each correct bullet to a maximum of 6. If used, a flowchart should represent the bulleted steps in the answer column.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Taking firstname, surname and teacher or student as input","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checking IF role is teacher/student (using appropriate selection)","marks":1},{"criteria":"For teacher: Generating last 3 letters of surname using appropriate string manipulation","marks":1},{"criteria":"...Generating first 2 of letters of firstname and adding to previous","marks":1},{"criteria":"For student: correctly calculating as before","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct concatenation and output","marks":1}]}}]'),MI={paper_id:OI,qualification:TI,component:PI,title:JI,year:II,session:EI,total_marks:RI,questions:DI},NI="J277-TOPIC-2.1.3",LI="J277",BI="TOPIC",FI="Searching and Sorting Algorithms",$I=null,zI=null,WI=92,QI=JSON.parse(`[{"question_id":"1a","parent_question":1,"topic":"merge sort","marks":4,"command_word":"show","response_style":"annotate-image","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Show the steps that a merge sort would take to put the data set into ascending numerical order. Annotate the image to show each split and merge stage clearly (use buckets / grouped lists)."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/topics/ocr/2.1.3_q1a_merge_sort_canvas.png","alt_text":"Starting list of eight numbers in boxes — 2, 7, 3, 1, 9, 6, 5, 4 — with blank space below for the student to draw the merge sort divide and merge stages."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["Do not allow merging out of order and then sorting for BP3 or BP4.","Splitting can be done in multiple stages or all at once.","If no attempt at splitting up, assume the initial list is already split and mark BP2, 3 and 4.","If splitting done incorrectly, do not give BP1 but allow access to further points.","Do not accept a final sorted list for BP4 if no steps shown or a wrong algorithm is used.","Brackets are not required but numbers must be reasonably clear in groups of 2s, 4s, etc.","If candidate uses other numbers or sorts into descending order, penalise once then follow through."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Split up into individual lists each of one item (divide stage)","marks":1},{"criteria":"[2,7] [1,3] [6,9] [4,5] (sorted lists of 2)","marks":1},{"criteria":"[1,2,3,7] [4,5,6,9] (sorted lists of 4)","marks":1},{"criteria":"[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,9] after reasonable attempt at merging from 4 to 8","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1b","parent_question":1,"topic":"sorting algorithms and arrays","marks":3,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each table to complete the descriptions."},{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort..."},{"type":"table","headers":["Description","Tick one box"],"rows":[["uses an array of numbers with a sorted part and unsorted part",""],["picks out the middle value to begin with",""],["merges arrays of numbers together",""]]},{"type":"text","value":"A bubble sort compares pairs of values and then:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Description","Tick one box"],"rows":[["always swaps them",""],["swaps them if they are in the correct order",""],["swaps them if they are in the incorrect order",""]]},{"type":"text","value":"A sorting algorithm can sort data in an array. An array:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Description","Tick one box"],"rows":[["is declared with a fixed length",""],["stores multiple values that cannot be changed while the program is running",""],["can only be declared with a maximum of one-dimension (1D)",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"No mark if more than one box ticked per section.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Insertion sort: 'uses an array of numbers with a sorted part and unsorted part' (top box)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Bubble sort: 'swaps them if they are in the incorrect order' (bottom box)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Array: 'is declared with a fixed length' (top box)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1ci","parent_question":1,"topic":"linear search identification","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm can be used to search for a value in an array. The algorithm follows these steps:"},{"type":"text","value":"- compares the first value in the array to the value being searched for\\n- stops if the comparison is true\\n- moves to the next value in the array if the comparison if false."},{"type":"text","value":"Give the name of the searching algorithm described."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept 'linear' by itself. Do not allow 'linear sort'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Linear search","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"1cii","parent_question":1,"topic":"linear search stopping condition","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one other condition that will cause the algorithm to stop."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Technically the condition is when the end of the list is reached and the value has not been found, but responses relating simply to reaching the end of the list or having checked every item are accepted. Do not accept 'value not found / value not in list' by itself; must include idea of reaching the end or having checked the entire list.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"End of list reached / gets to last value (and not found) / checked every value (and value not found)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2ai","parent_question":2,"topic":"data types for sports day variables","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Students take part in a sports day. The students are put into teams. Students gain points depending on their result and the year group they are in. The points are added to the team score. The team with the most points at the end of the sports day wins. Data about the teams and students is stored in a sports day program."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the most appropriate data type for each variable used by the program. Each data type must be different."},{"type":"table","headers":["Variable","Example","Data type"],"rows":[["teamName","\\"Super-Team\\"",""],["studentYearGroup","11",""],["javelinThrow","18.2",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept alternative equivalent correct data types (e.g. single/double/decimal for BP3). Do not accept char for BP1.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"teamName: String","marks":1},{"criteria":"studentYearGroup: Integer","marks":1},{"criteria":"javelinThrow: Real / Float","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2aii","parent_question":2,"topic":"linear search function design","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The student names for a team are stored in an array with the identifier theTeam. An example of the data in this array is shown (the array contains 6 student names; indices 0 to 5)."},{"type":"text","value":"A linear search function is used to find whether a student is in the team. The function:\\n- takes a student name as a parameter\\n- returns True if the student name is in the array\\n- returns False if the student name is not in the array."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the design of an algorithm for the linear search function."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"function linearSearch(studentName)\\n  for count = 0 to ____\\n    if theTeam[____] == ____ then\\n      return ____\\n    endif\\n  next count\\n  return False\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["Accept \`6\` / \`theTeam.length()\` for BP1 (Python).","Accept alternative length functions e.g. \`len()\`.","Accept \`count = 5\` (and equivalents) for BP1.","Accept \`'True'\` for BP4.","Do not allow obvious spaces in variable names.","Ignore capitalisation."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"BP1: theTeam.length() - 1 // 5 (loop upper bound)","marks":1},{"criteria":"BP2: count (index into theTeam)","marks":1},{"criteria":"BP3: studentName (value being compared)","marks":1},{"criteria":"BP4: True (returned when match found)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2b","parent_question":2,"topic":"trace table for javelin score algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This algorithm calculates the number of points a student gets for the distance they throw in the javelin:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"01 javelinThrow = input(\\"Enter distance\\")\\n02 yearGroup = input(\\"Enter year group\\")\\n03 if javelinThrow >= 20.0 then\\n04   score = 3\\n05 elseif javelinThrow >= 10.0 then\\n06   score = 2\\n07 else\\n08   score = 1\\n09 endif\\n10 if yearGroup != 11 then\\n11   score = score * 2\\n12 endif\\n13 print(\\"The score is\\", score)"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the trace table for the algorithm when a student in year 10 throws a distance of 14.3. You may not need to use all the rows in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Line number","javelinThrow","yearGroup","score","Output"],"rows":[["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""],["","","","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["Max 3 if in wrong order or additional (incorrect) changes.","Penalise line numbers once then follow through.","Allow FT for BP4 for current value of \`score\`.","BP4 must not include a comma.","Ignore superfluous spaces. Ignore quotation marks.","Treat any entry in output column as an output, even if \`x\`, \`-\` or \`0\`.","Answer may include lines where no changes or output happens (i.e. lines 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12).","Where a variable doesn't change, the current value may be repeated on subsequent lines."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"javelinThrow set to 14.3 on line 01","marks":1},{"criteria":"yearGroup set to 10 on line 02","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 2 on line 06","marks":1},{"criteria":"score set to 4 on line 11 and 'The score is 4' output on line 13 with no additional outputs (allow input statements)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2ci","parent_question":2,"topic":"height validation algorithm","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The height a student jumps in the high jump needs to be input and validated. The height is entered in centimetres (cm) and must be between 40.0 and 180.0 inclusive."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:\\n- take the height jumped as input\\n- output \\"VALID\\" or \\"NOT VALID\\" depending on the height input."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use **either**:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, **or**\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["Answers using AND/OR for BP2 and BP3 must be logically correct e.g. \`if height >= 40 and height <= 180\`.","Do not accept \`if height >= 40 and <= 180\`.","Answers using OR will reverse output for BP4.","BP4 needs reasonable attempt at either BP2 or BP3; must be clear what is being checked.","Ignore conversion to \`int\` on input. \`input\` cannot be used as a variable name.","Greater than / less than symbols must be appropriate for a high-level language / ERL.","Do not accept \`=>\` (wrong way around) or \`>\` for greater-than-or-equal.","No obvious spaces in variable names. Penalise once then follow through."]},{"type":"text","value":"**Example 1** (checking for valid input):"},{"type":"code","value":"h = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h >= 40 and h <= 180 then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"**Example 2** (checking for invalid input):"},{"type":"code","value":"h = input(\\"Enter height jumped\\")\\nif h < 40 or h > 180 then\\n  print(\\"not valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nendif"}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs a value from the user and stores/uses it","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks min value (>= 40.0 or < 40)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks max value (<= 180.0 or > 180)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs both valid / not valid correctly based on checks","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2cii","parent_question":2,"topic":"test data types for validation","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm is tested using a range of tests. Complete the table to identify an example of test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (height jumped in cm)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","\\"VALID\\""],["","Boundary","\\"VALID\\""],["","Erroneous","\\"NOT VALID\\""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["No need to include decimals, e.g. accept \`50\`.","Ignore \`cm\` if given.","Answer must be actual data (e.g. \`50\`) and not a description of data (e.g. 'a value between 40 and 180').","If descriptions given, do not accept this as non-numeric for BP3."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal: any value between 40 and 180 inclusive","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 or 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: any value less than 40, any value greater than 180, or any non-numeric value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2d","parent_question":2,"topic":"SQL SELECT statement","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database. The database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n____"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors.","Ignore capitals.","Do not accept \`*\` or additional fields for BP1.","Spelling must be accurate (e.g. not \`TblResults\`).","No spaces in field names; penalise obvious spaces once then FT.","Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and \`11\`.","Accept \`==\` for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE keyword","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2ei","parent_question":2,"topic":"abstraction in program design","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is. Give benefit of the doubt where unclear. Examples: 'focus on student names and events'; 'ignore data such as students' favourite subjects'; 'store year groups instead of ages or DOB'; 'shows student IDs instead of full student details'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design), applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2eii","parent_question":2,"topic":"decomposition in program design","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description of what decomposition is. Give BOD where unclear. Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields (e.g. split into StudentID, YearGroup, etc). BOD if answer discusses one table but suggests other tables could be used. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program. Do not give answers relating simply to data being split into smaller groups unless this clearly relates to how data is decomposed into tables in the database. Examples: 'splits the program up into different events'; 'separates the validation routines into subroutines'; 'breaks the database down into a table per event'.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables, applicable to the sports day context","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2f","parent_question":2,"topic":"winning team algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score)."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"For BP3, allow 'stop' to be entered for either team name or score (or both). Allow a third input (e.g. 'do you wish to stop?'). Allow use of break (or equivalent) to exit loop for BP3. Allow use of recursive function(s) for BP2/3. Initialisation of variables not needed (assume variables are 0 or empty string if not set). Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score. BP4/5 could be done in many ways. Allow use of max/sum functions and arrays/lists. FT for BP6 if attempt made at calculating highest score/name. If the answer simply asks for multiple entries (not using iteration), BP2 and 3 cannot be accessed but all others available. For minor syntax errors (e.g. missing quotation marks, == for assignment, spaces in variable names) penalise once then FT. 'input' cannot be used as a variable name.\\n\\nExample 1:\\nhighscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)\\n\\nExample 2 (alternative):\\nscores = []\\nteams = []\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  scores.append(score)\\n  teams.append(team)\\nendwhile\\nhighscore = max(scores)\\nhighteam = teams[scores.index(highscore)]\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store / use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"...to enter team/score until 'stop' entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3i","parent_question":3,"topic":"binary search trace","marks":3,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Show how a binary search will be used to find the number 10 in the following data set:"},{"type":"text","value":"1   2   5   6   7   10   20"}],"answer":{"guidance":"BP1 can be given for generic answer. BP2 and 3 must be linked to the data set given. For BP2, must remove 1, 2, 5 and 6 from the list if discussing individual numbers. Allow FT for BP3 if this is done incorrectly.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare to / pick out middle value (which is 6)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Discard only left side / retain only right side (because 6 < 10)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Compare to / pick out (middle value which is) 10","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3ii","parent_question":3,"topic":"binary search prerequisite","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State one pre-requisite for a binary search algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"The data must already be in order for a binary search to function.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Data must be sorted / in order","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3iii","parent_question":3,"topic":"merge sort identification","marks":1,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box to identify the name of the sorting algorithm that splits data into individual items before recombining in order."},{"type":"table","headers":["Sorting algorithm","Tick one box"],"rows":[["Bubble sort",""],["Insertion sort",""],["Merge sort",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Merge sort is the divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge sort","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4a","parent_question":4,"topic":"linear search identification","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An array stores a collection of words. The array has the identifier data."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"data = [\\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\", \\"when\\", \\"and\\", \\"or\\", \\"and\\", \\"it\\"]"},{"type":"text","value":"A searching algorithm is used to find a word in the array data. The algorithm used to search for a word is shown."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"word = input(\\"Enter a word to find\\")\\nfound = False\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == word then\\n    found = True\\n  endif\\nnext i\\nprint(found)"},{"type":"text","value":"State the name of this searching algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept Sequential Search.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Linear search","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4b","parent_question":4,"topic":"refining algorithm to count occurrences","marks":4,"command_word":"refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Some words appear in the array more than once."},{"type":"text","value":"Refine the algorithm to:\\n- count the number of times a word appears in the array\\n- output this number.\\nFor example, the algorithm should output 3 if the input is \\"and\\"."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow answers that completely rewrite the algorithm as long as the points on the left are met.\\n\\nExample answer:\\nsearch = input(\\"Enter a word\\")\\ncount = 0\\nfor i = 0 to 7\\n  if data[i] == search then\\n    count = count + 1\\nnext i\\nprint(count)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Declares variable to store the count (before the for loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Initialises variable to zero","marks":1},{"criteria":"Increments variable by 1 each time the word is found","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs count value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4ci","parent_question":4,"topic":"insertion sort sorted and unsorted sections","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is used to put the array with the identifier \`data\` into alphabetical order. The diagram shows the array when the insertion sort is almost complete."},{"type":"table","headers":[],"variant":"separated-boxes","sections":[{"label":"Section A","span":6},{"label":"Section B","span":2}],"rows":[["and","and","and","it","or","when","or","it"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Section A: the first six items (\`and\`, \`and\`, \`and\`, \`it\`, \`or\`, \`when\`).\\nSection B: the final two items (\`or\`, \`it\`).\\n\\nExplain why the array is shown with two sections."}],"answer":{"guidance":"An insertion sort maintains a sorted partition at one end of the array and an unsorted partition at the other.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A is sorted","marks":1},{"criteria":"B is not (yet) sorted","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4cii","parent_question":4,"topic":"completing insertion sort steps","marks":3,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps an insertion sort takes to complete sorting the array data."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 marks, 1 mark per point. Max two marks if candidate simply states to insert 'or' then 'it' into the correct place without discussing how this is determined. Max one if generic answer with no reference to given array. Allow answers referring to swapping items down the array to get to the correct position.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare 'or' with next item on left","marks":1},{"criteria":"'or' is smaller than 'when' so it repeats the comparison with next item and inserts 'or' / item in correct place, based on comparisons","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat to insert 'it'","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4d","parent_question":4,"topic":"merge sort identification","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"One sorting algorithm splits an array into single items and then combines them into an ordered array. State the name of this sorting algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other sorting algorithms that use divide and conquer.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge sort","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5a","parent_question":5,"topic":"purpose of temp variable in insertion sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is one type of sorting algorithm. A student has written a pseudocode algorithm to perform an insertion sort on a 1D array names."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"names = [\\"Kareem\\", \\"Sarah\\", \\"Zac\\", \\"Sundip\\", \\"Anika\\"]\\nfor count = 1 to names.length - 1\\n  pos = count\\n  while (pos > 0 and names[pos] < names[pos - 1])\\n    temp = names[pos]\\n    names[pos] = names[pos - 1]\\n    names[pos - 1] = temp\\n    pos = pos - 1\\n  endwhile\\nnext count"},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the purpose of the variable temp in the insertion sort pseudocode algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not allow answers that clearly refer to storing the position / index (or any other out-of-context data) for BP1; it is the name itself that is being stored, not the position. If unclear, allow BOD. E.g. do not allow 'holds the values of the index / holds value for position of the name'. Allow FT for subsequent points.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Stores/holds data/value/name/names[pos]","marks":1},{"criteria":"...so (value) can be changed / swapped / moved / overwritten / inserted / without being lost / will be assigned to names[pos-1]","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5b","parent_question":5,"topic":"condition-controlled inner loop","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort contains a nested loop; a loop within a loop. In this pseudocode algorithm the outer loop is a count-controlled loop and the inner loop is a condition-controlled loop."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the inner loop needs to be a condition-controlled loop."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 1 from each section, 2 marks total. Do not allow 'while names are in the wrong order'. BP4 must have reference to checking a condition / condition being met, not just having a condition.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reason (1 mark): do not know how many iterations / swaps needed, or do not know (at run time) how many times the value will change positions, or do not know how many times a condition-controlled loop will need to run / execute","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description (1 mark): condition-controlled loops run while/until a condition is true/false/met; repeats while value in [pos-1] is larger than value in [pos] / while further swap needed; will swap value until in correct position; more efficient than / does not need to iterate as many times as a count-controlled / for loop","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5ci","parent_question":5,"topic":"difference between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A bubble sort is a type of sorting algorithm."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one difference between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for insertion and bubble sort, max 2. Answer must reference both bubble sort and insertion sort for 2 marks (except if efficiency mark plus expansion given). Allow reference to Big O for efficiency discussion. Only award efficiency once. Only award fewer iterations once. Do not accept 'completes in one iteration' for insertion sort. Accept list / data / values / etc for array. 'When data more scrambled' only makes sense when discussing efficiency/speed; do not give marks for saying that either can handle data that is more scrambled. Do not accept 'bubble/insertion sort does not' for 2nd mark.\\n\\nInsertion sort points: inserts/moves values into correct position; inserts value once (then in correct position); stops when end of array reached / completes in one pass through the array; moves items down the array / left; start of array becomes sorted first; creates a sorted array within an array / has a sorted/unsorted partition; starts on 2nd value; more efficient/faster than bubble sort; ...because fewer iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.\\n\\nBubble sort points: compares/swaps pairs of values; value is repeatedly moved/swapped (until in correct position); repeats if a swap has been made / needs multiple passes; will complete a final iteration once sorted (to check for no swaps needed); moves items up the array; end of array becomes sorted first; moves/bubbles the highest value to the top; less efficient/slower than insertion sort (on large sets of values); ...more iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid point describing insertion sort behaviour","marks":1},{"criteria":"One valid point describing bubble sort behaviour (contrasting)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5cii","parent_question":5,"topic":"similarities between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two similarities between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Allow reference to both sorting / putting items into order for BP1. 'Allows sorting of numbers and strings' meets BP1. Allow answers relating to not needing additional memory as BP2. Allow 'breaking into smaller lists' as divide-and-conquer for BP2. If answer is a statement (e.g. 'uses loops'), assume candidate is talking about both algorithms doing this.\\n\\nValid similarities include: both produce a sorted list/array; both work in place / without duplicating data / without using divide-and-conquer; both need a temporary variable; both swap values; both use loops/iteration/repeats; both loops are nested/inside each other; both (may) need multiple passes; both use selection; both work with an array/list data structure; both work from left to right; both build up sorted list one item at a time (after every pass); both compare (pairs of) values; both (typically) less efficient/slower than merge sort; both inefficient/slow for larger/unsorted lists / efficient for small/(nearly) sorted lists; both start by comparing first two values.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid similarity","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid similarity","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6a","parent_question":6,"topic":"merge sort of positive and negative numbers","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program stores the following list of positive and negative numbers. The numbers need sorting into ascending order using a merge sort."},{"type":"text","value":"45   12   -99   100   -13   0   17   -27"},{"type":"text","value":"The first step is to divide the list into individual lists of one number each. This has been done for you."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the merge sort of the data by showing each step of the process."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not credit BP3 simply for a sorted list. Groups of numbers must clearly be the correct size. Do not allow answers that show lists being merged and then sorting in place; this is incorrect.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted lists of size 2: (12 45) (-99 100) (-13 0) (-27 17)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted lists of size 4: (-99 12 45 100) (-27 -13 0 17)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Merge into correct sorted list of size 8: (-99 -27 -13 0 12 17 45 100)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6b","parent_question":6,"topic":"binary search description","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Once a list of numbers are in order, a binary search can be run on the data."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps a binary search will follow to look for a number in a sorted list."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any four bullet points for 1 mark each. Do not allow 'split the list in half' on its own as first step; this is incorrect. Can get BP1 and BP2 in one step (e.g. 'check if the middle number is the one we're looking for'). For BP3, accept focussing on correct half. Repeat (BP4) must be in the context of an attempt at a binary search. Allow correct reference to recursion. 'Until number is not in the list' is NE for final BP; need to explain how this is known.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Select / choose / pick middle number (or left/right of middle if even count)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if selected number is equal to / matches target number (not just compare)","marks":1},{"criteria":"If searched number is larger, discard left half / if searched number is smaller, discard right half","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat until number found or remaining list is of size 1 / 0 (number not found)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6c","parent_question":6,"topic":"linear search when item not present","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A linear search could be used instead of a binary search."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the steps a linear search would follow when searching for a number that is not in the given list."}],"answer":{"guidance":"2nd bullet point must cover both ideas of checking all of the values AND being done in order. 'Checks each value' / 'one by one' / 'step by step' by itself is NE (does not say in order). Do not accept 'repeat until value found' for BP2 (question says number is not in the list). 'Checks each value from beginning to end' implies order so gets both BP1 and BP2.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Starting with the first value","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checking all values in order","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7a","parent_question":7,"topic":"translation of high-level code","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A computer game is written in a high-level programming language. State why the computer needs to translate the code before it is executed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Maximum 1 mark.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"To convert it to binary/machine code; the processor can only understand machine code","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7b","parent_question":7,"topic":"compiler vs interpreter differences","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Either a compiler or an interpreter can translate the code."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe two differences between how a compiler and an interpreter would translate the code."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark to be awarded for the correct identification and one for a valid description up to a maximum of 4 marks. No more than 2 marks for answers relating only to interpreters and no more than 2 marks for answers only relating to compilers.\\n\\nValid differences: compiler translates all the code in one go whereas an interpreter translates one line at a time; compiler creates an executable whereas an interpreter does not / executes one line at a time; compiler reports errors at the end whereas an interpreter stops when it finds an error.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First identification of a difference (compiler vs interpreter)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description/expansion of the first difference","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second identification of a difference (compiler vs interpreter)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description/expansion of the second difference","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8i","parent_question":8,"topic":"binary search on grades","marks":4,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A school uses a mobile phone app to allow parents to book appointments for parents' evenings. Parents must log in before they can use the system. They then choose to book a new appointment, view all appointments already made or update their personal details. If parents choose to view their appointments, they can either view them on-screen or print them off. Each teacher has the assessment grades for each student. These grades are stored in numerical order."},{"type":"text","value":"The grades for one student are shown:"},{"type":"text","value":"2   3   4   5   6   7   8"},{"type":"text","value":"Show the steps that a binary search would take to check whether the student has achieved a grade 7 in any assessment. Your answer must refer to the grades provided."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet, max 4. Do not accept generic answers that do not refer to the data given.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare 5 (middle value) to 7","marks":1},{"criteria":"5 is smaller than 7 / 7 is larger than 5 so...","marks":1},{"criteria":"Discard lower part of list / repeat with upper part of list","marks":1},{"criteria":"Compare 7 to 7 (item found)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8ii","parent_question":8,"topic":"binary search value not present","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how a binary search would determine that a value does not appear in a given array."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet, max 2. Do not accept answers relating to 'end of list'; this is linear search.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"List of size 1 to compare","marks":1},{"criteria":"...and item not matched to search term","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8iii","parent_question":8,"topic":"advantage of binary search","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one advantage of a binary search over a linear search."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Accept reference to Big O notation as equivalent to more efficient.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"More efficient / less time taken (to find item) / fewer comparisons to make (with large lists)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9a","parent_question":9,"topic":"bubble sort stages","marks":4,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program uses a file to store a list of words that can be used in a game. A sample of this data is shown in Fig. 3."},{"type":"text","value":"A sample of this data is shown in Fig. 3."},{"type":"table","headers":["1","2","3","4","5","6"],"rows":[["crime","bait","fright","victory","nibble","loose"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 3\\n\\nShow the stages of a bubble sort when applied to the data shown in Fig. 3."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for each row from rows 2-5. Allow multiple swaps in one stage, where it is clear that a bubble sort has been applied.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct first pass of bubble sort stages","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct second pass of bubble sort stages","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct third pass of bubble sort stages","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct final sorted row (bait, crime, fright, loose, nibble, victory)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9b","parent_question":9,"topic":"binary search stages","marks":4,"command_word":"show","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A second sample of data is shown in Fig. 4."},{"type":"table","headers":[],"rows":[["amber","house","kick","moose","orange","range","tent","wind","zebra"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 4\\n\\nShow the stages of a binary search to find the word \`zebra\` using the data shown in Fig. 4."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet (multiple ways through, marks awarded for appropriate comparison and creation of sub groups).\\n\\nExample working:\\ncompare zebra to orange\\ngreater, split right\\ncompare to wind\\ngreater, split right\\ncompare to zebra","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Comparing zebra to orange","marks":1},{"criteria":"Greater, so split and take right side","marks":1},{"criteria":"Further comparison (1 or 2 depending on choices made)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct identification of zebra using methodology above","marks":1}]}}]`),VI={paper_id:NI,qualification:LI,component:BI,title:FI,year:$I,session:zI,total_marks:WI,questions:QI},UI="J277-TOPIC-2.2.1",HI="J277",GI="TOPIC",XI="Programming Fundamentals",YI=null,KI=null,ZI=144,eE=JSON.parse('[{"question_id":"1","parent_question":1,"topic":"algorithm design; selection; string handling","marks":6,"command_word":"create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"As part of a security system, the user is asked to choose a 4-digit value for their PIN. This PIN cannot be 1234 or 4321"},{"type":"text","value":"Create an algorithm that:\\n• takes the PIN as input as a string\\n• checks that the PIN is 4 characters in length\\n• checks that the PIN is not either “1234” or “4321”\\n• outputs “VALID PIN” if the PIN meets all of the criteria\\n• outputs “INVALID PIN” if the PIN does not meet all of the criteria.\\n\\nYou do not need to check that the digits entered are numeric."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example code:"},{"type":"code","value":"pin = input(\\"Enter PIN\\")\\nif pin.length == 4 and pin != \\"1234\\" and pin != \\"4321\\" then\\n  print(\\"VALID PIN\\")\\nelse:\\n  print(\\"INVALID PIN\\")"},{"type":"list","items":["Candidates may respond in pseudocode, structured English, flowcharts, etc., but must not simply repeat the question.","Each condition in compound comparisons must be a valid comparison (e.g. `pin != \\"1234\\" and pin != \\"4321\\"`, not `pin != \\"1234\\" or \\"4321\\"`)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and storage/use of PIN","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at selection / condition-controlled loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for 4 digits","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks for both 1234 and 4321","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"INVALID PIN\\" after sensible attempt at all three checks","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output of \\"VALID PIN\\" only when PIN is fully valid and no contradictory output","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(i)","parent_question":2,"topic":"variables; input; OCR Exam Reference Language","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Rockfest is a yearly music festival. Visitors buy tickets to attend and watch the music acts.\\n\\nA computer system is created to sell tickets and store information about visitors and the music acts.\\n\\nVisitors are asked to choose a password. The password is entered twice and stored in the variables passA and passB"},{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box to identify the correct OCR Exam Reference Language statements for the design of this task."},{"type":"table","headers":["OCR Exam Reference Language to enter two passwords","Tick (✓) one box"],"rows":[["`passA and passB = input(\\"enter password twice\\")`",""],["`input(\\"enter your password\\") = passA`\\n`input(\\"enter your password again\\") = passB`",""],["`passA = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`passB = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""],["`\\"passA\\" = input(\\"enter your password\\")`\\n`\\"passB\\" = input(\\"enter your password again\\")`",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"3rd box ticked: passA = input(“enter your password”) / passB = input(“enter your password again”)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"3rd box ticked","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(a)(ii)","parent_question":2,"topic":"selection; comparison; output","marks":3,"command_word":"design","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Design an algorithm that:\\n• checks if the passwords stored in passA and passB are the same\\n• outputs “passwords match” if they are the same\\n• outputs “passwords do not match” if they are not the same."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\nif passA == passB:\\n  print(\\"passwords match\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"passwords do not match\\")\\nend if\\n\\nAllow alternative messages. Ignore superfluous code. Could be done as separate if statements.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks if passA and passB are equal","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if True","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct output if False","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"2(b)","parent_question":2,"topic":"iteration; selection; variables","marks":6,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 500 tickets available for the music festival. The number of tickets currently available is stored in the variable `tickets`."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the algorithm to:\\n• prompt the user to enter how many tickets they would like and take this value as input\\n• output “Tickets booked” and reduce the value stored in `tickets` if there are enough tickets available\\n• output “Not enough tickets” if there are not enough tickets available\\n• repeat the steps until there are no tickets left.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","value":"tickets = 500"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example code:\\ntickets = 500\\nwhile tickets > 0\\n  num = input(“enter num tickets”)\\n  if tickets >= num then\\n    print(“tickets booked”)\\n    tickets = tickets – num\\n  else\\n    print(“not enough tickets”)\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\n\\nMust allow booking when exact number remain. Do not repeatedly reset tickets to 500.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and store/use number of tickets needed","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if enough tickets left","marks":1},{"criteria":"Sensible confirmation message output correctly for both booked and not enough","marks":1},{"criteria":"tickets variable updated correctly","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat all above while tickets > 0 / until tickets <= 0","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(i)","parent_question":3,"topic":"iteration; programming constructs","marks":4,"command_word":"identify and describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This flowchart uses iteration."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"data/png/j277/diagrams/iteration-flowchart.png","alt_text":"Iteration flowchart that repeatedly outputs OK and subtracts 2 from x until x is less than 0."},{"type":"text","value":"Iteration is a programming construct.\\n\\nIdentify and describe two different examples of iteration that can be used in a high-level language."}],"answer":{"guidance":"2 marks per construct (max 4). Examples: FOR (count controlled, repeats a specified number of times); WHILE (condition controlled, repeats while a condition is true / condition tested before code runs); DO UNTIL / REPEAT UNTIL (condition controlled, repeats until a condition is true / condition tested after code runs). Allow suitable examples from other high-level languages.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Example 1: identification of iteration type","marks":1},{"criteria":"Example 1: description","marks":1},{"criteria":"Example 2: identification of iteration type","marks":1},{"criteria":"Example 2: description","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"3(ii)","parent_question":3,"topic":"programming constructs","marks":2,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the names of two other programming constructs that are used in a high-level language."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Sequence; Selection. Allow branching as an alternative to selection. Do not allow examples by themselves (e.g. IF).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Sequence","marks":1},{"criteria":"Selection","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4","parent_question":4,"topic":"functions; parameters; selection; return values","marks":6,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm stores the `position` of a character on a straight line as an integer. A user can move the character left or right.\\n\\nThe following algorithm:\\n• generates one random number between 1 and 512 (inclusive) to store as the `position`\\n• prompts the user to input a `direction` to move (left or right)\\n• takes a `direction` as input until a valid `direction` is input."},{"type":"code","value":"p = random(1, 512)\\nprint(\\"The position is \\", p)\\na = \\"\\"\\nwhile a != \\"left\\" and a != \\"right\\"\\n  a = input(\\"Enter direction, left or right\\")\\nendwhile"},{"type":"text","value":"If the character moves left, 5 is subtracted from the `position`.\\nIf the character moves right, 5 is added to the `position`.\\n\\nThe `position` of the character can only be between 1 and 512 inclusive.\\n\\nThe function `moveCharacter()`:\\n• takes the `direction` (left or right) and current `position` as parameters\\n• changes `position` based on `direction`\\n• sets `position` to 1 if the new `position` is less than 1\\n• sets `position` to 512 if the new `position` is greater than 512\\n• returns the new `position`.\\n\\nComplete the function `moveCharacter()`"},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"function moveCharacter(direction, position)\\n\\n\\nendfunction"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nif direction == \\"left\\" then\\n  position = position - 5\\nelseif direction == \\"right\\" then\\n  position = position + 5\\nendif\\nif position < 1 then\\n  position = 1\\nelseif position > 512 then\\n  position = 512\\nendif\\nreturn position","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Appropriate use of both parameters and no additional inputs / incorrect overwrites","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks `direction` is \\"left\\" and subtracts 5 from `position`","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly checks `direction` is \\"right\\" and adds 5 to `position`","marks":1},{"criteria":"Ensures `position` is between 1 and 512 inclusive","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns the updated `position`","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5","parent_question":5,"topic":"programming constructs; keywords","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box in each row to identify the programming construct where each keyword is used."},{"type":"table","headers":["Keyword","Selection","Iteration"],"rows":[["`if`","",""],["`for`","",""],["`while`","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"if = Selection; for = Iteration; while = Iteration.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"if = Selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"for = Iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"while = Iteration","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"tracing; selection","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the output of the algorithm."},{"type":"code","value":"temp = 7\\nif temp < 10 then\\n    print(temp)\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only: 7","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Output = 7","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)","parent_question":6,"topic":"tracing; arithmetic operators","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"code","value":"value = 3\\nvalue = value - 1\\nprint(value * 2)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only: 4","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Output = 4","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(c)","parent_question":6,"topic":"arrays; indexing","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"code","value":"scores = [3, 6, 6, 9, 2, 8]\\nnumber = scores[2]\\nprint(number)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only: 6","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Output = 6","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(d)","parent_question":6,"topic":"strings; length","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"code","value":"day = \\"Monday\\"\\nx = day.length\\nprint(x)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Correct Answer Only: 6","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Output = 6","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(a)","parent_question":7,"topic":"file handling; input; write to text file","marks":4,"command_word":"design","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Drones flies goods around the country using drones.\\n\\nA text file is used to store pilot’s codes and date of birth.\\n\\nThe text file is named pilots.txt. The contents of some of this file is shown:"},{"type":"code","value":"JP670,17/05/1986\\nCC200,31/06/1980\\nSM720,01/04/1976\\nGC730,06/06/1978"},{"type":"text","value":"Design an algorithm to:\\n• Ask the user to input a pilot code and a date of birth\\n• Write these inputs to the pilots.txt text file"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"One mark per bullet point. Award BP4 for implicit closing of file (e.g. using `with...` in Python). Allow data to be written either by writing both values or by concatenating into one string with a separating comma."},{"type":"text","value":"Example answer:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"dronepilotData = open(\\"pilots.txt\\")\\npilotCode = input(\\"enter code\\")\\ndob = input(\\"enter date of birth\\")\\ndronepilotData.writeLine(pilotCode, dob)\\ndronepilotData.close()"}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ask the user for two inputs and store/use these","marks":1},{"criteria":"Open pilots.txt (for write/append)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Write both inputs to opened text file","marks":1},{"criteria":"Close text file (implicit closing acceptable)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)","parent_question":7,"topic":"functions; 2D arrays; casting; selection","marks":6,"command_word":"create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A pilot is allowed to fly a maximum of 9 hours per day. The pilot code and the hours flown for each flight in one day are stored in a 2D array of strings with the identifier journeys.\\n\\nThe data in journeys is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["","[0]","[1]"],"rows":[["[0]","\\"SM720\\"","\\"4.5\\""],["[1]","\\"GC730\\"","\\"3\\""],["[2]","\\"JP670\\"","\\"2\\""],["[3]","\\"GC730\\"","\\"3.5\\""],["[4]","\\"CC200\\"","\\"9\\""],["[5]","\\"RY320\\"","\\"12\\""]]},{"type":"text","value":"The value of journeys[3,1] is \\"3.5\\".\\n\\nCreate a function, pilotValid(), that:\\n• takes a pilot code as a parameter\\n• uses the 2D array journeys to calculate the number of hours that the pilot has flown\\n• returns valid if the pilot has flown 9 hours or fewer or warning if the pilot has flown for more than 9 hours\\n• uses casting where needed.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nfunction pilotValid(pilotCode)\\n  total = 0\\n  status = \\"\\"\\n  for i = 0 to 5\\n    if journeys[i,0] == pilotCode then\\n      temp = float(journeys[i,1])\\n      total = total + temp\\n    endif\\n  next i\\n  if total > 9 then\\n    status = \\"warning\\"\\n  else\\n    status = \\"valid\\"\\n  endif\\n  return status\\nendfunction\\n\\nDo not allow casting to integer; data includes decimal places.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function header pilotValid() with at least one parameter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks array element [i,0] for each item / iterates array","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates total hours","marks":1},{"criteria":"Casting to float / real","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if 9 hours or fewer and returns a value","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns \\"warning\\" and \\"valid\\" correctly","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"8","parent_question":8,"topic":"operators; comparison; arithmetic","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box in each row to identify if each operator is a comparison operator or an arithmetic operator."},{"type":"table","headers":["Operator","Comparison","Arithmetic"],"rows":[["`==`","",""],["`+`","",""],["`DIV`","",""],["`>`","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"== Comparison; + Arithmetic; DIV Arithmetic; > Comparison.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"== = Comparison","marks":1},{"criteria":"+ = Arithmetic","marks":1},{"criteria":"DIV = Arithmetic","marks":1},{"criteria":"> = Comparison","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(i)","parent_question":9,"topic":"input validation; selection; iteration","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A player enters a code at the beginning of a computer game. The code is three characters long.\\nThe code sets the level of the game. The game has three levels.\\n\\nA variable level is assigned a value based on the code entered."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:\\n• asks the user to enter a three-character code\\n• repeats the previous step if the code is not 3 characters long\\n• if the code entered is SVA, assigns the value 2 to level\\n• if the code entered is UTV, assigns the value 3 to level\\n• assigns the value 1 to level if any other three-character code is entered."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nlevel = 0\\ncode = \\"\\"\\nwhile code.length != 3 then\\n   code = input(\\"enter a 3 character code\\")\\nendwhile\\nswitch code :\\n   case \\"SVA\\":\\n     level = 2\\n   case \\"UTV\\":\\n     level = 3\\n   default:\\n     level = 1\\nendswitch","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input code from user and store/use","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat for non-three character codes","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check for one code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Set level appropriately for that code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check for and set level for 2nd code","marks":1},{"criteria":"Set level to 1 for any other 3 character code","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(ii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"functions; parameters; return values","marks":3,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"When a player completes a level, they move to the next level.\\n\\nOnce level 3 is completed, the game starts again at level 1.\\n\\nWrite a function called nextlevel() that:\\n• takes the current level as a parameter\\n• works out the integer value of the next level the player moves to\\n• returns the integer value."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nfunction nextlevel(oldlevel)\\n    if oldlevel == 3 then\\n        return 1\\n    else\\n        return oldlevel + 1\\n    endif\\nend function\\n\\nAccept correct use of MOD.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"function nextlevel() defined with at least one parameter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns parameter plus 1","marks":1},{"criteria":"Except when parameter is 3, then returns 1","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"9(iii)","parent_question":9,"topic":"function calls; output","marks":1,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the line of code to output the value returned from the function `nextlevel()` when the integer value 3 is passed in as a parameter."},{"type":"code","value":"print(____(____))"}],"answer":{"guidance":"print(nextlevel(3))","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"print(nextlevel(3))","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(a)","parent_question":10,"topic":"testing; test plans; boundary/normal/erroneous","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Drones flies goods around the country using drones.\\n\\nA pilot’s flying experience is validated. An algorithm checks that the experience is between 0 and 20 years."},{"type":"code","value":"exp = input(\\"Enter number of years\\")\\nif exp >= 0 and exp <= 20 then\\n    print(True)\\nelse\\n    print(False)\\nendif"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete this test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Experience in years","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["","Normal","True"],["20","Boundary",""],["32","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Row 1 experience: any value between 0 and 20 (e.g. 4). Row 2 expected output: True. Row 3 type of test: Invalid / Erroneous. Row 3 expected output: False.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any value between 0 and 20 (e.g. 4)","marks":1},{"criteria":"True (expected output for 20)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Invalid / erroneous (type of test for 32)","marks":1},{"criteria":"False (expected output for 32)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(i)","parent_question":10,"topic":"selection; calculation; input","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Pilots are paid a set amount each day. Pilots also get an additional payment for each mile they have flown that day. These payments are shown in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Flying experience","Pay per day","Pay per mile"],"rows":[["Fewer than 2 years","£120.00","£0.45"],["2 years to 5 years inclusive","£150.00","£0.65"],["More than 5 years","£180.00","£0.85"]]},{"type":"text","value":"For example, a pilot with 3 years’ experience who flies 100 miles in one day will receive a total of £215.00 pay.\\nThis is calculated in the following way:\\n• £150.00 pay for the day\\n• £65.00 additional payment (£0.65 pay per mile x 100 miles)\\n• Add £150.00 and £65.00 together to get £215.00 total pay"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the algorithm to:\\n• Calculate the total pay for the pilot for that day\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","value":"experience = input(\\"Enter years of experience\\")\\nmiles = input(\\"Enter miles flown\\")\\ntotalPay = 0\\n\\n# Write your algorithm here to set totalPay based on experience\\n# (use selection: < 2 years, 2 to 5 years, more than 5 years)\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nprint(totalPay)"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nif experience < 2 then\\n    totalPay = 120+(0.45*miles)\\nelseif experience <= 5 then\\n    totalPay = 150+(0.65*miles)\\nelse\\n    totalPay = 180+(0.85*miles)\\nend if","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Attempt at using selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for pilots with less than 2 years experience","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for pilots with 2 to 5 years experience (must include both 2 and 5)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates pay correctly for pilots with more than 5 years experience","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"10(b)(ii)","parent_question":10,"topic":"functions; parameters; refactoring","marks":2,"command_word":"refine","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The programmer decides to make a function to calculate the total pay for the pilot.\\nThe function is called calculatePay().\\n\\nThe function takes the values for experience and miles as two parameters and returns the total pay for the pilot.\\n\\nRefine the algorithm to use this function and output the pay for the pilot.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","value":"experience = input(\\"Enter years of experience\\")\\nmiles = input(\\"Enter miles flown\\")\\n____ = calculatePay(____, ____)\\nprint( totalPay )"}],"answer":{"guidance":"totalPay = calculatePay(experience, miles)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"totalPay (on left of assignment)","marks":1},{"criteria":"(experience, miles) as parameters (either order)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"11","parent_question":11,"topic":"iteration; condition-controlled loops; insertion sort","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is one type of sorting algorithm.\\n\\nA student has written a pseudocode algorithm to perform an insertion sort on a 1D array names."},{"type":"code","value":"names = [\\"Kareem\\", \\"Sarah\\", \\"Zac\\", \\"Sundip\\", \\"Anika\\"]\\nfor count = 1 to names.length - 1\\n  pos = count\\n  while (pos > 0 and names[pos] < names[pos - 1])\\n    temp = names[pos]\\n    names[pos] = names[pos - 1]\\n    names[pos - 1] = temp\\n    pos = pos - 1\\n  endwhile\\nnext count"},{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort contains a nested loop; a loop within a loop. In this pseudocode algorithm the outer loop is a count-controlled loop and the inner loop is a condition-controlled loop.\\n\\nExplain why the inner loop needs to be a condition-controlled loop."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 1 from each section. Reason: do not know how many iterations/swaps needed / do not know how many times a condition-controlled loop will need to run. Condition: repeats while condition is true/false / repeats while value in [pos-1] is larger than value in [pos] / will swap until in correct position.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Do not know (at run time) how many iterations/swaps needed","marks":1},{"criteria":"Condition-controlled loop runs while/until a condition is true/false (e.g. while swap needed / until in correct position)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(a)","parent_question":12,"topic":"selection; Boolean logic; procedure calls","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An alarm has an algorithm that decides whether to sound the alarm by checking the data that is stored in the following three variables.\\n• SystemArmed\\n• DoorSensorActive\\n• WindowSensorActive\\n\\nThe alarm will only sound when the alarm has been activated and one or both of the door and window sensors are activated. When the system needs to sound the alarm it calls the pre-written procedure SoundAlarm()\\n\\nWrite a program that checks the data in the variables and calls SoundAlarm() when appropriate.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nif SystemArmed then\\n  if DoorSensorActive then\\n    SoundAlarm()\\n  else if WindowSensorActive then\\n    SoundAlarm()\\n  endif\\nendif\\n\\nAlternative: if SystemArmed and (DoorSensorActive or WindowSensorActive) then SoundAlarm() endif. Brackets/parentheses essential due to operator precedence.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Attempt at using selection / condition-controlled loop","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checking if system armed / while system armed","marks":1},{"criteria":"If Door Sensor active OR Window Sensor active (both checks required)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calling SoundAlarm correctly","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(b)(i)","parent_question":12,"topic":"string concatenation","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An alarm system has multiple sensors. Each type of sensor has a code. The code for each sensor is given in the table:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Code","Sensor"],"rows":[["MS","Motion sensor"],["DS","Door sensor"],["WS","Window sensor"]]},{"type":"text","value":"A program is written to reset the sensors. The program:\\n• asks the user to enter the code for the sensor they want to reset\\n• calls the prewritten function CheckSensorCode() to check whether the code entered is a valid code\\n• the sensor number is read as input if the code is valid and the function ResetSensor() is called for the sensor"},{"type":"code","value":"01 sensorType = input(\\"Enter code of the type of sensor to reset\\")\\n02 if(CheckSensorCode(sensorType)) then\\n03   sensorNumber = input(\\" Please input the number of the sensor to reset\\")\\n04   sensorID = sensorType + sensorNumber\\n05   ResetSensor(sensorID)\\n06 endif"},{"type":"text","value":"Give the line number where there is concatenation."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Line 04 joins together sensorType and sensorNumber, assigning the concatenated result to sensorID.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 04","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(b)(ii)","parent_question":12,"topic":"variables; identifiers","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the identifier of a variable used in the program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"sensorType / sensorNumber / sensorID. Do not penalise case, spacing or minor misspellings.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"sensorType, sensorNumber or sensorID","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(b)(iii)","parent_question":12,"topic":"data types; return values; Boolean","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify the data type of the data returned by the function CheckSensorCode()"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Boolean. Accept Bool. Inferred from use in selection statement.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Boolean","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(b)(iv)","parent_question":12,"topic":"function calls","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the line number that contains a function call."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Line 01 / Line 02 / Line 03 / Line 05. Explicit calls are on lines 02 (CheckSensorCode) and 05 (ResetSensor). Many languages implement input as a function (lines 01 and 03).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Line 01, 02, 03 or 05","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(b)(v)","parent_question":12,"topic":"programming constructs","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify two programming constructs that have been used in the program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Selection and Sequence. Ignore minor spelling errors. Do not accept examples (e.g. IF). Iteration is not used in this program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Sequence","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(c)","parent_question":12,"topic":"procedures; file handling; parameters","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A program written in a high-level language is used to access the data from a database.\\nThis program has a procedure, SaveLogs(), that stores the data to an external text file.\\n\\nThe procedure SaveLogs():\\n• takes the string of data to be stored to the text file as a parameter\\n• takes the filename of the text file as a parameter\\n• stores the string of data to the text file.\\n\\nWrite the procedure SaveLogs()\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nprocedure SaveLogs(data, filename)\\n  logFile = open(filename)\\n  logFile.writeLine(data)\\n  logFile.close()\\nendprocedure","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Define procedure SaveLogs","marks":1},{"criteria":"With two valid parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Open file (for write/append)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Using the file name passed in as parameter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Write data to file using data passed in as parameter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Close file","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(d)(i)","parent_question":12,"topic":"casting; data types","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Security Services need to identify the total number of seconds the sensors have been activated on a specific date.\\n\\nThe data from the database table events is imported into the program written in a highlevel programming language.\\n\\nThe program stores the data in a two-dimensional (2D) string array with the identifier arrayEvents\\n\\nThe data to be stored is shown in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Date","SensorID","SensorType","Length"],"rows":[["05/02/2023","WS2","Window","38"],["05/02/2023","MS1","Motion","2"],["06/02/2023","DS3","Door","1"],["06/02/2023","MS2","Motion","3"],["06/02/2023","MS1","Motion","2"],["07/02/2023","WS1","Window","24"],["07/02/2023","DS1","Door","1"]]},{"type":"text","value":"In this table, the value of `events[1, 1]` contains `\\"MS1\\"`.\\n\\nAn array can only store data of one data type. Any non-string data must be converted to a string before storing in the array.\\n\\nIdentify the process that converts integer data to string data."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Casting / cast. Accept type casting. Do not accept conversion. Do not accept examples of casting.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Casting / cast","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"12(d)(ii)","parent_question":12,"topic":"iteration; 2D arrays; selection; accumulation","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write a program that:\\n• asks the user to input a date\\n• totals the number of seconds sensors have been activated on the date input\\n• outputs the calculated total in an appropriate message including the date, for example:\\nSensors were activated for 40 seconds on 05/02/2023\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\ntotal = 0\\ndate = input(\\"Please enter date\\")\\nfor count = 0 to events.length-1\\n  if events[0, count] == date then\\n    total = total + events[3,count]\\n  endif\\nnext count\\nprint(\\"There were \\" + total + \\" events on \\" + date)","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input date and store in variable / use directly","marks":1},{"criteria":"Access all seven events in array / loop for each event","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"Compare date input against date in array (element 0)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Adding length (element 3) from array to total if dates match","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output calculated total and date in appropriate message at the end","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(a)","parent_question":13,"topic":"selection; iteration; constructs","marks":4,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box in each row to identify whether the OCR Reference Language code given is an example of selection or iteration."},{"type":"table","headers":["OCR Reference Language code","Selection","Iteration"],"rows":[["```\\nfor i = 1 to 10\\n   print(i)\\nnext i\\n```","",""],["```\\nwhile score != 0\\n   playgame()\\nendwhile\\n```","",""],["```\\nif playerHit() then\\n   score = 0\\nendif\\n```","",""],["```\\nswitch bonus:\\n   case 0:\\n      score = 9\\n   case 1:\\n      score = 7\\n   case 2:\\n      score = 5\\nendswitch\\n```","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"One mark per correct row."},{"type":"list","items":["`for ... next` = Iteration","`while ... endwhile` = Iteration","`if ... endif` = Selection","`switch ... endswitch` = Selection"]},{"type":"text","value":"Accept any response (ticks, crosses, etc.) that clearly indicates the candidate\'s choice."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"for = Iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"while = Iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"if = Selection","marks":1},{"criteria":"switch = Selection","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"13(b)","parent_question":13,"topic":"pseudocode; assignment; arithmetic","marks":1,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write pseudocode to increment a value held in a variable score by one."}],"answer":{"guidance":"score = score + 1 // score += 1 // score++. Do not accept score + 1 / score = +1. Accept valid structured English answers.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"score = score + 1 (or score += 1 / score++)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(i)","parent_question":14,"topic":"arithmetic operators","marks":2,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Jack’s program uses the addition ( + ) arithmetic operator. This adds together two numbers.\\n\\nState the purpose of each of the arithmetic operators in the table."},{"type":"table","headers":["Arithmetic operator","Purpose"],"rows":[["`*`",""],["`/`",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"* = Multiplication; / = Division. Accept floor / integer division / division with no remainder for / (Python v2.x).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"* = Multiplication","marks":1},{"criteria":"/ = Division","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"14(ii)","parent_question":14,"topic":"programming languages; translators; interpreters; compilers","marks":5,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the description of programming languages and translators by writing the correct term from the box in each space."},{"type":"text","value":"Word box: continues, crashes, debugging, error, executable, high-level, interpreter, language, low-level, many, no, one, stops, with, without"},{"type":"text","value":"Jack writes his program in a ____ language. This needs to be translated into assembly or machine code before it can be executed. This is done using a translator.\\n\\nOne type of translator is an interpreter. This converts one line of code and then executes it, before moving to the next line. It ____ when an error is found, and when corrected continues running from the same position. This translator is helpful when debugging code.\\n\\nA second type of translator is a compiler. This converts all of the code and produces an error report. The code will not run until there are ____ errors.\\n\\nThe ____ file produced can be run ____ the compiler."}],"answer":{"guidance":"high-level; stops (or crashes); no; executable; without.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"high-level","marks":1},{"criteria":"stops (or crashes)","marks":1},{"criteria":"no","marks":1},{"criteria":"executable","marks":1},{"criteria":"without","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(a)(i)","parent_question":15,"topic":"validation; selection; Boolean logic","marks":5,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Customers at a hotel can stay between 1 and 5 (inclusive) nights and can choose between a basic room or a premium room.\\n\\nWhen a new booking is recorded, the details are entered into a program to validate the values. The following criteria are checked:\\n- `firstName` and `surname` are not empty\\n- `room` is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"\\n- `nights` is between 1 and 5 (inclusive).\\n\\nIf any invalid data is found \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\nIf all data is valid \\"ALLOWED\\" is displayed.\\n\\nComplete the following program to validate the inputs.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- a high-level programming language that you have studied."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"firstName = input(\\"Enter a first name\\")\\nsurname = input(\\"Enter a surname\\")\\nroom = input(\\"Enter basic or premium\\")\\nnights = input(\\"Enter between 1 and 5 nights\\")\\nstayComplete = False"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"*General Mark Scheme guidance*"},{"type":"text","value":"1 mark each, to max 5."},{"type":"list","items":["Checks that both `firstName` and `surname` are not empty","Checks that `room` is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"","Checks that `nights` is between 1 and 5 (inclusive) — must check both upper and lower limits","Outputs \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) if any of the three checks are invalid (must check all three)","Outputs \\"ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) only if all three checks are valid"]},{"type":"text","value":"A sensible attempt must have been made at all three checks for output marks to be given. Iteration can be used as validation if input is repeatedly requested until valid. Do not accept logically incorrect Boolean conditions such as `if firstname or surname == \\"\\"`. Do not accept `<` or `>` for `>=` or `<=`. Ignore capitalisation."},{"type":"text","value":"Checks for valid data (e.g. `if firstName != \\"\\" and surname != \\"\\"`) are equally acceptable."},{"type":"code","value":"valid = True\\nif firstName == \\"\\" or surname == \\"\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif room != \\"basic\\" and room != \\"premium\\" then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif nights < 1 or nights > 5 then\\n  valid = False\\nendif\\nif valid then\\n  print(\\"ALLOWED\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"NOT ALLOWED\\")\\nendif"}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Checks that both `firstName` and `surname` are not empty","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that `room` is either \\"basic\\" or \\"premium\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks that `nights` is between 1 and 5 (inclusive) — both upper and lower limits","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"NOT ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) when any of the three checks fail","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"ALLOWED\\" (or equivalent) only when all three checks pass","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(a)(ii)","parent_question":15,"topic":"testing; test plans","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan to check whether the number of nights is validated correctly."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test data (number of nights)","Type of test","Expected output"],"rows":[["2","","ALLOWED"],["","Boundary","ALLOWED"],["","Erroneous / Invalid","NOT ALLOWED"]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"Row 1 type of test: Normal (valid / typical / acceptable). Row 2 test data: 1 or 5. Row 3 test data: any numeric value except 1 to 5 (e.g. 7) or any non-numeric input.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal (type of test for 2)","marks":1},{"criteria":"1 or 5 (boundary test data)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous value (e.g. 7) or non-numeric input","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(b)(i)","parent_question":15,"topic":"functions; parameters; return values; selection","marks":4,"command_word":"create","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A Basic room costs £60 each night. A Premium room costs £80 each night.\\n\\nCreate a function, newPrice(), that takes the number of nights and the type of room as parameters, calculates and returns the price to pay.\\n\\nYou do not have to validate these parameters.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nfunction newPrice(nights, room)\\n   if room == “basic” then\\n       price = 60 * nights\\n   elseif room == “premium” then\\n       price = 80 * nights\\n   endif\\n   return price\\nendfunction","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function header for newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"Taking at least two parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly calculates price based on parameters within function","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns this calculated price","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(b)(ii)","parent_question":15,"topic":"function calls; output","marks":3,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write program code, that uses newPrice(), to output the price of staying in a Premium room for 5 nights.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nprint(newPrice(“premium”, 5))\\n\\nOrder of parameters not important. “premium” must use string delimiters.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Call function newPrice","marks":1},{"criteria":"With (“premium”, 5) as parameters","marks":1},{"criteria":"Output returned value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"15(c)","parent_question":15,"topic":"iteration; selection; input/output; calculation","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A hotel car park charges £4 per hour. If the car is electric, this price is halved to £2 per hour.\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n• take as input the number of hours the user has parked and whether their car is electric or not\\n• calculate and output the total price\\n• repeat continually until the user enters 0 hours.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Example:\\nwhile hours != 0\\n   hours = input(“Enter hours”)\\n   electric = input(“enter Y for electric or N”)\\n   if electric == “Y” then\\n     price = hours * 2\\n   elseif electric == “N” then\\n     price = hours * 4\\n   endif\\n   print(price)\\nendwhile","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs hours AND electric (two separate inputs), storing or using these","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks if car is electric (IF/Select statement)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 2) for electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly calculates and outputs price (hours * 4) for non-electric","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at repetition of above","marks":1},{"criteria":"Until 0 hours entered","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"16","parent_question":16,"topic":"Boolean logic; NAND / truth tables","marks":2,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the truth table in Fig. 1 for the Boolean statement P = NOT(A AND B)."},{"type":"table","headers":["A","B","P"],"rows":[["0","0","1"],["0","1","____"],["1","0","____"],["1","1","0"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Fig. 1"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Row 0,1 P = 1. Row 1,0 P = 1. Accept True or T.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"A=0, B=1 gives P = 1","marks":1},{"criteria":"A=1, B=0 gives P = 1","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(a)","parent_question":17,"topic":"maintainability; comments; indentation; constants","marks":4,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The area of a circle is calculated using the formula π × r^2 where π is equal to 3.142 and r is the radius.\\n\\nA program is written to allow a user to enter the radius of a circle as a whole number between 1 and 30, then calculate and output the area of the circle."},{"type":"code","value":"01 radius = 0\\n02 area = 0.0\\n03 radius = input(\\"Enter radius\\")\\n04 if radius < 1 OR radius > 30 then\\n05   print(\\"Sorry, that radius is invalid\\")\\n06 else\\n07   area = 3.142 * (radius ^ 2)\\n08   print (area)\\n09 endif"},{"type":"text","value":"Explain, using examples from the program, two ways to improve the maintainability of the program."},{"type":"text","value":"Give your answer in the form:\\nImprovement 1: <name a technique and give an example from the program>\\nImprovement 2: <name a different technique and give an example from the program>"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for naming the example and 1 mark for an example related to that method. E.g. Comments/annotation (e.g. “line 4 checks the input is valid”); Indentation (e.g. indenting within IF statement); Using constants (e.g. π as a constant).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Improvement 1: named technique","marks":1},{"criteria":"Improvement 1: example from program","marks":1},{"criteria":"Improvement 2: named technique","marks":1},{"criteria":"Improvement 2: example from program","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(b)","parent_question":17,"topic":"variables","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify two variables used in the program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"radius; area. 1 mark per bullet up to a maximum of 2 marks.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"radius","marks":1},{"criteria":"area","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(c)(i)","parent_question":17,"topic":"constants","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one item in the program that could have been written as a constant."}],"answer":{"guidance":"3.142 / 2 / 1 / 30. 1 mark for one correct identification.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"3.142, 2, 1 or 30","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(c)(ii)","parent_question":17,"topic":"constants","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one reason why you have identified this item as a constant."}],"answer":{"guidance":"The number does not need to be changed while the program is running. / The number can be updated once and it updates throughout. Maximum of 1 mark.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Value does not change at runtime / can be updated once and used throughout","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"17(d)","parent_question":17,"topic":"programming constructs","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick (✓) one box in each row to identify whether each programming construct h