d be tested before use."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Allow answers that explain what would happen if not tested (e.g. \\"there might be bugs\\"). Valid points: check the program works (as intended); meets user requirements; gives the correct output / result; find / detect / check for errors / bugs; check the program does not crash / is robust / executes / runs; to try and break the program / destructive testing; test for / improve usability / user experience / performance / check user feedback is suitable; allow any errors to be fixed / make changes / improvements as a result of testing; ensure no problems / issues fixed when released; defensive design considerations / anticipating misuse / so cannot be misused.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid reason for testing","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second valid reason for testing","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(ii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"types of testing (iterative, final/terminal)","marks":2,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by naming and describing one type of test that should be used on Charlie\'s program before releasing it."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test type","Description"],"rows":[["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for name, 1 mark for matching description. Allow other sensible descriptive names for testing. Description must match test type. Must be a description and not just an example, but example may support description. Do not accept descriptions that simply repeat type of test without further clarification. Allow black box testing / white box testing / other sensible valid types. Do not accept examples of validation. Examples: Final / terminal testing - Completed at the end of development / before release / to test the product as a whole. Iterative / incremental testing - Completed during development / after each module is completed / test module in isolation. Normal testing - test using data that should be accepted. Boundary / Extreme testing - test using data that is on the edge of being acceptable. Invalid / Erroneous testing - test using data that should be rejected.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correctly names a type of test","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of that test type","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(iii)","parent_question":6,"topic":"IDE features for testing","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table by identifying and describing two features of an IDE that can be used when testing a program."},{"type":"table","headers":["Feature","Description"],"rows":[["",""],["",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for feature, 1 mark for matching description. Allow other sensible names for features. Description must add more than is given in the identification of the feature. Description must match feature. \\"finding errors\\" is NE for description of error reporting. Examples: Translator / compiler / interpreter - convert to low-level/machine code / allow program to be executed / produce executable file. Run-time environment / output window - allows program / code to be run / shows output. Error reporting / diagnostics - identify location/detail of errors / suggests fixes. Debugger - find errors. Stepping - execute/run the program line by line. Variable watch - see the contents/data held in variables. Break points - will allow the program to stop at a chosen / set position. Text/code editor - allows program code to be written/changed. Pretty printing / keyword highlighting - allows keywords / variables to be coloured / identified. Keyword completion / syntax suggestion - suggests code/syntax when first part entered.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First IDE feature correctly identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of first feature","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second IDE feature correctly identified","marks":1},{"criteria":"Matching description of second feature","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7","parent_quest